  <div id="watermark-tr">
arXiv:1405.3096v1 [math.AG] 13 May 2014</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theory
<br class="ltx_break">of mixed Hodge modules</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Christian Schnell
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics
<br class="ltx_break">Stony Brook University
<br class="ltx_break">Stony Brook, NY 11794-3651
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:cschnell@math.sunysb.edu">cschnell@math.sunysb.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: August 11, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">After explaining the definition of pure and mixed Hodge modules on complex manifolds, we
describe some of Saito’s most important results and their proofs, and then discuss
two simple applications of the theory.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">A. </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1. </span>Nature of Saito’s theory</h3>

<div id="SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hodge theory on complex manifolds bears a surprising likeness to <math id="SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic
cohomology theory on algebraic varieties defined over finite fields. This was pointed
out by Deligne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Deligne-I</span>]</cite>, who also proposed a “heuristic dictionary”
for translating results from one language into the other, and used it to predict,
among other things, the existence of limit mixed Hodge structures and the notion of
admissibility for variations of mixed Hodge structure. But the most important example of a
successful translation is without doubt Morihiko Saito’s theory of mixed Hodge
modules: it is the analogue, in Hodge theory, of the mixed <math id="SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic complexes
introduced by Beilinson, Bernstein, and Deligne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BBD</span>]</cite>. One of the main
accomplishments of Saito’s theory is a Hodge-theoretic proof for the decomposition
theorem; the original argument in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BBD</span>, §6.2]</cite> was famously based on reduction
to positive characteristic.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Contained in two long papers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-HM</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHM</span>]</cite>, Saito’s theory is a vast
generalization of classical Hodge theory, built on the foundations laid by many
people during the 1970s and 1980s: the theory of perverse sheaves, <math id="SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module
theory, and the study of variations of mixed Hodge structure and their degenerations.
Roughly speaking, classical Hodge theory can deal with two situations: the cohomology
groups of a single complex algebraic variety, to which it assigns a mixed Hodge
structure; and the cohomology groups of a family of smooth projective varieties, to
which it assigns a variation of Hodge structure. The same formalism, based on the
theory of harmonic forms and the Kähler identities, also applies to cohomology
groups of the form <math id="SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(B,\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(B,\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth projective variety and
<math id="SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> a polarizable variation of Hodge structure. Saito’s theory, on the other hand,
can deal with arbitrary families of algebraic varieties, and with cohomology groups
of the form <math id="SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(B,\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(B,\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is non-compact or singular, and where <math id="SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is
an admissible variation of mixed Hodge structure. It also provides a nice formalism,
in terms of filtered <math id="SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, that treats all those situations in a
consistent way.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Conceptually, there are two ways of thinking about mixed Hodge modules:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mixed Hodge modules are <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">perverse sheaves with mixed Hodge structure</em>.
They obey the same six-functor formalism as perverse sheaves; whenever an
operation on perverse sheaves produces a <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space, the corresponding
operation on mixed Hodge modules produces a mixed Hodge structure.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mixed Hodge modules are <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a special class of filtered <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules</em>
with good properties. Whereas arbitrary filtered <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules do not behave well
under various operations, the ones in Saito’s theory do. The
same is true for the coherent sheaves that make up the filtration on the
<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module; many applications of the theory rely on this fact.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The first point of view is more common among people working in Hodge theory;
the second one among people in other areas who are using mixed Hodge modules to
solve their problems. Saito’s theory has been applied with great success in
representation theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BW</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">SV</span>]</cite>, singularity theory
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sai91c</a>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BMS</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BFNP</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">DMS</span>]</cite>, algebraic geometry
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-K</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-AMS</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">one-forms</span>]</cite>, and nowadays also in Donaldson-Thomas theory
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BBDJS</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BBS</span>]</cite>. I should also mention that the whole theory has recently been generalized
to arbitrary semisimple representations of the fundamental group on algebraic varieties
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Sabbah-B</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2. </span>About this article</h3>

<div id="SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The purpose of this article is to explain the definition of pure and mixed Hodge
modules on complex manifolds; to describe some of the most important results and
their proofs; and to discuss two simple applications of the theory. Along the way,
the reader will also find some remarks about mixed Hodge modules on analytic spaces
and on algebraic varieties, and about the construction of various functors. While it
is easy to learn the formalism of mixed Hodge modules, especially for someone who is
already familiar with perverse sheaves, it helps to know something about the
internals of the theory, too. For that reason, more than half of the text is devoted
to explaining Saito’s definition and the proofs of two crucial theorems; in return, I
had to neglect other important parts of the story.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The article has its origins in two lectures that I gave during the workshop on
geometric methods in representation theory in Sanya. In editing the notes, I also tried to incorporate
what I learned during a week-long workshop about mixed Hodge modules and their
applications, held in August 2013 at the Clay Mathematics Institute in Oxford. Both
in my lectures and in the text, I decided to present Saito’s theory with a focus on
filtered <math id="SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules and their properties; in my experience, this aspect of the
theory is the one that is most useful in applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For those looking for additional information about mixed Hodge modules,
there are two very readable surveys by Saito himself: a short introduction that
focuses on “how to use mixed Hodge modules” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-intro</span>]</cite>, and a longer
article with more details <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-th</span>]</cite>. The technical aspects of the theory are
discussed in a set of lecture notes by Shimizu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">RIMS</span>]</cite>, and a brief axiomatic
treatment can be found in the book by Peters and Steenbrink <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">PS</span>, Ch. 14]</cite>. There
are also unpublished lecture notes by Sabbah <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Sabbah</span>]</cite>, who gives a
beautiful account of the theory on curves.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3. </span>Left and right D-modules</h3>

<div id="SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An issue that always comes up in connection with Saito’s theory is whether one
should use left or right <math id="SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. This is partly a matter of taste, because
the two notions are equivalent on complex manifolds, but each choice brings its
own advantages and disadvantages. Saito himself dealt with this problem by using left
<math id="SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules in the introduction to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-HM</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHM</span>]</cite>, and right
<math id="SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules in the definitions and proofs. After much deliberation, I decided to
follow Saito and to write this article entirely in terms of <em id="SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right
<math id="SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules</em>. Since this is sure to upset some readers, let me explain
why.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One reason is that the direct image and duality functors are naturally defined for
right <math id="SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Both functors play important roles in the theory: the duality
functor is needed to define polarizations, and the direct image functor is needed for
example to define mixed Hodge modules on singular spaces. The idea is that
<math id="SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules on a singular space <math id="SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> are the same thing as <math id="SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules on some
ambient complex manifold with support in <math id="SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; to make this definition independent of
the choice of embedding, it is better to use right <math id="SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Another reason
is that it makes sense to distinguish, conceptually, between variations of Hodge
structure and Hodge modules: for example, it is easy to define inverse images for
variations of Hodge structure, but not for Hodge modules. Replacing a flat bundle by
the corresponding right <math id="SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module further emphasizes this distinction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4. </span>Brief summary</h3>

<div id="SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex algebraic variety. Although Saito’s theory works more generally
on complex manifolds and analytic spaces, we shall begin with the case of algebraic
varieties, because it is technically easier and some of the results are
stronger. Saito constructs the following two categories:<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            To be precise, <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> should be <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> should
be <math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="SS4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS4.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{polarizable Hodge modules of weight $w$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>polarizable Hodge modules of weight </mtext><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{polarizable Hodge modules of weight $w$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="SS4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{graded-polarizable mixed Hodge modules}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mtext>graded-polarizable mixed Hodge modules</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{graded-polarizable mixed Hodge modules}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Both are abelian categories, with an exact and faithful functor</p>
<table id="SS4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat\colon\operatorname{MHM}(X)\to\operatorname{Perv}_{\mathbb{Q}}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rat</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Perv</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat\colon\operatorname{MHM}(X)\to\operatorname{Perv}_{\mathbb{Q}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">to the category of perverse sheaves (with coefficients in <math id="SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>). All the familiar
operations on perverse sheaves – such as direct and inverse images along arbitrary
morphisms, Verdier duality, nearby and vanishing cycles – are lifted to mixed Hodge
modules in a way that is compatible with the functor “<math id="SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>rat</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat</annotation></semantics></math>”. This means
that whenever some operation on perverse sheaves produces a <math id="SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector
space, the corresponding operation on mixed Hodge modules endows it with a mixed
Hodge structure. It also means that every semisimple perverse sheaf of geometric
origin is a direct summand of <math id="SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\rat M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mrow><mi>rat</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\rat M</annotation></semantics></math> for some polarizable Hodge
module <math id="SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. After going to the derived category <math id="SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, one has a formalism of
weights, similar to the one for mixed complexes in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BBD</span>, §5.1.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The relation between (mixed) Hodge modules and variations of (mixed) Hodge structure
is similar to the relation between perverse sheaves and local systems.
Every polarizable variation of Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math> on a
Zariski-open subset of an irreducible subvariety <math id="SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> extends uniquely to
a polarizable Hodge module <math id="SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> with strict support <math id="SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, which means
that <math id="SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rat</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat M</annotation></semantics></math> is the intersection complex of the underlying local system of
<math id="SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces. Conversely, every polarizable Hodge module on <math id="SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic
to a direct sum of objects of the above type, and this so-called <em id="SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decomposition by
strict support</em> is unique. The category <math id="SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> is semi-simple; the perverse
sheaves that one gets by applying the functor “<math id="SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>rat</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat</annotation></semantics></math>” are also semi-simple.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The category <math id="SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of graded-polarizable mixed Hodge modules is no longer
semi-simple, but every object <math id="SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> comes with a finite increasing
filtration <math id="SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\bullet}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\bullet}M</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <em id="SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weight filtration</em>, such that</p>
<table id="SS4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\ell}^{W}\!M=W_{\ell}M/W_{\ell-1}M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,\ell)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\ell}^{W}\!M=W_{\ell}M/W_{\ell-1}M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,\ell).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Every mixed Hodge module is therefore an extension of several pure Hodge modules;
the extensions among these are not arbitrary, but are
controlled by an “admissibility” condition similar to the one appearing in the
theory of variations of mixed Hodge structure by Steenbrink and Zucker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">SZ</span>]</cite> and
Kashiwara <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Kashiwara-st</span>]</cite>. In the same way that a perverse sheaf can be obtained
by gluing together local systems on a stratification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Verdier</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Beilinson</span>]</cite>, one
can think of a mixed Hodge module as being obtained by gluing together admissible
variations of mixed Hodge structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5. </span>Examples</h3>

<div id="SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to get some intuition, let us look at a few typical examples of mixed Hodge
modules. The exact definitions will be given in <a href="#SS12" title="12. Definition of pure Hodge modules ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></span></a> and
<a href="#SS20" title="20. Definition of mixed Hodge modules ‣ D. Mixed Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></span></a> below; for the time being, we shall describe mixed Hodge
modules by giving the underlying perverse sheaf, the underlying filtered
<math id="SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, and the weight filtration. In more detail, suppose that <math id="SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a mixed Hodge module on a smooth algebraic variety <math id="SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, considered as a complex
manifold. The perverse sheaf is <math id="SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rat</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat M</annotation></semantics></math>; its complexification is
isomorphic to the de Rham complex of a regular holonomic right <math id="SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module
<math id="SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the additional piece of data is an increasing filtration <math id="SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> by coherent <math id="SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, compatible with the order filtration on <math id="SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS5.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS5.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 5.1</span></span><span id="SS5.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS5.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS5.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most basic example of a polarizable Hodge module on a nonsingular algebraic
variety <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the canonical bundle <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is naturally a
right <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, and together with the filtration</p>
<table id="SS5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{-n-1}\,\omega_{X}=0\quad\text{and}\quad F_{-n}\,\omega_{X}=\omega_{X}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{-n-1}\,\omega_{X}=0\quad\text{and}\quad F_{-n}\,\omega_{X}=\omega_{X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS5.E1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the perverse sheaf <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{X}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{X}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, it is an object of the category
<math id="SS5.E1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}(X,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}(X,n)</annotation></semantics></math>, usually denoted by <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>. One can obtain many additional
examples by applying various functors, such as the direct image by a morphism <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, the filtration on <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>
starts with the coherent <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS5.E1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}f_{\ast}\omega_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}f_{\ast}\omega_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>; this fact is behind several results
in algebraic geometry, such as Kollár’s theorems about
higher direct images of dualizing sheaves (see <a href="#SS25" title="25. Kollár’s conjecture ‣ E. Two applications ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS5.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS5.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 5.2</span></span><span id="SS5.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS5.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS5.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, one can consider an irreducible subvariety <math id="SS5.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>, and a
polarizable variation of Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS5.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math> on a Zariski-open
subset of the smooth locus of <math id="SS5.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. As mentioned above, it extends uniquely to
a polarizable Hodge module <math id="SS5.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> with strict support <math id="SS5.E2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case,
the perverse sheaf <math id="SS5.E2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rat</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat M</annotation></semantics></math> is simply the intersection complex of the underlying
local system of <math id="SS5.E2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces. The filtration <math id="SS5.E2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on the
corresponding <math id="SS5.E2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module is also uniquely determined by the Hodge filtration, but
hard to make explicit (except in special cases, for instance when the variation
is defined on the complement of a normal crossing divisor).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS5.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS5.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 5.3</span></span><span id="SS5.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS5.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS5.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One case where one can describe the filtration in geometric terms is the family
of hyperplane sections of a smooth projective variety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">residues</span>]</cite>. Suppose that
<math id="SS5.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth projective variety of dimension <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> is a
nonsingular hyperplane section (in a fixed projective embedding). The entire
cohomology of <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by that of <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, with the exception of the so-called
<em id="SS5.E3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">variable part</em></p>
<table id="SS5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}^{n-1}(Y,\mathbb{Q})=\ker\Bigl(H^{n-1}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{n+1}\bigl(X,\mathbb{Q}(1)\bigr)\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}^{n-1}(Y,\mathbb{Q})=\ker\Bigl(H^{n-1}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{n+1}\bigl(X,\mathbb{Q}(1)\bigr)\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS5.E3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Now let <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be the projective space parametrizing hyperplane sections of <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\mathscr{Y}\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\mathscr{Y}\to B</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal family. Let <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}\subseteq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}\subseteq B</annotation></semantics></math> be the open subset
corresponding to nonsingular hyperplane sections, and let <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the variation of
Hodge structure on the variable part of their cohomology. As in the previous example,
it determines a Hodge module <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and one can show that <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a
direct summand of <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}f_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{\mathscr{Y}}^{H}[d+n-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒴</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}f_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{\mathscr{Y}}^{H}[d+n-1]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\dim B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\dim B</annotation></semantics></math>. The
filtration <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on the underlying <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module can be
described using residues of meromorphic forms. For a nonsingular hyperplane
section <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>, a classical theorem by Carlson and Griffiths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">CG</span>]</cite> says
that the <em id="SS5.E3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">residue mapping</em></p>
<table id="SS5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Res_{Y}\colon H^{0}\bigl(X,\Omega_{X}^{n}(pY)\bigr)\to F^{n-p}H_{0}^{n-1}(Y,\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Res</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Res_{Y}\colon H^{0}\bigl(X,\Omega_{X}^{n}(pY)\bigr)\to F^{n-p}H_{0}^{n-1}(Y,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS5.E3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is surjective, provided the line bundle <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{X}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{X}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently ample. Given an open
subset <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq B</annotation></semantics></math> and a meromorphic form</p>
<table id="SS5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in H^{0}\Bigl(U\times X,\Omega_{B\times X}^{d+n}\bigl((n+d+p)\mathscr{Y}\bigr)\Bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in H^{0}\Bigl(U\times X,\Omega_{B\times X}^{d+n}\bigl((n+d+p)\mathscr{Y}\bigr)\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS5.E3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">one can therefore take the residue along smooth fibers to obtain a holomorphic
section of <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{B}\otimes j_{\ast}F^{-d-p}\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{B}\otimes j_{\ast}F^{-d-p}\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\colon B_{0}\hookrightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\colon B_{0}\hookrightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> denotes
the inclusion. Then <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly the subsheaf of <math id="SS5.E3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{B}\otimes j_{\ast}F^{-d-p}\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{B}\otimes j_{\ast}F^{-d-p}\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> given by all sections of this kind.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS5.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS5.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 5.4</span></span><span id="SS5.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS5.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS5.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One can obtain mixed Hodge modules by taking the direct image along an
open embedding <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\colon U\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\colon U\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonsingular algebraic
variety of dimension <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=X\setminus D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=X\setminus D</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement of a
divisor. Then <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[n]</annotation></semantics></math> is a mixed Hodge module on <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; the underlying
perverse sheaf is <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, and the underlying regular holonomic
<math id="SS5.E4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module is <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega_{X}(\ast D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{X}(\ast D)</annotation></semantics></math>, the sheaf of meromorphic <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-forms on <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> that are
holomorphic on <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. When the divisor <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is nonsingular, one can also say explicitly
what the Hodge filtration and the weight filtration are. The Hodge filtration
satisfies</p>
<table id="SS5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{-n-1}\,\omega_{X}(\ast D)=0\quad\text{and}\quad F_{p}\,\omega_{X}(\ast D)=\omega_{X}\bigl((p+n+1)D\bigr)\quad\text{for $p\geq-n$,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>and</mtext><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{-n-1}\,\omega_{X}(\ast D)=0\quad\text{and}\quad F_{p}\,\omega_{X}(\ast D)=\omega_{X}\bigl((p+n+1)D\bigr)\quad\text{for $p\geq-n$,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS5.E4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and is therefore essentially the filtration by pole order; the weight filtration
satisfies</p>
<table id="SS5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{n}j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[n]=\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]\quad\text{and}\quad W_{n+1}j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[n]=j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[n]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[n]=\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]\quad\text{and}\quad W_{n+1}j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[n]=j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[n],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS5.E4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and the quotient <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{n+1}^{W}j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{n+1}^{W}j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[n]</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the direct
image of <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{D}^{H}(-1)[n-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{D}^{H}(-1)[n-1]</annotation></semantics></math>, with a Tate twist to change the weight from <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>
to <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>. When the divisor <math id="SS5.E4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is singular, both filtrations still exist, but are
hard to describe concretely.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS5.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS5.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 5.5</span></span><span id="SS5.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS5.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS5.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An interesting special case of the previous construction has been studied by
Cautis and Kamnitzer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Cautis</span>]</cite>. Let <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(k,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(k,n)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Grassmannian parametrizing
<math id="SS5.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspaces of <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the divisor</p>
<table id="SS5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\bigl\{\thinspace(V,W)\in G(k,n)\times G(n-k,n)\thinspace\thinspace\big|\thinspace\thinspace V\cap W\neq 0\thinspace\bigr\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.062em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.340em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\bigl\{\thinspace(V,W)\in G(k,n)\times G(n-k,n)\thinspace\thinspace\big|\thinspace\thinspace V\cap W\neq 0\thinspace\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS5.E5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">inside the product <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=G(k,n)\times G(n-k,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=G(k,n)\times G(n-k,n)</annotation></semantics></math>; without loss of generality, <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq n/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq n/2</annotation></semantics></math>. We would like to describe the direct image <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[d]\in\operatorname{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[d]\in\operatorname{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2k(n-k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2k(n-k)</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. The open set <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is one of the orbits of
the natural <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(n)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; the divisor <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the union of the other orbits</p>
<table id="SS5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r}=\bigl\{\thinspace(V,W)\in G(k,n)\times G(n-k,n)\thinspace\thinspace\big|\thinspace\thinspace\dim V\cap W\geq r\thinspace\bigr\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.062em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.229em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r}=\bigl\{\thinspace(V,W)\in G(k,n)\times G(n-k,n)\thinspace\thinspace\big|\thinspace\thinspace\dim V\cap W\geq r\thinspace\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS5.E5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq r\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq r\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore singular once <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the group
action, one can show that for <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0,\dotsc,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0,\dotsc,k</annotation></semantics></math>, the graded quotient</p>
<table id="SS5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{d+r}^{W}j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[d]\in\operatorname{HM}(X,d+r)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{d+r}^{W}j_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{U}^{H}[d]\in\operatorname{HM}(X,d+r)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS5.E5.p1.4" class="ltx_p">of the weight filtration is the Hodge module corresponding to the constant variation
of Hodge structure <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}\bigl(-\frac{1}{2}r(r+1)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}\bigl(-\frac{1}{2}r(r+1)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> on the orbit <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>; it has
the correct weight because</p>
<table id="SS5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Z_{r}+r(r+1)=d-r^{2}+r(r+1)=d+r." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Z_{r}+r(r+1)=d-r^{2}+r(r+1)=d+r.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS5.E5.p1.5" class="ltx_p">One can also compute the graded module <math id="SS5.E5.p1.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\omega_{X}(\ast D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\omega_{X}(\ast D)</annotation></semantics></math> and show that
the characteristic variety is the union of the conormal varieties of the orbit
closures.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6. </span>Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">I thank Wilfried Schmid for inviting me to Sanya, and the editors of the workshop
proceedings for giving me the time to rewrite my lecture notes. Morihiko Saito and
Claude Sabbah sent me several pages of detailed comments about the first version;
I am very grateful for their advice. I also take this opportunity to thank
the participants of the Clay workshop for many productive discussions about
Saito’s theory. During the preparation of this paper, I have been supported in part by
grant DMS-1331641 from the National Science Foundation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B. </span>Pure Hodge modules</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After a brief review of nearby and vanishing cycle functors, we explain the
definition of (polarizable) Hodge modules on complex manifolds. A Hodge module is
basically a certain type of <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with a filtration and a rational
structure. The goal is understand the need for the various technical conditions that
Saito imposes on such objects in order to define the category <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> of
polarizable Hodge modules of weight <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> on a complex manifold <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7. </span>Basic objects</h3>

<div id="SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Saito’s theory is an extension of classical Hodge theory, and the objects that
he uses are a natural generalization of variations of Hodge structure. Recall that a
<em id="SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">polarized variation of Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math></em> on a complex manifold
<math id="SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the following four things:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A local system <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of finite-dimensional <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A holomorphic vector bundle <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> with integrable connection
<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\colon\mathcal{V}\to\Omega_{X}^{1}\otimes\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">∇</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\colon\mathcal{V}\to\Omega_{X}^{1}\otimes\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Hodge filtration <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\bullet}\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\bullet}\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> by holomorphic subbundles.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A bilinear form <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\colon V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}V\to\mathbb{Q}(-w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\colon V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}V\to\mathbb{Q}(-w)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(-w)=(2\pi i)^{-w}\mathbb{Q}\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(-w)=(2\pi i)^{-w}\mathbb{Q}\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="SS7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">They are related by the condition that the local system of <math id="SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∇</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla</annotation></semantics></math>-flat holomorphic
sections of <math id="SS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="SS7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular, <math id="SS7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}\simeq V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathscr{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}\simeq V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathscr{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. At every point <math id="SS7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, the filtration <math id="SS7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\bullet}\mathcal{V}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\bullet}\mathcal{V}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and the rational structure <math id="SS7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> should define a Hodge structure of weight
<math id="SS7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="SS7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space <math id="SS7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, and this Hodge structure should be polarized
by the bilinear form <math id="SS7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Globally, the Hodge filtration is required to satisfy the
Griffiths transversality relation</p>
<table id="SS7.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS7.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\bigl(F^{p}\mathcal{V}\bigr)\subseteq\Omega_{X}^{1}\otimes F^{p-1}\mathcal{V}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\bigl(F^{p}\mathcal{V}\bigr)\subseteq\Omega_{X}^{1}\otimes F^{p-1}\mathcal{V}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS7.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By the holomorphic Poincaré lemma, the holomorphic de Rham complex</p>
<table id="SS7.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS7.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}\to\Omega_{X}^{1}\otimes\mathcal{V}\to\dotsb\to\Omega_{X}^{\dim X}\otimes\mathcal{V}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}\to\Omega_{X}^{1}\otimes\mathcal{V}\to\dotsb\to\Omega_{X}^{\dim X}\otimes\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS7.p1.4" class="ltx_p">is a resolution of the locally constant sheaf <math id="SS7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>; this gives
another way to describe the relationship between <math id="SS7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V},\nabla)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V},\nabla)</annotation></semantics></math>. Lastly,
recall that a variation of Hodge structure is called <em id="SS7.p1.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">polarizable</em> if it
admits at least one polarization.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Saito’s idea is to generalize variations of Hodge structure by allowing perverse
sheaves instead of local systems; because of the Riemann-Hilbert correspondence, it
then becomes necessary to use regular holonomic <math id="SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules instead of vector
bundles with integrable connection. This leads to the following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS7.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS7.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.1</span></span><span id="SS7.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS7.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="SS7.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS7.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS7.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure</em> is a triple
<math id="SS7.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of the following
objects:</p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A constructible complex of <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A regular holonomic right <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with an isomorphism</p>
<table id="SS7.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS7.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\DR(\mathcal{M})\simeq\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}K." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\DR(\mathcal{M})\simeq\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}K.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By the Riemann-Hilbert correspondence, this makes <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> a perverse sheaf.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A good filtration <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent subsheaves of <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that</p>
<table id="SS7.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS7.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}\mathcal{M}\cdot F_{k}\mathscr{D}\subseteq F_{p+k}\mathcal{M}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\mathcal{M}\cdot F_{k}\mathscr{D}\subseteq F_{p+k}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and such that <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is coherent over <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}\simeq\Sym^{\bullet}\mathscr{T}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}\simeq\Sym^{\bullet}\mathscr{T}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the tangent sheaf of the complex manifold <math id="SS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="SS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">de
Rham complex</em> of a right <math id="SS7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is the following complex:</p>
<table id="SS7.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS7.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\DR(\mathcal{M})=\Bigl[\mathcal{M}\otimes\textstyle\bigwedge\nolimits^{n}\mathscr{T}_{X}\to\dotsb\to\mathcal{M}\otimes\mathscr{T}_{X}\to\mathcal{M}\Bigr][n]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mstyle><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\DR(\mathcal{M})=\Bigl[\mathcal{M}\otimes\textstyle\bigwedge\nolimits^{n}\mathscr{T}_{X}\to\dotsb\to\mathcal{M}\otimes\mathscr{T}_{X}\to\mathcal{M}\Bigr][n]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS7.p3.2" class="ltx_p">It is concentrated in degrees <math id="SS7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-n,\dotsc,-1,0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-n,\dotsc,-1,0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="SS7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>; and naturally
filtered by the family of subcomplexes</p>
<table id="SS7.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS7.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{p}\DR(\mathcal{M})=\Bigl[F_{p-n}\mathcal{M}\otimes\textstyle\bigwedge\nolimits^{n}\mathscr{T}_{X}\to\dotsb\to F_{p-1}\mathcal{M}\otimes\mathscr{T}_{X}\to F_{p}\mathcal{M}\Bigr][n]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>DR</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mstyle><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\DR(\mathcal{M})=\Bigl[F_{p-n}\mathcal{M}\otimes\textstyle\bigwedge\nolimits^{n}\mathscr{T}_{X}\to\dotsb\to F_{p-1}\mathcal{M}\otimes\mathscr{T}_{X}\to F_{p}\mathcal{M}\Bigr][n].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS7.p3.3" class="ltx_p">As I mentioned earlier, one can think of <math id="SS7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as a perverse sheaf with an
additional filtration on the corresponding regular holonomic <math id="SS7.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module; the
functor “<math id="SS7.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>rat</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat</annotation></semantics></math>” to the category of perverse sheaves is of course defined by
setting <math id="SS7.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat M=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rat</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat M=K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS7.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS7.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 7.2</span></span><span id="SS7.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS7.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To every variation of Hodge structure, one can associate a filtered regular
holonomic <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure by the following procedure. The
perverse sheaf is <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=V[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=V[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, and the regular holonomic <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module is</p>
<table id="SS7.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS7.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathcal{V}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathcal{V},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS7.E2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with right <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure given by the rule <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\omega\otimes m)\cdot\xi=(\omega\cdot\xi)\otimes m-\omega\otimes\nabla_{\xi}m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\omega\otimes m)\cdot\xi=(\omega\cdot\xi)\otimes m-\omega\otimes\nabla_{\xi}m</annotation></semantics></math> for local holomorphic
sections <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\omega_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\omega_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in\mathscr{T}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in\mathscr{T}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is filtered by
the coherent subsheaves</p>
<table id="SS7.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS7.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}\mathcal{M}=\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}F^{-p-n}\mathcal{V};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\mathcal{M}=\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}F^{-p-n}\mathcal{V};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS7.E2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">we will see later that the shift in the filtration gives <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> the weight <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w+n</annotation></semantics></math>. The
definitions are set up in such a way that the de Rham complex <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\DR(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\DR(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is
isomorphic to the holomorphic de Rham complex of <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, shifted <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> steps to the
left; because of the holomorphic Poincaré lemma, this means that <math id="SS7.E2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\DR(\mathcal{M})\simeq\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\DR(\mathcal{M})\simeq\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS7.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS7.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 7.3</span></span><span id="SS7.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS7.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A typical example is the constant variation of Hodge structure <math id="SS7.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="SS7.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding triple is <math id="SS7.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\omega_{X},F_{\bullet}\omega_{X},\mathbb{Q}_{X}[n]\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\omega_{X},F_{\bullet}\omega_{X},\mathbb{Q}_{X}[n]\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>;
here <math id="SS7.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally a right <math id="SS7.E3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, and the filtration is such that
<math id="SS7.E3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{p}^{F}\omega_{X}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{p}^{F}\omega_{X}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="SS7.E3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">≠</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq-n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS7.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS7.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 7.4</span></span><span id="SS7.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS7.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="SS7.E4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Tate twist</em> of <math id="SS7.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by an integer <math id="SS7.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the new triple</p>
<table id="SS7.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS7.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(k)=\Bigl(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet-k}\mathcal{M},K\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}(k)\Bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(k)=\Bigl(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet-k}\mathcal{M},K\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}(k)\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS7.E4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS7.E4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(k)=(2\pi i)^{k}\mathbb{Q}\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(k)=(2\pi i)^{k}\mathbb{Q}\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. For variations of Hodge structure,
this definition specializes to the usual notion.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS7.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS7.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 7.5</span></span><span id="SS7.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS7.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="SS7.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is compact, the cohomology groups <math id="SS7.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}\bigl(X,\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}\bigl(X,\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> are
finite-dimensional <math id="SS7.E5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces; they come with a filtration induced
by <math id="SS7.E5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\DR(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\DR(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, and a <math id="SS7.E5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure induced by
the isomorphism with <math id="SS7.E5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X,K)\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,K)\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will see later that they are
polarizable Hodge structures when <math id="SS7.E5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and <math id="SS7.E5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a polarizable Hodge
module.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The class of all filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="SS7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure is
of course much too large for the purposes of Hodge theory; the problem is to find a
subclass of “Hodge modules” that is small enough to have good properties, but still
large enough to contain all polarizable variations of Hodge structure. We would also
like the resulting theory to be similar to the theory of perverse sheaves: for
example, there should be a way of extending a polarizable variation of Hodge
structure on a Zariski-open subset of an irreducible subvariety <math id="SS7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> to a
polarizable Hodge module on <math id="SS7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, similar to taking the intersection complex of a
local system.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We shall define polarizable Hodge modules by imposing several additional conditions
on <math id="SS7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>; these conditions should be strong enough
that, at the end of the day, every polarizable Hodge module <math id="SS7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="SS7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is of
the form</p>
<table id="SS7.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS7.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\simeq\bigoplus_{Z\subseteq X}M_{Z}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\simeq\bigoplus_{Z\subseteq X}M_{Z},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS7.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from a polarizable variation of Hodge structure of
weight <math id="SS7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math> on a Zariski-open subset of the irreducible subvariety <math id="SS7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. This is
a reasonable demand for two reasons: (1) The perverse sheaves appearing in the
decomposition theorem are direct sums of intersection complexes. (2) The underlying
local system of a polarizable variation of Hodge structure is semisimple
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Deligne-fin</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, we know from the beginning what objects we want in the theory; the
problem is to find a good description by conditions of a local nature. Moreover, the
conditions have to be such that we can check them after applying various operations.
Saito’s solution is, roughly speaking, to consider only those objects
whose “restriction” to every point is a polarized Hodge structure; this idea does not
directly make sense at points where <math id="SS7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is singular, but can be implemented with
the help of the nearby and vanishing cycle functors (which are a replacement for the
naive operation of restricting to a divisor).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmnotex1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_note">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmnotex1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Note</span></span><span id="Thmnotex1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmnotex1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmnotex1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As Schmid pointed out after my lectures, the <math id="Thmnotex1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure is not essential. In
fact, Saito’s theory works just as well when <math id="Thmnotex1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a constructible complex of
<math id="Thmnotex1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces; with a little bit of extra effort, one can even get by with
coefficients in <math id="Thmnotex1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. This point is discussed in more detail in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">DS</span>, Section 3.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8. </span>Review of nearby and vanishing cycles</h3>

<div id="SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before we can understand Saito’s definition, we need to become sufficiently familiar
with nearby and vanishing cycle functors, both for perverse sheaves and for regular
holonomic <math id="SS8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. This topic is somewhat technical – but it is really at the
heart of Saito’s theory, and so we shall spend some time reviewing it. I decided
to state many elementary properties in the form of exercises; working them out
carefully is strongly recommended.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To begin with, suppose that <math id="SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex manifold, and that <math id="SS8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a holomorphic function that is submersive over the punctured unit disk
<math id="SS8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\ast}=\Delta\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\ast}=\Delta\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Remembering that the function <math id="SS8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\colon\mathbb{H}\to\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℍ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\colon\mathbb{H}\to\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="SS8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(z)=e^{2\pi iz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(z)=e^{2\pi iz}</annotation></semantics></math>, makes the upper half-plane into the universal covering space
of <math id="SS8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get the following commutative diagram:</p>
<table id="SS8.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="84.97" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-41.68px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 193.58 84.97" width="193.58"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,84.97) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(96.79,0) translate(0,41.68)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -92.18 -29)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 74.14)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.61)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 1.35 0) translate(15.37,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -10.76 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.72em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.99" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.99)" width="7.69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\tilde{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\tilde{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 33.44 0) translate(53.55,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -13.19 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="12.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 105.79 0) translate(56.65,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -16.3 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="18.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 66.07)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(16.72,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.11 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 33.95 0) translate(53.04,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.68 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.83em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="11.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 104.8 0) translate(57.65,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -17.3 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="20.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\{0\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\{0\}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -75.46 17.58 L -75.46 -14.66"></path></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -59.81 25.92 L -23.27 25.92"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -44.6 33.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.73" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.12"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -5.19 17.58 L -5.19 -14.66"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 2.47 -0.96)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle f</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 70.27 15.5 L 70.27 -13.73"></path></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 49.08 25.92 L 12.89 25.92"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 29.03 33.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.28em;--ltx-fo-height:0.46em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.41)" width="3.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle i</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -58.46 -25.54 L -22.76 -25.54"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -43.24 -17.88)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.38em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="5.25"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS8.Ex1.m1.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle e</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 48.08 -25.54 L 12.38 -25.54"></path></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="SS8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the fiber product of <math id="SS8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="SS8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. For a constructible
complex of <math id="SS8.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces <math id="SS8.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS8.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the complex of <em id="SS8.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nearby cycles</em></p>
<table id="SS8.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}K=i^{-1}\mathbf{R}k_{\ast}\bigl(k^{-1}K\bigr);" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}K=i^{-1}\mathbf{R}k_{\ast}\bigl(k^{-1}K\bigr);</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p2.3" class="ltx_p">roughly speaking, this means that we pull back <math id="SS8.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> to the “generic fiber” of <math id="SS8.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>,
and then retract onto the “special fiber”. Accordingly, <math id="SS8.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}K</annotation></semantics></math> contains
more information about the behavior of <math id="SS8.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> near the divisor <math id="SS8.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> than the naive
restriction <math id="SS8.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{-1}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{-1}K</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an obvious morphism <math id="SS8.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{-1}K\to\psi_{f}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{-1}K\to\psi_{f}K</annotation></semantics></math>,
and we define the <em id="SS8.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vanishing cycles</em></p>
<table id="SS8.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{f}K=\Cone\Bigl(i^{-1}K\to\psi_{f}K\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{f}K=\Cone\Bigl(i^{-1}K\to\psi_{f}K\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p2.4" class="ltx_p">By construction, there is a canonical morphism <math id="SS8.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\can\colon\psi_{f}K\to\phi_{f}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>can</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\can\colon\psi_{f}K\to\phi_{f}K</annotation></semantics></math>;
it is also possible to construct a morphism <math id="SS8.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\var\colon\phi_{f}K\to\psi_{f}K(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\var\colon\phi_{f}K\to\psi_{f}K(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>
going in the opposite direction.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            For historical reasons, Saito denotes this morphism by the symbol
“<math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Var}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Var</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Var}</annotation></semantics></math>”.</span></span></span>
Both <math id="SS8.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS8.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{f}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{f}K</annotation></semantics></math> are constructible
complexes of <math id="SS8.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces on <math id="SS8.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Gabber showed that when <math id="SS8.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a perverse
sheaf, the shifted complexes</p>
<table id="SS8.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f}K=\psi_{f}K[-1]\quad\text{and}\quad{}^{p}\phi_{f}K=\phi_{f}K[-1]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ϕ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f}K=\psi_{f}K[-1]\quad\text{and}\quad{}^{p}\phi_{f}K=\phi_{f}K[-1]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p2.5" class="ltx_p">are again perverse sheaves (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Brylinski</span>, p. 14]</cite> for more information). By
construction, the complex <math id="SS8.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f}K</annotation></semantics></math> has a monodromy operator <math id="SS8.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, induced by the
automorphism <math id="SS8.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\mapsto z+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\mapsto z+1</annotation></semantics></math> of the upper half-plane <math id="SS8.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since perverse sheaves
form an abelian category, we can decompose into generalized eigenspaces</p>
<table id="SS8.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f,\lambda}K=\ker(T-\lambda\id)^{m},\qquad\text{$m\gg 0$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f,\lambda}K=\ker(T-\lambda\id)^{m},\qquad\text{$m\gg 0$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p2.6" class="ltx_p">In summary, we have a decomposition</p>
<table id="SS8.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f}K=\bigoplus_{\lambda\in\mathbb{C}^{\times}}{}^{p}\psi_{f,\lambda}K" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f}K=\bigoplus_{\lambda\in\mathbb{C}^{\times}}{}^{p}\psi_{f,\lambda}K</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p2.7" class="ltx_p">in the category of perverse sheaves on <math id="SS8.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a similar
decomposition for <math id="SS8.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\phi_{f}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ϕ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\phi_{f}K</annotation></semantics></math>; by construction, <math id="SS8.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f,\lambda}K\simeq{}^{p}\phi_{f,\lambda}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ϕ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f,\lambda}K\simeq{}^{p}\phi_{f,\lambda}K</annotation></semantics></math> for every complex number <math id="SS8.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. On the
unipotent part <math id="SS8.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f,1}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f,1}K</annotation></semantics></math>, the composition <math id="SS8.p2.m37" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\var\circ\can" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>can</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\var\circ\can</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the
nilpotent operator <math id="SS8.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=(2\pi i)^{-1}\log T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=(2\pi i)^{-1}\log T</annotation></semantics></math>; the same goes for <math id="SS8.p2.m39" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\can\circ\var" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>can</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>var</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\can\circ\var</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="SS8.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\phi_{f,1}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ϕ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\phi_{f,1}K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As the Riemann-Hilbert correspondence would suggest, there is also a notion of nearby
and vanishing cycles for regular holonomic <math id="SS8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. It is due to Malgrange
and Kashiwara, and involves the use of an additional filtration
called the <em id="SS8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration</em>. Such a filtration only exists
when the divisor <math id="SS8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth; in general, one uses the graph embedding to reduce
to this situation.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let us first consider the case where we have a smooth function <math id="SS8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
and a global vector field <math id="SS8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="SS8.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\partial_{t},t]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\partial_{t},t]=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the <em id="SS8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration</em> on a right <math id="SS8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS8.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is
an increasing filtration <math id="SS8.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, indexed by <math id="SS8.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">each <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is coherent over <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}\mathscr{D}_{X}=\bigl\{\thinspace P\in\mathscr{D}_{X}\thinspace\thinspace\big|\thinspace\thinspace P\cdot I_{X_{0}}\subseteq I_{X_{0}}\thinspace\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.340em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}\mathscr{D}_{X}=\bigl\{\thinspace P\in\mathscr{D}_{X}\thinspace\thinspace\big|\thinspace\thinspace P\cdot I_{X_{0}}\subseteq I_{X_{0}}\thinspace\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}\cdot t\subseteq\mathcal{V}_{k-1}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}\cdot t\subseteq\mathcal{V}_{k-1}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}\cdot\partial_{t}\subseteq\mathcal{V}_{k+1}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}\cdot\partial_{t}\subseteq\mathcal{V}_{k+1}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}\cdot t=\mathcal{V}_{k-1}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}\cdot t=\mathcal{V}_{k-1}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\ll 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\ll 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">all eigenvalues of <math id="S2.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}/\mathcal{V}_{k-1}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}/\mathcal{V}_{k-1}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> have real part in <math id="S2.I3.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(k-1,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1,k]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="SS8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Kashiwara proved that a filtration with the above properties exists and is unique
provided that <math id="SS8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is holonomic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Kashiwara-V</span>]</cite>. One can also show that when
<math id="SS8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is (regular) holonomic, all the graded quotients <math id="SS8.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are
again (regular) holonomic <math id="SS8.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules on the complex submanifold <math id="SS8.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{-1}(0)\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{-1}(0)\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS8.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS8.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 8.1</span></span><span id="SS8.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS8.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prove the following results about the Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration:</p>
<ol id="S2.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{V}_{k-1}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{V}_{k-1}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism for <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{k+1}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{k+1}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
isomorphism for <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\neq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0em">≠</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\neq-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated, as a <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, by the subsheaf <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{0}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{0}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS8.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS8.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 8.2</span></span><span id="SS8.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS8.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Calculate the Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration when <math id="SS8.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{M}\subseteq t^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{M}\subseteq t^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS8.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS8.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 8.3</span></span><span id="SS8.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS8.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prove the uniqueness of the Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration by showing that there can
be at most one filtration <math id="SS8.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with the above properties.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS8.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS8.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 8.4</span></span><span id="SS8.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS8.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="SS8.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is the right <math id="SS8.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module defined by a vector bundle with
integrable connection. Calculate the Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration, and
show that <math id="SS8.E4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{-1}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{-1}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the restriction of <math id="SS8.E4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to the
submanifold <math id="SS8.E4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Now suppose that <math id="SS8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary non-constant holomorphic
function. To reduce to the smooth case, we consider the graph embedding</p>
<table id="SS8.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\id,f)\colon X\hookrightarrow X\times\mathbb{C},\qquad x\mapsto\bigl(x,f(x)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\id,f)\colon X\hookrightarrow X\times\mathbb{C},\qquad x\mapsto\bigl(x,f(x)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> be the coordinate on <math id="SS8.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="SS8.p6.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{t}=\partial/\partial t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}=\partial/\partial t</annotation></semantics></math>; note
that <math id="SS8.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\circ(\id,f)=f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\circ(\id,f)=f</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead of the original <math id="SS8.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS8.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider
the direct image</p>
<table id="SS8.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{f}=(\id,f)_{+}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}[\partial_{t}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{f}=(\id,f)_{+}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}[\partial_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p6.3" class="ltx_p">on <math id="SS8.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>; here the action by <math id="SS8.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the obvious one, and the action
by <math id="SS8.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by using the relation <math id="SS8.p6.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\partial_{t},t]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\partial_{t},t]=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="SS8.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="SS8.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> uniquely determine each other, it makes sense to consider the
Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration on <math id="SS8.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the smooth function <math id="SS8.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="SS8.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is regular holonomic, the Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration for <math id="SS8.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>
exists, and the graded quotients <math id="SS8.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> are again regular holonomic
<math id="SS8.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules on <math id="SS8.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=t^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=t^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> whose support is contained in the original divisor
<math id="SS8.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}=f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}=f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The regular holonomic <math id="SS8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="SS8.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> are related to the
nearby and vanishing cycles for the perverse sheaf <math id="SS8.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\DR(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\DR(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> in the following
manner. For any complex number <math id="SS8.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, let us define</p>
<table id="SS8.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{f,\alpha}=\ker(t\partial_{t}-\alpha\id)^{m},\qquad\text{$m\gg 0$,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ker</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>id</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{f,\alpha}=\ker(t\partial_{t}-\alpha\id)^{m},\qquad\text{$m\gg 0$,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p7.2" class="ltx_p">as the generalized eigenspace with eigenvalue <math id="SS8.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> for the action of <math id="SS8.p7.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS8.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="SS8.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique integer with <math id="SS8.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1&lt;\operatorname{Re}\alpha\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1&lt;\operatorname{Re}\alpha\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for <math id="SS8.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=e^{2\pi i\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=e^{2\pi i\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</p>
<table id="SS8.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\DR\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{f,\alpha}\bigr)&amp;\simeq{}^{p}\psi_{f,\lambda}\bigl(\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)\quad\text{for $-1\leq\operatorname{Re}\alpha&lt;0$},\\
\DR\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{f,\alpha}\bigr)&amp;\simeq{}^{p}\phi_{f,\lambda}\bigl(\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)\quad\text{for $-1&lt;\operatorname{Re}\alpha\leq 0$},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>DR</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>DR</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\DR\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{f,\alpha}\bigr)&amp;\simeq{}^{p}\psi_{f,\lambda}\bigl(\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)\quad\text{for $-1\leq\operatorname{Re}\alpha&lt;0$},\\
\DR\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{f,\alpha}\bigr)&amp;\simeq{}^{p}\phi_{f,\lambda}\bigl(\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)\quad\text{for $-1&lt;\operatorname{Re}\alpha\leq 0$},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p7.3" class="ltx_p">and the operator <math id="SS8.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> coming from the monodromy action corresponds to</p>
<table id="SS8.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e^{2\pi it\partial_{t}}=e^{2\pi i\alpha}\cdot e^{2\pi i(t\partial_{t}-\alpha)}=\lambda\cdot e^{2\pi i(t\partial_{t}-\alpha)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{2\pi it\partial_{t}}=e^{2\pi i\alpha}\cdot e^{2\pi i(t\partial_{t}-\alpha)}=\lambda\cdot e^{2\pi i(t\partial_{t}-\alpha)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p7.4" class="ltx_p">under this isomorphism. In the same way, the two morphisms</p>
<table id="SS8.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\can\colon{}^{p}\psi_{f,1}K\to{}^{p}\phi_{f,1}K\quad\text{and}\quad\var\colon{}^{p}\phi_{f,1}K\to{}^{p}\phi_{f,1}K(-1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>can</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ϕ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ϕ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ϕ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\can\colon{}^{p}\psi_{f,1}K\to{}^{p}\phi_{f,1}K\quad\text{and}\quad\var\colon{}^{p}\phi_{f,1}K\to{}^{p}\phi_{f,1}K(-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p7.5" class="ltx_p">correspond, on the level of <math id="SS8.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, to</p>
<table id="SS8.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS8.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon\mathcal{M}_{f,-1}\to\mathcal{M}_{f,0}\quad\text{and}\quad t\colon\mathcal{M}_{f,0}\to\mathcal{M}_{f,-1};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon\mathcal{M}_{f,-1}\to\mathcal{M}_{f,0}\quad\text{and}\quad t\colon\mathcal{M}_{f,0}\to\mathcal{M}_{f,-1};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS8.p7.6" class="ltx_p">the composition <math id="SS8.p7.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N=\var\circ\can" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>can</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\var\circ\can</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore represented by <math id="SS8.p7.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{t}t=t\partial_{t}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}t=t\partial_{t}+1</annotation></semantics></math> (since we are using right <math id="SS8.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules).</p>
</div>
<div id="SS8.E6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS8.E6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 8.6</span></span><span id="SS8.E6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS8.E6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS8.E6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS8.E6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V},\nabla)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V},\nabla)</annotation></semantics></math> be a flat bundle on the punctured disk <math id="SS8.E6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="SS8.E6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the right <math id="SS8.E6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module corresponding to the Deligne’s canonical meromorphic
extension. Show that the Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration with respect to the
coordinate <math id="SS8.E6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS8.E6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> contains exactly the same information as Deligne’s
canonical lattices for <math id="SS8.E6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V},\nabla)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V},\nabla)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9. </span>Nearby and vanishing cycles in Saito’s theory</h3>

<div id="SS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For reasons that will become clear in a moment, Saito does not directly use the
Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration; instead, he uses a refined notion that works better
for filtered <math id="SS9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Let <math id="SS9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)</annotation></semantics></math> be a
filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure on a complex manifold
<math id="SS9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a non-constant holomorphic function <math id="SS9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we would like to
define the nearby cycles <math id="SS9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}M</annotation></semantics></math> and the vanishing cycles <math id="SS9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{f}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{f}M</annotation></semantics></math> in the
category of filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="SS9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As before, we use the graph embedding <math id="SS9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\id,f)\colon X\hookrightarrow X\times\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\id,f)\colon X\hookrightarrow X\times\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and
replace the original filtered <math id="SS9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> by its
direct image</p>
<table id="SS9.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS9.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{M}_{f}&amp;=(\id,f)_{+}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}[\partial_{t}],\\
F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}&amp;=F_{\bullet}(\id,f)_{+}\mathcal{M}=\bigoplus_{i=0}^{\infty}F_{\bullet-i}\mathcal{M}\otimes\partial_{t}^{i}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⊗</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{M}_{f}&amp;=(\id,f)_{+}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}[\partial_{t}],\\
F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}&amp;=F_{\bullet}(\id,f)_{+}\mathcal{M}=\bigoplus_{i=0}^{\infty}F_{\bullet-i}\mathcal{M}\otimes\partial_{t}^{i}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS9.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration with respect to
<math id="SS9.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following Saito, we shall only consider objects with quasi-unipotent local monodromy;
this assumption comes from the theory of variations of Hodge structure
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Schmid</span>]</cite>. In our situation, it means that all eigenvalues of the monodromy
operator <math id="SS9.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS9.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f}K</annotation></semantics></math> are roots of unity.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            Saito makes this assumption in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-HM</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHM</span>]</cite>, but not in
certain other papers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-Kaehler</span>]</cite>.</span></span></span>
Then all eigenvalues of <math id="SS9.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS9.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{0}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{0}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> are rational
numbers, and one can introduce a refined filtration <math id="SS9.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by
<math id="SS9.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="SS9.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="SS9.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\lceil\alpha\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\lceil\alpha\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>, and define
<math id="SS9.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f}\subseteq\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f}\subseteq\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> as the preimage of</p>
<table id="SS9.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS9.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{k-1&lt;\beta\leq\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f,\beta}\subseteq\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{k-1&lt;\beta\leq\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f,\beta}\subseteq\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS9.p3.2" class="ltx_p">under the projection <math id="SS9.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{k}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>; we define <math id="SS9.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{&lt;\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{&lt;\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> in the same way, but taking the direct sum over <math id="SS9.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1&lt;\beta&lt;\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1&lt;\beta&lt;\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resulting filtration <math id="SS9.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <em id="SS9.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(rational)
<math id="SS9.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration</em>, to distinguish it from the Kashiwara-Malgrange filtration.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS9.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS9.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 9.2</span></span><span id="SS9.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS9.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS9.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prove the following assertions about the <math id="SS9.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration:</p>
<ol id="S2.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The operator <math id="S2.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t\partial_{t}-\alpha\id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\partial_{t}-\alpha\id</annotation></semantics></math> acts nilpotently on <math id="S2.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}=V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}\big/V_{&lt;\alpha}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}=V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}\big/V_{&lt;\alpha}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}\to V_{\alpha-1}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}\to V_{\alpha-1}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism
for <math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{\alpha+1}^{V}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{\alpha+1}^{V}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
isomorphism for <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\neq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0em">≠</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\neq-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(d)</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I5.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> only depends on the restriction of <math id="S2.I5.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.I5.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmnotex2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_note">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmnotex2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Note</span></span><span id="Thmnotex2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmnotex2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmnotex2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, one can consider the case where the eigenvalues of <math id="Thmnotex2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmnotex2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f}K</annotation></semantics></math> have absolute value <math id="Thmnotex2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>; the <math id="Thmnotex2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration is then naturally indexed by <math id="Thmnotex2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS9.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The correct choice of filtration on the nearby and vanishing cycles is a little bit
subtle. Let me explain what the issue is. The formulas in (<a href="#SS8.E5" title="In 8. Review of nearby and vanishing cycles ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.5</span></a>),
together with the obvious isomorphisms <math id="SS9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{f,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{f,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, yield</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="SS9.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS9.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\DR\left(\bigoplus_{-1\leq\alpha&lt;0}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\DR\left(\bigoplus_{-1\leq\alpha&lt;0}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS9.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\simeq\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}{}^{p}\psi_{f}K," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\simeq\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}{}^{p}\psi_{f}K,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="SS9.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS9.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\DR\left(\bigoplus_{-1&lt;\alpha\leq 0}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\DR\left(\bigoplus_{-1&lt;\alpha\leq 0}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS9.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\simeq\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}{}^{p}\phi_{f}K," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ϕ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\simeq\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}{}^{p}\phi_{f}K,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS9.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and the two <math id="SS9.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules on the left-hand side are regular holonomic. To make them
into filtered <math id="SS9.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, we should use a filtration that is compatible with the
decompositions above. There are two reasons for this: (1) In the case of a polarizable
variation of Hodge structure on a punctured disk, the correct choice of filtration on
the nearby cycles is the limit Hodge filtration; according to Schmid’s nilpotent
orbit theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Schmid</span>]</cite>, this filtration is compatible with the decomposition
into generalized eigenspaces. (2) On a more formal level, it turns out that certain
properties of <math id="SS9.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be described very naturally in terms of the filtration on the
individual <math id="SS9.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="SS9.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>; we shall revisit this point below.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS9.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS9.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The upshot is that we should endow each <math id="SS9.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS9.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> with
the filtration induced by <math id="SS9.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>; concretely, for <math id="SS9.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we set</p>
<table id="SS9.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS9.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}=\frac{F_{p}\mathcal{M}_{f}\cap V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f}}{F_{p}\mathcal{M}_{f}\cap V_{&lt;\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}=\frac{F_{p}\mathcal{M}_{f}\cap V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f}}{F_{p}\mathcal{M}_{f}\cap V_{&lt;\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS9.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Note that this leads to a different filtration on the <math id="SS9.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module</p>
<table id="SS9.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS9.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{0}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}\simeq\bigoplus_{-1&lt;\alpha\leq 0}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{0}^{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{M}_{f}\simeq\bigoplus_{-1&lt;\alpha\leq 0}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS9.p5.3" class="ltx_p">than if we simply took the filtration induced by <math id="SS9.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>; while this
choice might seem more natural, it would be wrong from the point of
view of Hodge theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS9.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS9.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For any non-constant holomorphic function <math id="SS9.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can now set</p>
<table id="SS9.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS9.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\psi_{f}M&amp;=\bigoplus_{-1\leq\alpha&lt;0}\Bigl(\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},F_{\bullet-1}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},{}^{p}\psi_{f,e^{2\pi i\alpha}}K\Bigr)\\
\psi_{f,1}M&amp;=\Bigl(\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},F_{\bullet-1}\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},{}^{p}\psi_{f,1}K\Bigr)\\
\phi_{f,1}M&amp;=\Bigl(\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},F_{\bullet}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},{}^{p}\phi_{f,1}K\Bigr)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ϕ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\psi_{f}M&amp;=\bigoplus_{-1\leq\alpha&lt;0}\Bigl(\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},F_{\bullet-1}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},{}^{p}\psi_{f,e^{2\pi i\alpha}}K\Bigr)\\
\psi_{f,1}M&amp;=\Bigl(\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},F_{\bullet-1}\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},{}^{p}\psi_{f,1}K\Bigr)\\
\phi_{f,1}M&amp;=\Bigl(\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},F_{\bullet}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f},{}^{p}\phi_{f,1}K\Bigr)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS9.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Except for <math id="SS9.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the individual perverse sheaves <math id="SS9.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f,\lambda}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f,\lambda}K</annotation></semantics></math> are
generally not defined over <math id="SS9.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>; in order to have a <math id="SS9.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure on
<math id="SS9.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}M</annotation></semantics></math>, we are forced to keep them together. Provided that the induced
filtration <math id="SS9.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is good for every <math id="SS9.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[-1,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[-1,0]</annotation></semantics></math>, all three objects are filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS9.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with
<math id="SS9.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure on <math id="SS9.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; by construction, their support is contained in the divisor
<math id="SS9.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. The logarithm of the unipotent part of the monodromy</p>
<table id="SS9.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS9.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\log T_{u}\colon\psi_{f}M\to\psi_{f}M(-1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\log T_{u}\colon\psi_{f}M\to\psi_{f}M(-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS9.p6.3" class="ltx_p">is a nilpotent endomorphism (up to a Tate twist).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS10" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10. </span>Decomposition by strict support</h3>

<div id="SS10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we can start thinking about the definition of Hodge modules. Recall that the
category <math id="SS10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> should be such that every polarizable Hodge module decomposes
into a finite sum</p>
<table id="SS10.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS10.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\simeq\bigoplus_{Z\subseteq X}M_{Z}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\simeq\bigoplus_{Z\subseteq X}M_{Z},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on an irreducible subvariety <math id="SS10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and in fact comes from a
variation of Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math> on a Zariski-open subset of the
smooth locus of <math id="SS10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. On the level of perverse sheaves, this means that <math id="SS10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> should
be an intersection complex, and should therefore not have nontrivial subobjects or
quotient objects that are supported on proper subvarieties of <math id="SS10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. The following
definition captures this property in the case of <math id="SS10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS10.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS10.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 10.1</span></span><span id="SS10.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS10.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS10.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS10.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> be an irreducible subvariety. We say that a <math id="SS10.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module
<math id="SS10.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> has <em id="SS10.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strict support</em> <math id="SS10.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> if the support of every nonzero subobject
or quotient object of <math id="SS10.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="SS10.E1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a regular holonomic <math id="SS10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module has strict support <math id="SS10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
the corresponding perverse sheaf <math id="SS10.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\DR(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\DR(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is the intersection complex of a local
system on a dense Zariski-open subset of <math id="SS10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. It turns out that this property can
also be detected with the help of the <math id="SS10.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS10.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS10.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 10.2</span></span><span id="SS10.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS10.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS10.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS10.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-constant holomorphic function, and let <math id="SS10.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be a
regular holonomic <math id="SS10.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module on <math id="SS10.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ol id="S2.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Show that <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> has no nonzero subobject supported on <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only
if <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Show that <math id="S2.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> has no nonzero quotient object supported on <math id="S2.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> if
and only if <math id="S2.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS10.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, one can use the <math id="SS10.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration to test whether or not <math id="SS10.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>
decomposes into a direct sum of <math id="SS10.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with strict support.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS10.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS10.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 10.3</span></span><span id="SS10.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS10.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS10.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS10.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-constant holomorphic function, and let <math id="SS10.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be a
regular holonomic <math id="SS10.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module on <math id="SS10.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Show that</p>
<table id="SS10.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS10.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}=\ker\bigl(t\colon\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\bigr)\oplus\im\bigl(\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ker</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>im</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.278em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}=\ker\bigl(t\colon\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\bigr)\oplus\im\bigl(\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS10.E3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">if and only if <math id="SS10.E3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\simeq\mathcal{M}^{\prime}\oplus\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\simeq\mathcal{M}^{\prime}\oplus\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="SS10.E3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{M}^{\prime}\subseteq f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{M}^{\prime}\subseteq f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="SS10.E3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> does not have nonzero subobjects or quotient objects whose
support is contained in <math id="SS10.E3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS10.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS10.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using the result of the preceding exercise, one can easily prove the following local
criterion for the existence of a decomposition by strict support. Note that such a
decomposition is necessarily unique, because there are no nontrivial morphisms
between <math id="SS10.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with different strict support.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS10.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS10.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.5</span></span><span id="SS10.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS10.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS10.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS10.E5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="SS10.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be a regular holonomic <math id="SS10.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module on <math id="SS10.E5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="SS10.E5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes
into a direct sum of <math id="SS10.E5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with strict support if and only if
(<a href="#SS10.E4" title="In Exercise 10.3. ‣ 10. Decomposition by strict support ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.4</span></a>) is true for every <math id="SS10.E5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS10.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS10.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This is the first indication that nearby and vanishing cycle functors are useful in
describing global properties of <math id="SS10.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules in terms of local conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">11. </span>Compatibility with the filtration</h3>

<div id="SS11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)</annotation></semantics></math> be a filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module
with <math id="SS11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure on a complex manifold <math id="SS11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Saito realized that the nearby and
vanishing cycle functors are also good for imposing restrictions on the filtration
<math id="SS11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. To see why this might be the case, let us suppose for a moment
that <math id="SS11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> has strict support <math id="SS11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="SS11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a
holomorphic function whose restriction to <math id="SS11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is not constant; the most interesting
case is when <math id="SS11.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the singular locus of <math id="SS11.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, or in other words,
when <math id="SS11.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the intersection complex of a local system on <math id="SS11.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\setminus Z\cap X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\setminus Z\cap X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify the notation, suppose that we are actually dealing with a smooth function
<math id="SS11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that there is a global vector field <math id="SS11.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="SS11.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\partial_{t},t]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\partial_{t},t]=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly, we can always put ourselves into this
situation by considering the <math id="SS11.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS11.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> on the product <math id="SS11.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>:
it will have strict support <math id="SS11.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{f}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{f}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the two filtrations <math id="SS11.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="SS11.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> determine each other by (<a href="#SS9.E1" title="In 9. Nearby and vanishing cycles in Saito’s theory ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>).
We know from <a href="#SS10.E2" title="Exercise 10.2. ‣ 10. Decomposition by strict support ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Exercise <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></span></a> that</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="SS11.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS11.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle t</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS11.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\colon\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\quad\text{is injective,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>gr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>V</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>gr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>V</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>is injective,</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\colon\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\quad\text{is injective,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="SS11.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS11.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS11.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\quad\text{is surjective.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>gr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>V</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>gr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>V</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>is surjective.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\quad\text{is surjective.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Because of the properties of the <math id="SS11.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration, one then has</p>
<table id="SS11.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS11.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\bigl(V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\partial_{t}^{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\bigl(V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\partial_{t}^{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and so <math id="SS11.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="SS11.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> as a right <math id="SS11.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module; recall from
<a href="#SS9.E2" title="Exercise 9.2. ‣ 9. Nearby and vanishing cycles in Saito’s theory ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Exercise <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.2</span></span></a> that this
is the part of the <math id="SS11.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration that only depends on the restriction of <math id="SS11.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="SS11.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We would like to make sure that the filtration <math id="SS11.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>
is also determined by its restriction to <math id="SS11.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, we need a
condition that allows us to recover the coherent sheaves</p>
<table id="SS11.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS11.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}=V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}\cap F_{p}\mathcal{M}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}=V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}\cap F_{p}\mathcal{M}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.p2.4" class="ltx_p">from information on <math id="SS11.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS11.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS11.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 11.1</span></span><span id="SS11.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS11.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="SS11.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\colon X\setminus X_{0}\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\colon X\setminus X_{0}\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> the inclusion. Show that one has</p>
<table id="SS11.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS11.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}=V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}\cap j_{\ast}j^{\ast}F_{p}\mathcal{M}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}=V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}\cap j_{\ast}j^{\ast}F_{p}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.E1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">if and only if <math id="SS11.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon F_{p}V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}\to F_{p}V_{\alpha-1}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon F_{p}V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}\to F_{p}V_{\alpha-1}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is
surjective for every <math id="SS11.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS11.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we observe that <math id="SS11.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="SS11.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl(F_{p-i}V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\partial_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(F_{p-i}V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\partial_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="SS11.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The following exercise gives a criterion for when
these subsheaves generate <math id="SS11.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS11.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS11.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 11.2</span></span><span id="SS11.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS11.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="SS11.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective. Show
that one has</p>
<table id="SS11.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS11.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{p}\mathcal{M}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\bigl(F_{p-i}V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\partial_{t}^{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\mathcal{M}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\bigl(F_{p-i}V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\partial_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.E2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">if and only if <math id="SS11.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon F_{p}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to F_{p+1}\gr_{\alpha+1}^{V}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon F_{p}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to F_{p+1}\gr_{\alpha+1}^{V}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective for every <math id="SS11.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\geq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\geq-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS11.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This criterion is one reason for considering the induced filtration on each
<math id="SS11.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Almost exactly the same condition also turns out to be useful
for describing the filtration in the case where the support of <math id="SS11.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained
in the divisor <math id="SS11.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS11.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS11.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 11.3</span></span><span id="SS11.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS11.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="SS11.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{M}\subseteq X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{M}\subseteq X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define a filtered <math id="SS11.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module on
<math id="SS11.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by setting <math id="SS11.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{0}=\ker(t\colon\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ker</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{0}=\ker(t\colon\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS11.E3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}\mathcal{M}_{0}=F_{p}\mathcal{M}\cap\mathcal{M}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\mathcal{M}_{0}=F_{p}\mathcal{M}\cap\mathcal{M}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Show that</p>
<table id="SS11.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS11.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{0}[\partial_{t}]\quad\text{and}\quad F_{p}\mathcal{M}\simeq\bigoplus_{i=0}^{\infty}F_{p-i}\mathcal{M}_{0}\otimes\partial_{t}^{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{0}[\partial_{t}]\quad\text{and}\quad F_{p}\mathcal{M}\simeq\bigoplus_{i=0}^{\infty}F_{p-i}\mathcal{M}_{0}\otimes\partial_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.E3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">if and only if <math id="SS11.E3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon F_{p}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to F_{p+1}\gr_{\alpha+1}^{V}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon F_{p}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to F_{p+1}\gr_{\alpha+1}^{V}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective for every <math id="SS11.E3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS11.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We now return to the case of an arbitrary filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS11.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module
<math id="SS11.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="SS11.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-constant holomorphic
function. The three exercises above motivate the following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS11.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS11.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 11.4</span></span><span id="SS11.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS11.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="SS11.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="SS11.E4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quasi-unipotent along <math id="SS11.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math></em> if
all eigenvalues of the monodromy operator on <math id="SS11.E4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f}K</annotation></semantics></math> are roots of unity, and if
the <math id="SS11.E4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration <math id="SS11.E4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the following two
additional conditions:</p>
<ol id="S2.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon F_{p}V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to F_{p}V_{\alpha-1}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon F_{p}V_{\alpha}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to F_{p}V_{\alpha-1}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective for
<math id="S2.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon F_{p}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to F_{p+1}\gr_{\alpha+1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon F_{p}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to F_{p+1}\gr_{\alpha+1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>
is surjective for <math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="SS11.E4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="SS11.E4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="SS11.E4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">regular along <math id="SS11.E4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math></em> if
<math id="SS11.E4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is a good filtration for every <math id="SS11.E4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1\leq\alpha\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1\leq\alpha\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS11.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The properties of the <math id="SS11.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration guarantee that the two morphisms in the
definition are isomorphisms in the given range. Note that we do not include <math id="SS11.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=-1</annotation></semantics></math> on the second line, because we want a notion that also makes sense when
<math id="SS11.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is not surjective. The
regularity condition ensures that <math id="SS11.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS11.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{f,1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{f,1}M</annotation></semantics></math> are filtered regular
holonomic <math id="SS11.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="SS11.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS11.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To connect the definition with the discussion above, suppose that <math id="SS11.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> has strict support <math id="SS11.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and that it is quasi-unipotent and
regular along <math id="SS11.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> for a holomorphic function <math id="SS11.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> whose
restriction to <math id="SS11.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is not constant. Then</p>
<table id="SS11.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS11.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{p}\mathcal{M}_{f}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\bigl(V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}_{f}\cap j_{\ast}j^{\ast}F_{p-i}\mathcal{M}_{f}\bigr)\partial_{t}^{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\mathcal{M}_{f}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\bigl(V_{&lt;0}\mathcal{M}_{f}\cap j_{\ast}j^{\ast}F_{p-i}\mathcal{M}_{f}\bigr)\partial_{t}^{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.p7.2" class="ltx_p">provided that <math id="SS11.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon F_{p}\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to F_{p+1}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon F_{p}\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to F_{p+1}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is
also surjective. This last condition will be automatically satisfied for Hodge
modules: the recursive definition (in <a href="#SS12" title="12. Definition of pure Hodge modules ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></span></a>) implies that the morphism</p>
<table id="SS11.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS11.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.p7.3" class="ltx_p">is strictly compatible with the filtrations (up to a shift by <math id="SS11.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>), and
(<a href="#SS11.E5" title="In 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.5</span></a>) therefore follows from the surjectivity of <math id="SS11.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. What
this means is that the filtered <math id="SS11.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS11.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is
uniquely determined by its restriction to <math id="SS11.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\setminus Z\cap X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\setminus Z\cap X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS11.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Another good feature of the conditions in <a href="#SS11.E4" title="Definition 11.4. ‣ 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Definition <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.4</span></span></a> is that
they say something interesting even when the support of <math id="SS11.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the
divisor <math id="SS11.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS11.E6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS11.E6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 11.6</span></span><span id="SS11.E6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS11.E6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.E6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="SS11.E6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{M}\subseteq f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{M}\subseteq f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Show that <math id="SS11.E6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent and regular along <math id="SS11.E6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
the filtration satisfies</p>
<table id="SS11.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS11.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F_{p}\mathcal{M})\cdot f\subseteq F_{p-1}\mathcal{M}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F_{p}\mathcal{M})\cdot f\subseteq F_{p-1}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.E6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="SS11.E6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS11.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We can also upgrade the criterion in <a href="#SS10.E5" title="Proposition 10.5. ‣ 10. Decomposition by strict support ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Proposition <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.5</span></span></a> to filtered
regular holonomic <math id="SS11.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="SS11.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure. In the statement, note that
the filtration on the image of <math id="SS11.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\can\colon\psi_{f,1}M\to\phi_{f,1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>can</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\can\colon\psi_{f,1}M\to\phi_{f,1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is induced
by that on <math id="SS11.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f,1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f,1}M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS11.E7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS11.E7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11.7</span></span><span id="SS11.E7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS11.E7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS11.E7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS11.E7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="SS11.E7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS11.E7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS11.E7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure, and
suppose that <math id="SS11.E7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent and regular along
<math id="SS11.E7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> for every locally defined holomorphic function <math id="SS11.E7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="SS11.E7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a
decomposition</span></p>
<table id="SS11.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS11.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\simeq\bigoplus_{Z\subseteq X}M_{Z}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\simeq\bigoplus_{Z\subseteq X}M_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.E7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS11.E7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">by strict support, in which each <math id="SS11.E7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is again a filtered regular holonomic
<math id="SS11.E7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS11.E7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure, if and only if one has</span></p>
<table id="SS11.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS11.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi_{f,1}M=\ker\bigl(\var\colon\phi_{f,1}M\to\psi_{f,1}M(-1)\bigr)\oplus\im\bigl(\can\colon\psi_{f,1}M\to\phi_{f,1}M\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ker</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>var</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>im</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>can</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>M</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{f,1}M=\ker\bigl(\var\colon\phi_{f,1}M\to\psi_{f,1}M(-1)\bigr)\oplus\im\bigl(\can\colon\psi_{f,1}M\to\phi_{f,1}M\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS11.E7.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS11.E7.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for every locally defined holomorphic function <math id="SS11.E7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS12" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">12. </span>Definition of pure Hodge modules</h3>

<div id="SS12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After these technical preliminaries, we are now ready to define the category of pure
Hodge modules. The basic objects are filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with
<math id="SS12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure; we have to decide when a given <math id="SS12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)</annotation></semantics></math>
should be called a Hodge module. Saito uses a recursive procedure to define a family
of auxiliary categories</p>
<table id="SS12.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS12.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}_{\leq d}(X,w)=\left\{\,\text{\parbox{147.95424pt}{Hodge modules of weight $w$ on $X$ \\
whose support has dimension $\leq d$}}\,\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_parbox">
<span class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:148.0pt;"> 
<span class="ltx_p">Hodge modules of weight <math alttext="w" display="inline"><mi>w</mi></math> on <math alttext="X" display="inline"><mi>X</mi></math></span> 
<span class="ltx_p">whose support has dimension <math alttext="\leq d" display="inline"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> 
</span></mtext><mo lspace="0.170em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}_{\leq d}(X,w)=\left\{\,\text{\parbox{147.95424pt}{Hodge modules of weight $w$ on $X$ \\
whose support has dimension $\leq d$}}\,\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS12.p1.2" class="ltx_p">indexed by <math id="SS12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. This procedure has the advantage that results can then be
proved by induction on the dimension of the support.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            Saito says that he found the definition by axiomatizing certain
arguments that he used to prove <a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a> in the case <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[\dim X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[\dim X]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>
Since the definition involves the nearby and vanishing cycle functors, we impose the
following condition:</p>
<table id="SS12.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="SS12.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">
<span id="SS12.E1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:284.5pt;">
<span id="SS12.E1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">The pair <math id="SS12.E1.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent and regular along <math id="SS12.E1.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="SS12.E1.2.1.2" class="ltx_p">for every locally defined holomorphic function <math id="SS12.E1.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon U\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon U\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS12.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Both <math id="SS12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{f,1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{f,1}M</annotation></semantics></math> are then well-defined filtered regular holonomic
<math id="SS12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="SS12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure on <math id="SS12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, whose support is contained in
<math id="SS12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we are only interested in objects that admit a
decomposition by strict support, we require:</p>
<table id="SS12.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="SS12.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">
<span id="SS12.E2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:284.5pt;">
<span id="SS12.E2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="SS12.E2.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a decomposition by strict support,
in the category</span>
<span id="SS12.E2.2.1.2" class="ltx_p">of regular holonomic <math id="SS12.E2.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="SS12.E2.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure.</span>
</span></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS12.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Recall that this can also be tested with the help of the nearby and vanishing cycle
functors, using the criterion in <a href="#SS11.E7" title="Theorem 11.7. ‣ 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.7</span></span></a>. Now the problem is
reduced to defining, for each irreducible closed subvariety <math id="SS12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>, a
suitable category</p>
<table id="SS12.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS12.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}_{Z}(X,w)=\left\{\,\text{\parbox{142.26378pt}{Hodge modules of weight $w$ on $X$ \\
with strict support equal to $Z$}}\,\right\};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_parbox">
<span class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:142.3pt;"> 
<span class="ltx_p">Hodge modules of weight <math alttext="w" display="inline"><mi>w</mi></math> on <math alttext="X" display="inline"><mi>X</mi></math></span> 
<span class="ltx_p">with strict support equal to <math alttext="Z" display="inline"><mi>Z</mi></math></span> 
</span></mtext><mo lspace="0.170em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}_{Z}(X,w)=\left\{\,\text{\parbox{142.26378pt}{Hodge modules of weight $w$ on $X$ \\
with strict support equal to $Z$}}\,\right\};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS12.p1.5" class="ltx_p">we can then take for <math id="SS12.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}_{\leq d}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}_{\leq d}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> the direct sum of all <math id="SS12.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}_{Z}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}_{Z}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="SS12.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Z\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Z\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS12.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first case to consider is obviously when <math id="SS12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a point. Here the
perverse sheaf <math id="SS12.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct image of a <math id="SS12.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space by the morphism
<math id="SS12.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\colon\{x\}\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\colon\{x\}\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math>, and this suggests defining</p>
<table id="SS12.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS12.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}_{\{x\}}(X,w)=\bigl\{\thinspace i_{\ast}H\thinspace\thinspace\big|\thinspace\thinspace\text{$H$ is a $\mathbb{Q}$-Hodge structure of weight $w$}\thinspace\bigr\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.340em">|</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mtext> is a </mtext><mi>ℚ</mi><mtext>-Hodge structure of weight </mtext><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}_{\{x\}}(X,w)=\bigl\{\thinspace i_{\ast}H\thinspace\thinspace\big|\thinspace\thinspace\text{$H$ is a $\mathbb{Q}$-Hodge structure of weight $w$}\thinspace\bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS12.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Now comes the most important part of the definition: for
arbitrary <math id="SS12.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that <math id="SS12.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}_{Z}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}_{Z}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if, for
every locally defined holomorphic function <math id="SS12.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon U\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon U\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> that does not vanish
identically on <math id="SS12.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\cap U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\cap U</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</p>
<table id="SS12.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS12.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\ell}^{W(N)}(\psi_{f}M)\in\operatorname{HM}_{\leq d-1}(X,w-1+\ell)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\ell}^{W(N)}(\psi_{f}M)\in\operatorname{HM}_{\leq d-1}(X,w-1+\ell).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS12.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="SS12.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(N)</annotation></semantics></math> is the monodromy filtration of the nilpotent operator <math id="SS12.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=(2\pi i)^{-1}\log T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=(2\pi i)^{-1}\log T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> on the nearby cycles <math id="SS12.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}M</annotation></semantics></math>. One can deduce from this last condition
that</p>
<table id="SS12.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS12.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\ell}^{W(N)}(\phi_{f,1}M)\in\operatorname{HM}_{\leq d-1}(X,w+\ell)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\ell}^{W(N)}(\phi_{f,1}M)\in\operatorname{HM}_{\leq d-1}(X,w+\ell),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS12.p2.4" class="ltx_p">by using the isomorphism in <a href="#SS11.E7" title="Theorem 11.7. ‣ 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.7</span></span></a>. Of course, the motivation
for using the monodromy filtration comes from Schmid’s <math id="SS12.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL(2)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit theorem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Schmid</span>]</cite>: in the case of a polarizable variation of Hodge structure on the
punctured disk, the nearby cycles carry a mixed Hodge structure whose Hodge
filtration is the limit Hodge filtration and whose weight filtration is the monodromy
filtration of <math id="SS12.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, up to a shift by the weight of the variation.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS12.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS12.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 12.5</span></span><span id="SS12.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS12.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS12.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The category of <em id="SS12.E5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hodge modules of weight <math id="SS12.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math></em> on <math id="SS12.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has objects</p>
<table id="SS12.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS12.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}(X,w)=\bigcup_{d\geq 0}\operatorname{HM}_{\leq d}(X,w)=\bigoplus_{Z\subseteq X}\operatorname{HM}_{Z}(X,w);" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}(X,w)=\bigcup_{d\geq 0}\operatorname{HM}_{\leq d}(X,w)=\bigoplus_{Z\subseteq X}\operatorname{HM}_{Z}(X,w);</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS12.E5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">its morphisms are the morphisms of regular holonomic <math id="SS12.E5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with
<math id="SS12.E5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS12.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS12.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One can show that every morphism between two Hodge modules is strictly compatible
with the filtrations, and that <math id="SS12.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore an abelian category.
Moreover, one can show that there are no nontrivial morphisms from one Hodge module
to another Hodge module of strictly smaller weight.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS12.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS12.p4.1" class="ltx_p">An important point is that all the conditions in the definition are local; Saito’s
insight is that they are strong enough to have global consequences, such as the
decomposition theorem. Of course, the recursive nature of the definition makes it
hard to prove that a given <math id="SS12.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge module: in fact, it takes considerable work
to establish that even a very basic object like</p>
<table id="SS12.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS12.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\omega_{X},F_{\bullet}\omega_{X},\mathbb{Q}_{X}[n]\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\omega_{X},F_{\bullet}\omega_{X},\mathbb{Q}_{X}[n]\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS12.p4.2" class="ltx_p">is a Hodge module of weight <math id="SS12.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>. It is also not clear that Hodge modules are
stable under various operations such as direct or inverse images – about the only
thing that is obvious from the definition is that</p>
<table id="SS12.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS12.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}(pt,w)=\bigl\{\text{$\mathbb{Q}$-Hodge structures of weight $w$}\bigr\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mtext>-Hodge structures of weight </mtext><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}(pt,w)=\bigl\{\text{$\mathbb{Q}$-Hodge structures of weight $w$}\bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS12.p4.3" class="ltx_p">On the positive side, Hodge modules are by definition stable under the application of
nearby and vanishing cycle functors: once we know that something is a Hodge module,
we immediately get a large collection of other Hodge modules by taking nearby and
vanishing cycles.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS12.E6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS12.E6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 12.6</span></span><span id="SS12.E6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS12.E6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS12.E6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="SS12.E6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge module on a smooth curve <math id="SS12.E6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for every local coordinate
<math id="SS12.E6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the nearby cycles <math id="SS12.E6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{t}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{t}M</annotation></semantics></math> carry a mixed Hodge structure.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS13" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">13. </span>Polarizations</h3>

<div id="SS13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to define polarizable Hodge modules, we also need to introduce the concept
of a polarization. Let <math id="SS13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)</annotation></semantics></math> be a filtered regular
holonomic <math id="SS13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure on an <math id="SS13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional complex manifold
<math id="SS13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; of course, we will be mostly interested in the case <math id="SS13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS13.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that in the case of a variation of Hodge structure <math id="SS13.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="SS13.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-n</annotation></semantics></math>, a
polarization is a bilinear form <math id="SS13.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}V\to\mathbb{Q}(-w+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}V\to\mathbb{Q}(-w+n)</annotation></semantics></math>
whose restriction to every point <math id="SS13.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math> polarizes the Hodge structure
<math id="SS13.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>; equivalently, it is a <math id="SS13.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear mapping <math id="SS13.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(w-n)\to V^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(w-n)\to V^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same
property. The natural analogue for perverse sheaves is to consider
morphisms of the form</p>
<table id="SS13.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS13.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(w)\to\mathbf{D}K," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(w)\to\mathbf{D}K,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS13.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS13.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}K=\mathbf{R}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}\bigl(K,\mathbb{Q}_{X}(n)\bigr)[2n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑜𝑚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}K=\mathbf{R}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}\bigl(K,\mathbb{Q}_{X}(n)\bigr)[2n]</annotation></semantics></math> is the Verdier dual
of the perverse sheaf <math id="SS13.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, with a Tate twist to conform to Saito’s
conventions for weights. This suggests that a polarization should be a
morphism <math id="SS13.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(w)\to\mathbf{D}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(w)\to\mathbf{D}M</annotation></semantics></math> … except that there is no duality functor for
arbitrary filtered <math id="SS13.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. The holonomic dual</p>
<table id="SS13.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS13.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{n}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{D}_{X}}\bigl(\mathcal{M},\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{D}_{X}\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑜𝑚</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{n}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{D}_{X}}\bigl(\mathcal{M},\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{D}_{X}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS13.p2.3" class="ltx_p">of <math id="SS13.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a regular holonomic <math id="SS13.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, whose de Rham complex is isomorphic
to <math id="SS13.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}\bigl(\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}\bigl(\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>; but this operation does not interact well with the
filtration <math id="SS13.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, a necessary and sufficient condition is that
<math id="SS13.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is Cohen-Macaulay, meaning that <math id="SS13.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> should be a Cohen-Macaulay module over <math id="SS13.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fortunately, Saito has shown that Hodge modules always have this property, and that
every <math id="SS13.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> has a well-defined dual <math id="SS13.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,-w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,-w)</annotation></semantics></math>; see
<a href="#SS29" title="29. Duality functor ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS13.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS13.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With this issue out of the way, here is the definition. A <em id="SS13.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">polarization</em> on a
Hodge module <math id="SS13.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism <math id="SS13.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(w)\to\mathbf{D}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(w)\to\mathbf{D}K</annotation></semantics></math> with the following
properties:</p>
<ol id="S2.I8" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is nondegenerate and compatible with the filtration, meaning that it extends to
an isomorphism <math id="S2.I8.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(w)\simeq\mathbf{D}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(w)\simeq\mathbf{D}M</annotation></semantics></math> in the category of Hodge modules.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every summand <math id="S2.I8.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> in the decomposition of <math id="S2.I8.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by strict support, and for
every locally defined holomorphic function <math id="S2.I8.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon U\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon U\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> that is not
identically zero on <math id="S2.I8.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\cap Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\cap Z</annotation></semantics></math>, the induced morphism</p>
<table id="SS13.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS13.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f}K_{Z}(w)\to\mathbf{D}({}^{p}\psi_{f}K_{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f}K_{Z}(w)\to\mathbf{D}({}^{p}\psi_{f}K_{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I8.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a polarization of Hodge-Lefschetz type (= on primitive parts for <math id="S2.I8.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I8.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I8.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I8.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I8.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\Supp M_{Z}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\Supp M_{Z}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.I8.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{Z}(w)\to\mathbf{D}K_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{Z}(w)\to\mathbf{D}K_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by
a polarization of Hodge structures in the usual sense.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="SS13.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS13.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The second condition uses the compatibility of the duality functor with nearby
cycles, and the fact that <math id="SS13.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(w)\to\mathbf{D}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(w)\to\mathbf{D}K</annotation></semantics></math> is automatically compatible with the
decomposition by strict support (because there are nonontrivial morphisms
between perverse sheaves with different strict support). Since <math id="SS13.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\psi_{f}K_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>f</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\psi_{f}K_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is by
construction supported in a subset of smaller dimension, the
definition is again recursive.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS13.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS13.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 13.1</span></span><span id="SS13.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS13.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS13.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS13.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be the filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS13.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS13.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure
associated with a variation of Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS13.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. Provided one is
sufficiently careful with signs, a polarization <math id="SS13.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}V\to\mathbb{Q}(-w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}V\to\mathbb{Q}(-w)</annotation></semantics></math>
determines a morphism <math id="SS13.E1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(w+\dim X)\to\mathbf{D}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(w+\dim X)\to\mathbf{D}M</annotation></semantics></math>; we will see later that it is a
polarization in the above sense.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS13.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS13.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that we do not directly require any “positivity” of the morphism giving the
polarization; instead, we are asking that once we apply sufficiently many nearby
cycle functors to end up with a vector space, certain induced Hermitian forms on this
vector space are positive definite. Because of the indirect nature of this definition,
the problem of signs is quite nontrivial, and one has to be extremely careful in
choosing the signs in various isomorphisms. For that reason, Saito actually defines
polarizations not as morphisms <math id="SS13.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(w)\to\mathbf{D}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(w)\to\mathbf{D}K</annotation></semantics></math>, but as pairings <math id="SS13.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}K\to\mathbb{Q}(-w)[2n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}K\to\mathbb{Q}(-w)[2n]</annotation></semantics></math>; this is equivalent to our definition, but makes it
easier to keep track of signs.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS13.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS13.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 13.2</span></span><span id="SS13.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS13.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS13.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a Hodge module is <em id="SS13.E2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">polarizable</em> if it admits at least one
polarization, and we denote by</p>
<table id="SS13.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS13.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)\subseteq\operatorname{HM}(X,w)\quad\text{and}\quad\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)\subseteq\operatorname{HM}_{Z}(X,w)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)\subseteq\operatorname{HM}(X,w)\quad\text{and}\quad\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)\subseteq\operatorname{HM}_{Z}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS13.E2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">the full subcategories of polarizable Hodge modules.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS14" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">14. </span>Kashiwara’s equivalence and singular spaces</h3>

<div id="SS14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To close this chapter, let me say a few words about the definition of Hodge modules
on analytic spaces; a systematic treatment of <math id="SS14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules on analytic spaces can
be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-D</span>]</cite>. The idea is similar to how one defines holomorphic
functions on analytic spaces: embed a given analytic space <math id="SS14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> into a complex
manifold, and consider objects on the ambient manifold that are supported on
<math id="SS14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Of course, such embeddings may only exist locally, and so in the most general
case, one has to cover <math id="SS14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by embeddable open subsets and impose conditions on the
pairwise intersections.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS14.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS14.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify the discussion, we shall only consider those analytic spaces <math id="SS14.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> that are
globally embeddable into a complex manifold; this class includes, for example, all
quasi-projective algebraic varieties. By definition, a right <math id="SS14.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module on
<math id="SS14.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a right <math id="SS14.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module on the ambient manifold whose support is contained in
<math id="SS14.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. This definition is independent of the choice of embedding because of the
following fundamental result by Kashiwara <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Kashiwara-th</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS14.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS14.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 14.1</span></span><span id="SS14.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Kashiwara’s Equivalence)<span id="SS14.E1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS14.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS14.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS14.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="SS14.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex manifold, and <math id="SS14.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\colon Z\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\colon Z\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> the inclusion of a
closed submanifold. Then the direct image functor</span></p>
<table id="SS14.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS14.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{+}\colon M_{\mathit{coh}}(\mathscr{D}_{Z})\to M_{\mathit{coh}}(\mathscr{D}_{X})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{+}\colon M_{\mathit{coh}}(\mathscr{D}_{Z})\to M_{\mathit{coh}}(\mathscr{D}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS14.E1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS14.E1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gives an equivalence between the category of coherent right <math id="SS14.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules
and the category of coherent right <math id="SS14.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules whose support is contained in <math id="SS14.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS14.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS14.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 14.2</span></span><span id="SS14.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS14.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS14.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="SS14.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=t^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=t^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> for a holomorphic function <math id="SS14.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Prove
Kashiwara’s equivalence in this case, by using the decomposition given by the kernels
of the operators <math id="SS14.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t\partial_{t}-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\partial_{t}-i</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="SS14.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS14.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS14.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since the direct image functor for left <math id="SS14.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules involves an additional twist
by the relative canonical bundle <math id="SS14.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{Z}\otimes\omega_{X}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{Z}\otimes\omega_{X}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, both Kashiwara’s
equivalence and the definition of <math id="SS14.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules on singular spaces are more natural
for right <math id="SS14.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS14.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS14.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 14.3</span></span><span id="SS14.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS14.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS14.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS14.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be an analytic space that can be globally embedded into a complex manifold
<math id="SS14.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we define</p>
<table id="SS14.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS14.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}(X,w)\subseteq\operatorname{HM}(Y,w)\quad\text{and}\quad\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)\subseteq\operatorname{HM}^{p}(Y,w)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}(X,w)\subseteq\operatorname{HM}(Y,w)\quad\text{and}\quad\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)\subseteq\operatorname{HM}^{p}(Y,w)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS14.E3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">by taking all (polarizable) Hodge modules on <math id="SS14.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> whose support is contained in <math id="SS14.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS14.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS14.p4.1" class="ltx_p">One subtle point is that Kashiwara’s equivalence is not true for filtered
<math id="SS14.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, because a coherent sheaf with support in <math id="SS14.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is not the same thing
as a coherent sheaf on <math id="SS14.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. To prove that the category <math id="SS14.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of
the choice of embedding, one has to use the fact that the filtered <math id="SS14.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules in
Saito’s theory are always quasi-unipotent and regular.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS14.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS14.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 14.4</span></span><span id="SS14.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS14.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS14.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the notation of <a href="#SS14.E1" title="Proposition 14.1 (Kashiwara’s Equivalence). ‣ 14. Kashiwara’s equivalence and singular spaces ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Proposition <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.1</span></span></a>, suppose that <math id="SS14.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{M}\subseteq Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{M}\subseteq Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="SS14.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent and regular
along <math id="SS14.E4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> for every locally defined holomorphic function in the ideal of <math id="SS14.E4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
Show that</p>
<table id="SS14.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS14.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})\simeq i_{+}(\mathcal{M}_{Z},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})\simeq i_{+}(\mathcal{M}_{Z},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS14.E4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for a filtered <math id="SS14.E4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS14.E4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M}_{Z},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M}_{Z},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS14.E4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, unique up to
isomorphism.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS14.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS14.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now let <math id="SS14.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>. The underlying regular holonomic right <math id="SS14.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module
<math id="SS14.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as the coherent sheaves <math id="SS14.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Hodge filtration,
are really defined on the ambient complex manifold <math id="SS14.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>; they are not objects on <math id="SS14.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
although the support of <math id="SS14.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="SS14.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, the result in
<a href="#SS11.E6" title="Exercise 11.6. ‣ 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Exercise <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.6</span></span></a> can be used to show that each <math id="SS14.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}^{F}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}^{F}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a coherent sheaf
on <math id="SS14.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS14.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS14.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 14.5</span></span><span id="SS14.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS14.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS14.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS14.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>. Deduce from <a href="#SS14.E4" title="Exercise 14.4. ‣ 14. Kashiwara’s equivalence and singular spaces ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Exercise <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.4</span></span></a> that the graded
quotients <math id="SS14.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> of the de Rham complex are well-defined complexes of
coherent sheaves on <math id="SS14.E5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, independent of the choice of embedding.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">C. </span>Two important theorems</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this chapter, we describe two of Saito’s most important results: the structure
theorem and the direct image theorem. The structure theorem relates polarizable Hodge
modules and polarizable variations of Hodge structure; the direct image theorem says
that polarizable Hodge modules are stable under direct images by projective
morphisms. Taken together, they justify the somewhat complicated definition of the
category <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="SS15" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">15. </span>Structure theorem</h3>

<div id="SS15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of the main results in Saito’s second paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHM</span>]</cite> is that polarizable
Hodge modules on <math id="SS15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with strict support <math id="SS15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> are the same thing as generically
defined polarized variations of Hodge structure on <math id="SS15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS15.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS15.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 15.1</span></span><span id="SS15.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Structure Theorem)<span id="SS15.E1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS15.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS15.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS15.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="SS15.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex manifold, and <math id="SS15.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> an irreducible closed
analytic subvariety.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every polarizable variation of </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-Hodge structure of weight </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on a
Zariski-open subset of </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> extends uniquely to an object of </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every object of </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is obtained in this way.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS15.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS15.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Together with the condition (<a href="#SS12.E2" title="In 12. Definition of pure Hodge modules ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.2</span></a>) in the definition of Hodge modules,
this result implies that every polarizable Hodge <math id="SS15.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form</p>
<table id="SS15.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS15.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\bigoplus_{Z\subseteq X}M_{Z}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\bigoplus_{Z\subseteq X}M_{Z},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS15.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS15.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from a polarizable variation of Hodge structure of
weight <math id="SS15.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math> on a Zariski-open subset of the smooth locus of <math id="SS15.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>; conversely,
every object of this type is a polarizable Hodge module. We have therefore achieved
our goal, which was to describe this class in terms of local conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS15.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS15.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 15.2</span></span><span id="SS15.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS15.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS15.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On every complex manifold <math id="SS15.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="SS15.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS15.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]=\bigl(\omega_{X},F_{\bullet}\omega_{X},\mathbb{Q}_{X}[n]\bigr)\in\operatorname{HM}_{X}^{p}(X,n)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]=\bigl(\omega_{X},F_{\bullet}\omega_{X},\mathbb{Q}_{X}[n]\bigr)\in\operatorname{HM}_{X}^{p}(X,n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS15.E2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a polarizable Hodge module of weight <math id="SS15.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS15.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS15.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 15.3</span></span><span id="SS15.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS15.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS15.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, we can consider the constant variation of Hodge structure on the
smooth locus of an irreducible analytic subvariety <math id="SS15.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>. By
<a href="#SS15.E1" title="Theorem 15.1 (Structure Theorem). ‣ 15. Structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></span></a>, it determines a polarizable Hodge module of weight <math id="SS15.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS15.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; the underlying perverse sheaf is the intersection complex of <math id="SS15.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS15.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS15.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 15.4</span></span><span id="SS15.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS15.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS15.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Another consequence of the structure theorem is that the inverse image of a polarizable
Hodge module under a smooth morphism <math id="SS15.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is again a polarizable Hodge
module (with a shift in weight by <math id="SS15.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Y-\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Y-\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>); see <a href="#SS30" title="30. Inverse image functors ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></span></a>.
This statement looks innocent enough, but trying to prove it directly from the
definition is hopeless, because there are too many additional functions on <math id="SS15.E4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS15.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS15.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of the second assertion in <a href="#SS15.E1" title="Theorem 15.1 (Structure Theorem). ‣ 15. Structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></span></a> is not hard. In
fact, given <math id="SS15.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a Zariski-open subset of the smooth locus
of <math id="SS15.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="SS15.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rat</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat M</annotation></semantics></math> is a local system (up to a shift), and one has all the data
necessary to define a polarizable variation of Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS15.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math>. One then uses the definition to argue that the variation of Hodge structure uniquely
determines the original <math id="SS15.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>: for example, (<a href="#SS11.E5" title="In 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.5</span></a>) shows how to recover
<math id="SS15.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. The real content is therefore in the first
assertion; we shall say more about the proof in <a href="#SS18" title="18. Proof of the structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></span></a> below.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS16" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">16. </span>Direct image theorem</h3>

<div id="SS16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the local nature of the definition, it is also not clear that the category of Hodge
modules is preserved by the direct image functor. The main result in Saito’s first
paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-HM</span>]</cite> is that this is true for direct images by projective morphisms.
Along the way, Saito also proved the decomposition theorem for those perverse sheaves
that underlie polarizable Hodge modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS16.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS16.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS16.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a projective morphism between complex manifolds, and let</p>
<table id="SS16.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS16.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS16.p2.2" class="ltx_p">be a filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS16.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS16.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure. In a suitable
derived category, one can define the direct image <math id="SS16.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math>: the underlying
constructible complex is <math id="SS16.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}K</annotation></semantics></math>, and the underlying complex of filtered
<math id="SS16.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules is <math id="SS16.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>; see <a href="#SS27" title="27. Direct image functor ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></span></a> for
more details. The content of the direct image theorem is, roughly speaking, that <math id="SS16.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="SS16.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(Y,w+i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(Y,w+i)</annotation></semantics></math>. A tricky point is that the
cohomology sheaves <math id="SS16.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> are in general not filtered
<math id="SS16.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, but live in a larger abelian category (see <a href="#SS26" title="26. Derived category and strictness ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></span></a> below).
Unless the complex <math id="SS16.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is what is called “strict”, it
is therefore not possible to define <math id="SS16.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math> as a filtered regular
holonomic <math id="SS16.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS16.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS16.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS16.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Before we can give the precise statement of the direct image theorem, we need to
introduce the Lefschetz operator; it plays an even greater role here than in
classical Hodge theory. Let <math id="SS16.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in H^{2}\bigl(X,\mathbb{Z}(1)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in H^{2}\bigl(X,\mathbb{Z}(1)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> be the first Chern
class of a relatively ample line bundle on <math id="SS16.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. It gives rise to a morphism
<math id="SS16.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\colon K\to K(1)[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\colon K\to K(1)[2]</annotation></semantics></math> in the derived category of constructible complexes
on <math id="SS16.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; using the fact that <math id="SS16.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> can also be represented by a closed <math id="SS16.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form,
one can lift this morphism to a morphism <math id="SS16.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\colon M\to M(1)[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\colon M\to M(1)[2]</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we
apply the direct image functor; provided that <math id="SS16.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is strict,
we obtain a collection of morphisms</p>
<table id="SS16.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS16.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\colon\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M\to\mathcal{H}^{i+2}f_{\ast}M(1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\colon\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M\to\mathcal{H}^{i+2}f_{\ast}M(1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS16.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which together constitute the <em id="SS16.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lefschetz operator</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS16.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS16.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 16.1</span></span><span id="SS16.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Direct Image Theorem)<span id="SS16.E1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS16.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS16.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS16.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="SS16.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a projective morphism between two complex manifolds, and
let <math id="SS16.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then one has:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The complex </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is strict, and </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(Y,w+i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(Y,w+i)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the morphism</span></p>
<table id="SS16.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS16.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{i}\colon\mathcal{H}^{-i}f_{\ast}M\to\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M(i)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{i}\colon\mathcal{H}^{-i}f_{\ast}M\to\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M(i)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is an isomorphism of Hodge modules.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any polarization on </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> induces a polarization on </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{i}\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{i}\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in
the Hodge-Lefschetz sense (= on primitive parts for </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS16.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS16.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 16.2</span></span><span id="SS16.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS16.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS16.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case where <math id="SS16.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathit{pt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝑝𝑡</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathit{pt}</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism from a smooth projective variety
to a point, the direct image theorem says that the <math id="SS16.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th cohomology group <math id="SS16.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X,K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,K)</annotation></semantics></math> of the perverse sheaf <math id="SS16.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> carries a polarized Hodge structure of
weight <math id="SS16.E2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w+i</annotation></semantics></math>, and that the hard Lefschetz theorem holds.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS16.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS16.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The most notable consequence of <a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a> is the decomposition
theorem for perverse sheaves underlying polarizable Hodge modules. This result, which
contains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BBD</span>, Théorème 6.2.5]</cite> as a special case, is one of the major
accomplishments of Saito’s theory.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            De Cataldo and Migliorini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">dCM</span>]</cite> later found a more elementary
Hodge-theoretic proof for the decomposition theorem in the special case <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\mathbb{Q}_{X}[\dim X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\mathbb{Q}_{X}[\dim X]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="SS16.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS16.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 16.3</span></span><span id="SS16.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS16.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS16.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS16.E3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="SS16.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a projective morphism between complex manifolds, and
<math id="SS16.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> a perverse sheaf that underlies a polarizable Hodge module. Then</span></p>
<table id="SS16.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS16.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}K\simeq\bigoplus_{i\in\mathbb{Z}}\bigl({}^{p}\!R^{i}f_{\ast}K\bigr)[-i]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>R</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}K\simeq\bigoplus_{i\in\mathbb{Z}}\bigl({}^{p}\!R^{i}f_{\ast}K\bigr)[-i]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS16.E3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS16.E3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in the derived category of perverse sheaves on <math id="SS16.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS16.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="SS16.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS16.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS16.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have a morphism <math id="SS16.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\colon\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}K\to\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}K(1)[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\colon\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}K\to\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}K(1)[2]</annotation></semantics></math> with the
property that</span></p>
<table id="SS16.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS16.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{i}\colon{}^{p}\!R^{-i}f_{\ast}K\to{}^{p}\!R^{i}f_{\ast}K(i)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>R</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>R</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{i}\colon{}^{p}\!R^{-i}f_{\ast}K\to{}^{p}\!R^{i}f_{\ast}K(i)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS16.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS16.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">is an isomorphism for every <math id="SS16.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. We can now apply a result by Deligne
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Deligne-Lef</span>]</cite> to obtain the desired splitting; note that it typically depends
on <math id="SS16.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS16.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS16.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The decomposition theorem also holds for the underlying filtered <math id="SS16.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules,
because of the strictness of the complex <math id="SS16.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS16.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS16.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 16.4</span></span><span id="SS16.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS16.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS16.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS16.E4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the same assumptions as above, let <math id="SS16.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> be a filtered
<math id="SS16.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module that underlies a polarizable Hodge module. Then</span></p>
<table id="SS16.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS16.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})\simeq\bigoplus_{i\in\mathbb{Z}}\bigl(\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})\bigr)[-i]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})\simeq\bigoplus_{i\in\mathbb{Z}}\bigl(\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})\bigr)[-i]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS16.E4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS16.E4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in the derived category of filtered <math id="SS16.E4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules on <math id="SS16.E4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS17" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">17. </span>Proof of the direct image theorem</h3>

<div id="SS17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both <a href="#SS15.E1" title="Theorem 15.1 (Structure Theorem). ‣ 15. Structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></span></a> and <a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a> are ultimately
consequences of results about polarized variations of Hodge structure. In the case of
the structure theorem, the main input are the results of Cattani, Kaplan, and Schmid
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Schmid</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">CKS</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">CKS-L2</span>]</cite> and Kashiware and Kawai <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">KK</span>]</cite> about degenerating polarized
variations of Hodge on the complement of a normal crossing divisor. In the case of
the direct image theorem, the main input are the results of Zucker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Zucker</span>]</cite>
about the cohomology of a polarized variation of Hodge structure
on a punctured Riemann surface. Note that the classical theory only deals with
objects that are mildly singular; Saito’s theory can thus be seen as a mechanism that
reduces problems about polarizable variations of Hodge structure with arbitrary
singularities to ones with mild singularities.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS17.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let me begin by explaining the proof of the direct image theorem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-HM</span>, §5.3]</cite>. Given the recursive nature of the definitions, it is clear
that <a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a> has to be proved by induction on <math id="SS17.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math>. The argument has three parts:</p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Establish the theorem for <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prove the theorem in the case <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f(Z)\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f(Z)\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prove the theorem in the case <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f(Z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f(Z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="SS17.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the third part, we shall allow ourselves to use the structure theorem; one can
avoid this by using some ad-hoc arguments, but it simplifies the presentation. This
may look like a circular argument, because the proof of the structure theorem relies
on the direct image theorem – but in fact it is not: the direct image theorem is only
used for resolutions of singularities, which fall into the case <math id="SS17.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f(Z)\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f(Z)\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Although I decided to keep the two proofs separate, one can actually make
everything work out by proving both theorems together by induction on <math id="SS17.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="SS17.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Part 1</h4>

<div id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Saito first proves <a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a> for the mapping from a compact
Riemann surface to a point; in fact, only the case <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is
needed. Let <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> be a polarized Hodge module with strict
support <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is either a point, in which case <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> comes from a
polarized Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>; or <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
comes from a polarized variation of Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-1</annotation></semantics></math> on the complement
of finitely many points <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dotsc,x_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dotsc,x_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. The first case is trivial; in the second
case, the direct image theorem follows from Zucker’s work. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Zucker</span>]</cite>, which
predates the theory of Hodge modules, Zucker proves that the <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-cohomology groups
of the variation of Hodge structure carry a polarized Hodge structure. Although he
does not explicitly mention <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, he also proves that the filtered
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-complex of the variation of Hodge structure is quasi-isomorphic to the filtered
de Rham complex of <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>. The key point is that the asymptotic
behavior of the Hodge norm near each <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is controlled by the <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration on
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and by the weight filtration on the nearby cycles; this is a consequence of
Schmid’s results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Schmid</span>]</cite>. Zucker’s construction therefore leads to the same
filtration and to the same pairing as in Saito’s theory, and hence it
implies the direct image theorem. The interested reader can find a detailed
account of the proof in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Sabbah</span>, Chapter 3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS17.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Part 2</h4>

<div id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The next step is to prove the direct image theorem in the case <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f(Z)\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f(Z)\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. By
induction, we can assume that we already know the result for all polarized Hodge
modules with strict support of dimension less than <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math>. The key point in the
case <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f(Z)\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f(Z)\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> is that applying a nearby or vanishing cycle functor on <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
will reduce the dimension of <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and can therefore be handled by induction.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To show that each <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge module of weight <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w+i</annotation></semantics></math>, we have to verify
the conditions in the definition; the same goes for showing that the polarization on
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> induces a polarization on the primitive part <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\ell}\mathcal{H}^{-i}f_{\ast}M=\ker\ell^{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\ell}\mathcal{H}^{-i}f_{\ast}M=\ker\ell^{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This involves applying the functors <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>
is a locally defined holomorphic function on <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Some care is needed because <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a priori not a filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure, but only lives in a larger abelian category (see
<a href="#SS27" title="27. Direct image functor ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=g\circ f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=g\circ f</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider first the case where <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(Z)\not\subseteq g^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(Z)\not\subseteq g^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, or equivalently, <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\not\subseteq h^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\not\subseteq h^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the abelian category
mentioned above, one has a spectral sequence</p>
<table id="SS17.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS17.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{p,q}=\mathcal{H}^{p+q}f_{\ast}\bigl(\gr_{-p}^{W}\psi_{h}M\bigr)\Longrightarrow\mathcal{H}^{p+q}f_{\ast}\psi_{h}M." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{p,q}=\mathcal{H}^{p+q}f_{\ast}\bigl(\gr_{-p}^{W}\psi_{h}M\bigr)\Longrightarrow\mathcal{H}^{p+q}f_{\ast}\psi_{h}M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Each <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{-p}^{W}\psi_{h}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{-p}^{W}\psi_{h}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a polarized Hodge module of weight <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-1-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-1-p</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{-1}(0)\cap Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{-1}(0)\cap Z</annotation></semantics></math>; by induction, <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore a polarized Hodge module
of weight <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-1+q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-1+q</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we only have a polarization on the primitive part of
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{-p}^{W}\psi_{h}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{-p}^{W}\psi_{h}M</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a>
only produces a polarization on the primitive part with respect to <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>; this
makes it necessary to study the simultaneous action of <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
In any case, it follows that the entire spectral sequence is taking place in the
category of Hodge modules, and so it degenerates at <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for weight reasons. Now
some general results about the compatibility of the direct image functor with nearby
and vanishing cycles imply that</p>
<table id="SS17.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS17.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{g}\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M\simeq\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}\psi_{h}M\quad\text{and}\quad\phi_{g,1}\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M\simeq\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}\phi_{h,1}M." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{g}\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M\simeq\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}\psi_{h}M\quad\text{and}\quad\phi_{g,1}\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M\simeq\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}\phi_{h,1}M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">This already shows that the objects on the left-hand side are Hodge modules on <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Another key point is that the filtration induced by the spectral sequence is the
monodromy filtration for the action of <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{g}\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{g}\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math>; this is proved
by showing that the Lefschetz property for <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ell,N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell,N)</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page of the
spectral sequence continues to hold on the <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-page.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">At this point, we can finally prove that the complex <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>
is strict, and hence that each <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a filtered regular holonomic
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure. By controlling <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{g}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{g}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{g,1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{g,1}M</annotation></semantics></math> in
the case when <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(Z)\not\subseteq g^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(Z)\not\subseteq g^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, we actually know the whole associated
graded of <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration. In sufficiently negative
degrees, the <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration is equivalent to the <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-adic filtration, and so we
obtain information about <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> in a small analytic
neighborhood of <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f(Z)\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f(Z)\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, such neighborhoods cover
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>; this shows that <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is strict on all of <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similar reasoning is used to prove the Lefschetz isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now we can start checking the conditions in the definition. Since <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent and regular along <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the arguments leading to
(<a href="#SS17.E1" title="In Part 2 ‣ 17. Proof of the direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17.1</span></a>) show that <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent
and regular along <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, too. This proves (<a href="#SS12.E1" title="In 12. Definition of pure Hodge modules ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.1</span></a>) in the
case <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(Z)\not\subseteq g^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(Z)\not\subseteq g^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>; the case <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(Z)\subseteq g^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(Z)\subseteq g^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> is
more or less trivial. The recursive condition in (<a href="#SS12.E4" title="In 12. Definition of pure Hodge modules ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.4</span></a>), as well as the
assertions about the polarization, then follow from the analysis of the spectral
sequence above.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The only remaining condition is (<a href="#SS12.E3" title="In 12. Definition of pure Hodge modules ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.3</span></a>), namely that <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a
decomposition by strict support. Somewhat surprisingly, the proof of this fact uses
the polarization in an essential way. The idea is that the criterion in
<a href="#SS11.E7" title="Theorem 11.7. ‣ 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.7</span></span></a> reduces the problem to an identity among Hodge modules,
which can be checked pointwise after decomposing by strict support; in the end, it
becomes a linear algebra problem about certain families of polarized Hodge
structures. (The same result is used again during the proof of the structure theorem.)</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS17.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Part 3</h4>

<div id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It remains to prove <a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a> in the case <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f(Z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f(Z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We may
clearly assume that <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective space and <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a point; now the idea is to
use a pencil of hyperplane sections to get into a situation where one can apply the
inductive hypothesis. Let <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon\tilde{X}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon\tilde{X}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be the blowup of <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> at a generic
linear subspace of codimension <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>; as shown in the diagram</p>
<table id="SS17.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS17.Ex2.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="96.3" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-48.15px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 137.14 96.3" width="137.14"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,96.3) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(68.57,0) translate(0,48.15)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -63.96 -36.24)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 87.08)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.61)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 3.97 0) translate(15.37,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -10.76 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.72em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.99" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.99)" width="7.69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS17.Ex2.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\tilde{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\tilde{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 38.69 0) translate(71.42,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -13.19 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="12.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS17.Ex2.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 79.78)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(19.34,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -14.73 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.13em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="15.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS17.Ex2.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 39.13 0) translate(70.98,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.75 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.84em;--ltx-fo-height:0.62em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.2" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="11.67"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS17.Ex2.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathit{pt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑝𝑡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathit{pt}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -44.61 24.04 L -44.61 -20.08"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -36.95 0.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.41em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="5.7"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS17.Ex2.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -32.99 24.04 L 32.31 -22.84"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.32 8.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.72em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.99" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.99)" width="7.69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS17.Ex2.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -28.96 32.39 L 28.07 32.39"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.68 40.05)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.47"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS17.Ex2.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 46.15 24.04 L 46.15 -22.84"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 53.81 -1.82)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS17.Ex2.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle f</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -24.99 -32.78 L 28.51 -32.78"></path></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">we also get a new morphism <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon\tilde{X}\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon\tilde{X}\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. To apply the inductive
hypothesis, we need to construct a polarizable Hodge module <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math>; this can
easily be done with the help of the structure theorem. Let <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Z}\subseteq\pi^{-1}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Z}\subseteq\pi^{-1}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> be the irreducible component birational to <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. From
<a href="#SS15.E1" title="Theorem 15.1 (Structure Theorem). ‣ 15. Structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></span></a>, we get an equivalence of categories</p>
<table id="SS17.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS17.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)\simeq\operatorname{HM}_{\tilde{Z}}^{p}(\tilde{X},w)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)\simeq\operatorname{HM}_{\tilde{Z}}^{p}(\tilde{X},w),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and so <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> determines a Hodge module <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}\in\operatorname{HM}_{\tilde{Z}}^{p}(\tilde{X},w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}\in\operatorname{HM}_{\tilde{Z}}^{p}(\tilde{X},w)</annotation></semantics></math> with a
polarization. We already know that the direct image theorem is true for <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}\pi_{\ast}\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}\pi_{\ast}\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polarizable Hodge module of weight <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>; it is
clear from the construction that the summand with strict support <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (with the original polarization).</p>
</div>
<div id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now we apply the direct image theorem to <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon\tilde{X}\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon\tilde{X}\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then
again to the Hodge modules <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}p_{\ast}\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}p_{\ast}\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; the result is that the complex
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}_{+}(\tilde{\mathcal{M}},F_{\bullet}\tilde{\mathcal{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}_{+}(\tilde{\mathcal{M}},F_{\bullet}\tilde{\mathcal{M}})</annotation></semantics></math> is strict, and that <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\tilde{X},\tilde{M})=\mathcal{H}^{i}\tilde{f}_{\ast}\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\tilde{X},\tilde{M})=\mathcal{H}^{i}\tilde{f}_{\ast}\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w+i</annotation></semantics></math>. Both properties are inherited by direct
summands, and so it follows that <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is also strict, and
that <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X,M)=\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,M)=\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math> is also a Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w+i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of the argument consists in proving the Lefschetz isomorphism and the
assertion about the polarization. Here Saito’s method is to use the weak Lefschetz
theorem as much as possible, rather than trying to compare Lefschetz operators and
polarizations for <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> directly; the point is that <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\simeq i_{\ast}\circ i^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\simeq i_{\ast}\circ i^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\colon Y\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\colon Y\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> is the inclusion of a general hyperplane. This takes
care of all cases except for proving that the polarization on <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> induces a
polarization of the Hodge structure <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\ell}H^{0}(X,M)=\ker\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\ell}H^{0}(X,M)=\ker\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. In this remaining
case, one has</p>
<table id="SS17.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS17.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\ell}H^{0}(X,M)\hookrightarrow H^{0}\bigl(\mathbb{P}^{1},P_{\tilde{\ell}}\mathcal{H}^{0}p_{\ast}\tilde{M}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\ell}H^{0}(X,M)\hookrightarrow H^{0}\bigl(\mathbb{P}^{1},P_{\tilde{\ell}}\mathcal{H}^{0}p_{\ast}\tilde{M}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with a corresponding identity for the polarizations; since we control the
polarization on <math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\tilde{\ell}}\mathcal{H}^{0}p_{\ast}\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\tilde{\ell}}\mathcal{H}^{0}p_{\ast}\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> by induction, an appeal to the theorem on
<math id="SS17.SSS0.Px3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> finishes the proof.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="SS18" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">18. </span>Proof of the structure theorem</h3>

<div id="SS18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now let me explain how Saito proves the difficult half <a href="#SS15.E1" title="Theorem 15.1 (Structure Theorem). ‣ 15. Structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></span></a>,
namely that a generically defined polarized variation of Hodge structure on <math id="SS18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of
weight <math id="SS18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math> extends uniquely to an object of <math id="SS18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since it is
easy to deduce from the definition that there can be at most one extension, we shall
concentrate on the problem of constructing it.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS18.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first consider a simplified local version of the problem to which we can apply the
theory of degenerating variations of Hodge structure. Let <math id="SS18.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\Delta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\Delta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a product
of disks, with coordinates <math id="SS18.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\dotsc,t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\dotsc,t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="SS18.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V},F^{\bullet}\mathcal{V},V,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V},F^{\bullet}\mathcal{V},V,Q)</annotation></semantics></math> be a polarized variation of Hodge structure on the complement of the divisor <math id="SS18.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}\dotsb t_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\dotsb t_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We shall do the following:</p>
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extend the variation of Hodge structure to a filtered regular holonomic
<math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and construct a candidate for a
polarization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Show that <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is of normal crossing type, and that many of its properties are
therefore determined by combinatorial data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Verify the conditions in the special case of a monomial function <math id="S3.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="SS18.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Afterwards, we can use resolution of singularities and the direct image
theorem to show that this special case is enough to solve the problem in general.</p>
</div>
<section id="SS18.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Part 1</h4>

<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the time being, we assume that we have a polarized variation of Hodge structure of
weight <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-n</annotation></semantics></math>, defined on the complement of the divisor <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}\dotsm t_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\dotsm t_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\Delta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\Delta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with quasi-unipotent local monodromy. We have to construct an
extension <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)</annotation></semantics></math> to a
filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure. Define <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> as the
intersection complex of the local system <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> as the corresponding
regular holonomic <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module; both have strict support <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
<math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is supposed to be quasi-unipotent and regular
along <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}\dotsm t_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\dotsm t_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we are forced to define the filtration <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#SS11.E5" title="In 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.5</span></a>). More concretely,</p>
<table id="SS18.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS18.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\Bigl(\omega_{X}\otimes\tilde{\mathcal{V}}^{(-1,0]}\Bigr)\cdot\mathscr{D}_{X}\subseteq\omega_{X}\otimes\tilde{\mathcal{V}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\Bigl(\omega_{X}\otimes\tilde{\mathcal{V}}^{(-1,0]}\Bigr)\cdot\mathscr{D}_{X}\subseteq\omega_{X}\otimes\tilde{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is the <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule generated by the lattice <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{V}}^{(-1,0]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{V}}^{(-1,0]}</annotation></semantics></math> in Deligne’s
meromorphic extension <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the flat bundle <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V},\nabla)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V},\nabla)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the filtration
is given by the formula</p>
<table id="SS18.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS18.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{p}\mathcal{M}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\Bigl(\omega_{X}\otimes F^{i-p-n}\tilde{\mathcal{V}}^{(-1,0]}\Bigr)\cdot F_{i}\mathscr{D}_{X}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}\mathcal{M}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\Bigl(\omega_{X}\otimes F^{i-p-n}\tilde{\mathcal{V}}^{(-1,0]}\Bigr)\cdot F_{i}\mathscr{D}_{X}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">From the polarization on <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, one can also construct a morphism <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(w)\to\mathbf{D}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(w)\to\mathbf{D}K</annotation></semantics></math> that
is a candidate for being a polarization on <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS18.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Part 2</h4>

<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To prove that <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a polarized Hodge module, we use induction on <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Here
it is useful to work inside a larger class of objects that is preserved by various
operations. From the nilpotent orbit theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Schmid</span>]</cite>, one can deduce that <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
has the following properties:</p>
<ol id="S3.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The perverse sheaf <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent, and the natural stratification
of <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is adapted to <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> filtrations <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},V_{\bullet}^{1}\mathcal{M},\dotsc,V_{\bullet}^{n}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mo>∙</mo><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},V_{\bullet}^{1}\mathcal{M},\dotsc,V_{\bullet}^{n}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are compatible, where <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\bullet}^{i}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\bullet}^{i}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration with
respect to the function <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="S3.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{i}=\partial/\partial t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{i}=\partial/\partial t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S3.I5.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dotsc,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dotsc,n</annotation></semantics></math>; then one has</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="SS18.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS18.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F_{p}V_{\alpha}^{i}\mathcal{M}\cdot t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F_{p}V_{\alpha}^{i}\mathcal{M}\cdot t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS18.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=F_{p}V_{\alpha-1}^{i}\mathcal{M}\quad\text{for $\alpha&lt;0$,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=F_{p}V_{\alpha-1}^{i}\mathcal{M}\quad\text{for $\alpha&lt;0$,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="SS18.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS18.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle F_{p}\gr_{\alpha}^{V^{i}}\mathcal{M}\cdot\partial_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>gr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F_{p}\gr_{\alpha}^{V^{i}}\mathcal{M}\cdot\partial_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS18.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=F_{p+1}\gr_{\alpha+1}^{V^{i}}\mathcal{M}\quad\text{for $\alpha&gt;-1$,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>gr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=F_{p+1}\gr_{\alpha+1}^{V^{i}}\mathcal{M}\quad\text{for $\alpha&gt;-1$,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I5.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is a special case of the conditions in <a href="#SS11.E4" title="Definition 11.4. ‣ 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Definition <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.4</span></span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In general, we say that an object with these properties is of <em id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normal crossing
type</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This definition is closely related to the combinatorial description of regular holonomic
<math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">GGM</span>]</cite>. If the first condition is satisfied,
then <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the following <em id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">combinatorial data</em>: the
collection of vector spaces</p>
<table id="SS18.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS18.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}=\gr_{\alpha_{1}}^{V^{1}}\dotsb\gr_{\alpha_{n}}^{V^{n}}\mathcal{M},\quad\text{for $\alpha\in(\mathbb{Q}\cap[-1,0])^{n}$,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\alpha}^{V}\mathcal{M}=\gr_{\alpha_{1}}^{V^{1}}\dotsb\gr_{\alpha_{n}}^{V^{n}}\mathcal{M},\quad\text{for $\alpha\in(\mathbb{Q}\cap[-1,0])^{n}$,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and the linear mappings induced by <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\dotsc,t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\dotsc,t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1},\dotsc,\partial_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1},\dotsc,\partial_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1},\dotsc,N_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1},\dotsc,N_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. As in <a href="#SS11.E4" title="Definition 11.4. ‣ 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Definition <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.4</span></span></a>, Saito
introduces the other two conditions in order to deal with the filtration <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> (which is not itself combinatorial). For objects of normal crossing type, the
combinatorial data controls the properties of morphisms: for example, if <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon M_{1}\to M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon M_{1}\to M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism between objects of normal crossing type, then <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\im\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\im\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> are again of normal crossing type, provided that the
induced morphisms on combinatorial data are strictly compatible with the Hodge
filtration.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Saito also shows that if <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is of normal crossing type, then the filtered
<math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is Cohen-Macaulay, and quasi-unipotent
and regular along any monomial <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=t_{1}^{m_{1}}\dotsm t_{n}^{m_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=t_{1}^{m_{1}}\dotsm t_{n}^{m_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>;
moreover, the vanishing cycles <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{g}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{g}M</annotation></semantics></math> are again of normal crossing type. Part of
the argument consists in finding formulas for the combinatorial data of <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{g}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{g}M</annotation></semantics></math>, and
for the nilpotent operator <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, in terms of the combinatorial data of <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> itself.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS18.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Part 3</h4>

<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we can verify all the conditions in the case of a monomial <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=t_{1}^{m_{1}}\dotsm t_{n}^{m_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=t_{1}^{m_{1}}\dotsm t_{n}^{m_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we got <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> from a polarized variation of Hodge
structure, the combinatorial data lives in the category of mixed Hodge
structures: for nearby cycles, this follows from the <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL(2)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit theorem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">CKS</span>]</cite>, and for vanishing cycles from the “vanishing cycle theorem” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">KK</span>]</cite>
respectively “descent lemma” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">CKS-L2</span>]</cite>. In particular, <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\dotsc,t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\dotsc,t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1},\dotsc,\partial_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1},\dotsc,\partial_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are morphisms of mixed Hodge structure, and
therefore strictly compatible with the Hodge filtration.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is of normal crossing type, <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent
and regular along <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{g}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{g}M</annotation></semantics></math> is again an object of normal crossing type.
From the combinatorial data for <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, one can write down that for <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{g}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{g}M</annotation></semantics></math>, and
show that it also lives in the category of mixed Hodge structures. Because <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is a
morphism of mixed Hodge structures, and therefore strictly compatible with the Hodge
filtration, it follows that the monodromy filtration <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(N)_{\bullet}\psi_{g}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(N)_{\bullet}\psi_{g}M</annotation></semantics></math>, as well
as the primitive decomposition for <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, are also of normal crossing type. In particular,
the primitive part <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{N}\gr_{\ell}^{W(N)}\psi_{g}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{N}\gr_{\ell}^{W(N)}\psi_{g}M</annotation></semantics></math> is again an object of normal crossing
type.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The next step is to prove that <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{N}\gr_{\ell}^{W(N)}\psi_{g}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{N}\gr_{\ell}^{W(N)}\psi_{g}M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a decomposition by
strict support. It is enough to check the condition in <a href="#SS11.E7" title="Theorem 11.7. ‣ 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.7</span></span></a>
for the functions <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\dotsc,t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\dotsc,t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>; because we are dealing with objects of normal
crossing type, the problem further reduces to a statement about polarized Hodge
structures (which has already been used during the proof of the direct image theorem).
Note that every summand in the decomposition by strict support is again of normal
crossing type.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now let <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be any summand in the decomposition; after permuting
the coordinates, its support will be of the form <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{m}\times\{0\}\subseteq\Delta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{m}\times\{0\}\subseteq\Delta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. To conclude the argument, we have to show that <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is again an
extension of a polarized variation of Hodge structure: by induction, this will
guarantee that <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge module of weight <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-1+\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-1+\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and that the induced
morphism <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}(w-1+\ell)\to\mathbf{D}K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}(w-1+\ell)\to\mathbf{D}K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polarization. Because <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an object
of normal crossing type, <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M}^{\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M}^{\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent and
regular along <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}\dotsm t_{m}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\dotsm t_{m}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> must be the extension of a variation of
Hodge structure <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V}^{\prime},F^{\bullet}\mathcal{V}^{\prime},V^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V}^{\prime},F^{\bullet}\mathcal{V}^{\prime},V^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>; the problem is to show that the
induced bilinear form <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polarization. Here Saito appeals to the
<math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL(2)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit theorem and its consequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">CKS</span>]</cite>: by looking at the
combinatorial data, one sees that <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{t_{1}}\dotsm\psi_{t_{m}}M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{t_{1}}\dotsm\psi_{t_{m}}M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nilpotent
orbit; this implies that <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> polarizes the variation of Hodge structure in a small
neighborhood of the origin in <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#SS15.E1" title="Theorem 15.1 (Structure Theorem). ‣ 15. Structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></span></a>.</h6>
<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose first that <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on the complement of a normal crossing divisor <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>
in a complex manifold <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. The same construction as above produces an extension <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
to a filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure and strict
support <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we have to check that all the conditions are satisfied for an arbitrary
locally defined holomorphic function <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Resolution of singularities, together with
some technical results from the proof of the direct image theorem, reduces this to
the case where <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a product of disks and <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is a monomial; since we have already
proved the result in that case, we conclude that <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a polarized Hodge module.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">To deal with the general case, we again use resolution of singularities and the
direct image theorem. Choose an embedded resolution of singularities <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\tilde{Z}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\tilde{Z}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> that is an isomorphism over the subset of <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> where the variation of Hodge
structure is defined, and that makes the complement into a normal crossing divisor.
We already know that we can extend the variation of Hodge structure to an object <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}\in\operatorname{HM}_{\tilde{Z}}^{p}(\tilde{Z},w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}\in\operatorname{HM}_{\tilde{Z}}^{p}(\tilde{Z},w)</annotation></semantics></math>; since we can choose <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to be projective, we can then apply
<a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a> to conclude that <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}f_{\ast}\tilde{M}\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}f_{\ast}\tilde{M}\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now
the direct summand with strict support <math id="SS18.SSS0.Px3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired extension.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">D. </span>Mixed Hodge modules</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cohomology groups of algebraic varieties that are not smooth and projective still
carry mixed Hodge structures. To include those cases into his theory, Saito was
led to consider mixed objects. In fact, the definition of pure Hodge modules already
hints at the existence of a more general theory: one of the conditions says that
<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\ell}^{W(N)}(\psi_{f}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\ell}^{W(N)}(\psi_{f}M)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge module of weight <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-1+\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-1+\ell</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>; this suggests that the nearby cycles <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}M</annotation></semantics></math>, together with the
monodromy filtration for <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> shifted by <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-1</annotation></semantics></math> steps, should actually be a “mixed”
Hodge module.</p>
</div>
<section id="SS19" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">19. </span>Weakly mixed Hodge modules</h3>

<div id="SS19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To define mixed Hodge modules, Saito first introduces an auxiliary category <math id="SS19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MHW</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of
<em id="SS19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weakly mixed Hodge modules</em>. Its objects are pairs <math id="SS19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="SS19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure, and
<math id="SS19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\bullet}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\bullet}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite increasing filtration with the property that</p>
<table id="SS19.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS19.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\ell}^{W}\!M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,\ell)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\ell}^{W}\!M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,\ell).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS19.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A weakly mixed Hodge module is called <em id="SS19.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded-polarizable</em> if the individual
Hodge modules <math id="SS19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\ell}^{W}\!M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\ell}^{W}\!M</annotation></semantics></math> are polarizable; we denote this category by
the symbol <math id="SS19.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHW}^{p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MHW</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHW}^{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS19.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS19.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Certain results from the pure case carry over to this setting without additional
effort. For example, suppose that <math id="SS19.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)\in\operatorname{MHW}^{p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MHW</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)\in\operatorname{MHW}^{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, and that we have
a projective morphism <math id="SS19.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the spectral sequence</p>
<table id="SS19.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS19.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{p,q}=\mathcal{H}^{p+q}f_{\ast}\bigl(\gr_{-p}^{W}M\bigr)\Longrightarrow\mathcal{H}^{p+q}f_{\ast}M" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{p,q}=\mathcal{H}^{p+q}f_{\ast}\bigl(\gr_{-p}^{W}M\bigr)\Longrightarrow\mathcal{H}^{p+q}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS19.p2.2" class="ltx_p">degenerates at <math id="SS19.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and each <math id="SS19.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math>, together with the filtration induced
by the spectral sequence, again belongs to <math id="SS19.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHW}^{p}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MHW</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHW}^{p}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is an easy
consequence of <a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a> and the fact that there are no
nontrivial morphisms between polarizable Hodge modules of different weights. One can also
show that every morphism in the category <math id="SS19.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MHW</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly compatible with the
filtrations <math id="SS19.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS19.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> (in the strong sense).</p>
</div>
<div id="SS19.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS19.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In order to have a satisfactory theory of mixed Hodge modules, however, we will need
to impose restrictions on how the individual pure Hodge modules can be put together.
In fact, the same problem already appears in the study of variations of mixed Hodge
structure. The work of Cattani, Kaplan, and Schmid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">CKS</span>]</cite> shows that every
polarizable variation of Hodge structure degenerates in a controlled way; but for
variations of mixed Hodge structure, this is no longer the case. To get a theory
similar to the pure case, one has to restrict to <em id="SS19.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">admissible</em> variations of
mixed Hodge structure; this notion was introduced by Steenbrink and Zucker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">SZ</span>]</cite>,
and further developed by Kashiwara <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Kashiwara-st</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS19.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS19.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 19.1</span></span><span id="SS19.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS19.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS19.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When we assemble a mixed Hodge structure from <math id="SS19.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS19.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the
result is determined by a non-zero complex number, because</p>
<table id="SS19.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS19.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Ext_{\operatorname{MHS}}^{1}\bigl(\mathbb{Z}(0),\mathbb{Z}(1)\bigr)\simeq\mathbb{C}^{\times}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ext</mi><mi>MHS</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Ext_{\operatorname{MHS}}^{1}\bigl(\mathbb{Z}(0),\mathbb{Z}(1)\bigr)\simeq\mathbb{C}^{\times}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS19.E1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A variation of mixed Hodge structure of this type, say on the punctured disk,
is therefore the same thing as a holomorphic function <math id="SS19.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\Delta^{\ast}\to\mathbb{C}^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\Delta^{\ast}\to\mathbb{C}^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>;
note that <math id="SS19.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> may have an essential singularity at the origin. In this simple
example, the admissibility condition amounts to allowing only meromorphic functions <math id="SS19.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS19.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS19.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that admissibility is always defined relative to a partial compactification;
two partial compactifications that are not bimeromorphically equivalent may lead to
different notions of admissibility. Only in the case of algebraic varieties,
where any two compactifications are automatically birationally equivalent, can we
speak of admissibility without specifying the compactification.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS20" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">20. </span>Definition of mixed Hodge modules</h3>

<div id="SS20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present a simplified definition of the category of mixed
Hodge modules that Saito has developed over the years.<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            I learned the details of this new definition from a lecture by Mochizuki.</span></span></span>
The point is that there is some redundancy in the original definition
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHM</span>, §2.d]</cite>, which can be eliminated by a systematic use of the stability
of <math id="SS20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> under taking subquotients.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS20.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS20.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We shall only define mixed Hodge modules on complex manifolds; more general analytic
spaces are again dealt with by local embeddings into complex manifolds. Let <math id="SS20.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math> be a weakly mixed Hodge module on a complex manifold <math id="SS20.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. We have to
decide when <math id="SS20.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> should be called a mixed Hodge module; the idea is to adapt the
recursive definition from the pure case, by also imposing a condition similar to
admissibility in terms of nearby and vanishing cycle functors.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS20.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS20.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose then that <math id="SS20.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-constant holomorphic function on <math id="SS20.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. By
induction on the length of the weight filtration, one can easily show that the
underlying filtered <math id="SS20.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS20.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent
and regular along <math id="SS20.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>; recall that this means the existence of a rational
<math id="SS20.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration <math id="SS20.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> that interacts well with the Hodge filtration
<math id="SS20.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. Together with the weight filtration, we now have three
filtrations on <math id="SS20.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so we require that</p>
<table id="SS20.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="SS20.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">the three filtrations <math id="SS20.E1.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="SS20.E1.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="SS20.E1.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> are compatible.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS20.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Roughly speaking, compatibility means that when we compute the associated graded, the
order of the three filtrations does not matter; this is automatic in the case of two
filtrations, but not usually true in the case of three or more.<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
              
              
              
            This problem has been studied at length in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHM</span>, §1]</cite>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="SS20.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS20.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming the condition above, the nearby and vanishing cycles of each <math id="SS20.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}M</annotation></semantics></math> are
defined, and so we can introduce the naive limit filtrations</p>
<table id="SS20.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS20.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}(\psi_{f}M)=\psi_{f}\bigl(W_{i+1}M\bigr)\quad\text{and}\quad L_{i}(\phi_{f,1}M)=\phi_{f,1}\bigl(W_{i}M\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}(\psi_{f}M)=\psi_{f}\bigl(W_{i+1}M\bigr)\quad\text{and}\quad L_{i}(\phi_{f,1}M)=\phi_{f,1}\bigl(W_{i}M\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS20.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Now recall that both <math id="SS20.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS20.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{f,1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{f,1}M</annotation></semantics></math> are equipped with a nilpotent
endomorphism <math id="SS20.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=(2\pi i)^{-1}\log T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=(2\pi i)^{-1}\log T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>; since <math id="SS20.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the weight
filtration, it also preserves the two limit filtrations. As in the admissibility
condition of Steenbrink and Zucker, we ask for the existence of the so-called
<em id="SS20.p4.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative monodromy filtration</em>:</p>
<table id="SS20.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="SS20.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">
<span id="SS20.E2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:290.2pt;">
<span id="SS20.E2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">the relative monodromy filtrations <math id="SS20.E2.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\bullet}(\psi_{f}M)=W_{\bullet}\bigl(N,L_{\bullet}(\psi_{f}M)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\bullet}(\psi_{f}M)=W_{\bullet}\bigl(N,L_{\bullet}(\psi_{f}M)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="SS20.E2.2.1.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="SS20.E2.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\bullet}(\phi_{f,1}M)=W_{\bullet}\bigl(N,L_{\bullet}(\phi_{f,1}M)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\bullet}(\phi_{f,1}M)=W_{\bullet}\bigl(N,L_{\bullet}(\phi_{f,1}M)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> for the action of <math id="SS20.E2.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> exist.</span>
</span></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS20.p4.3" class="ltx_p">This is the natural replacement for the monodromy filtration of <math id="SS20.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> in the pure case;
but while the existence of the monodromy filtration is automatic, the existence of
the relative monodromy filtration is a nontrivial requirement. We say that <math id="SS20.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="SS20.p4.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">admissible along <math id="SS20.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math></em> if both of the conditions in
(<a href="#SS20.E1" title="In 20. Definition of mixed Hodge modules ‣ D. Mixed Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20.1</span></a>) and (<a href="#SS20.E2" title="In 20. Definition of mixed Hodge modules ‣ D. Mixed Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20.2</span></a>) are satisfied; one should think of this
as saying that the restriction of <math id="SS20.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math> to the open subset <math id="SS20.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> is admissible relative to <math id="SS20.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS20.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS20.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 20.3</span></span><span id="SS20.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS20.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS20.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a weakly mixed Hodge module <math id="SS20.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math> on the disk <math id="SS20.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>,
whose restriction to <math id="SS20.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is a variation of mixed Hodge structure with unipotent
monodromy. Show that <math id="SS20.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math> is admissible along <math id="SS20.E3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
the variation is admissible in the sense of Steenbrink and Zucker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">SZ</span>, §3.13]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS20.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS20.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now we can define the category of mixed Hodge modules; as in the pure case, the
definition is recursive. A weakly mixed Hodge module <math id="SS20.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)\in\operatorname{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MHW</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)\in\operatorname{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>
is called a <em id="SS20.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixed Hodge module</em> if, for every locally defined
holomorphic function <math id="SS20.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon U\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon U\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, the pair <math id="SS20.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math> is
admissible along <math id="SS20.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
<table id="SS20.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="SS20.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="SS20.E4.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\psi_{f}M,W_{\bullet}(\psi_{f}M)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\psi_{f}M,W_{\bullet}(\psi_{f}M)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="SS20.E4.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\phi_{f,1}M,W_{\bullet}(\phi_{f,1}M)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\phi_{f,1}M,W_{\bullet}(\phi_{f,1}M)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> are mixed Hodge modules</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS20.p5.2" class="ltx_p">whenever <math id="SS20.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain any irreducible components of <math id="SS20.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\cap\Supp\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\cap\Supp\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. This definition makes sense because <math id="SS20.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS20.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{f,1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{f,1}M</annotation></semantics></math> are then
supported in a subset of strictly smaller dimension. Note that the shifts by <math id="SS20.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-1</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="SS20.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> from the pure case are now built into the definition of the relative
monodromy filtration.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS20.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS20.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 20.5</span></span><span id="SS20.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS20.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS20.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote by</p>
<table id="SS20.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS20.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}(X)\subseteq\operatorname{MHW}(X)\quad\text{and}\quad\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)=\operatorname{MHM}(X)\cap\operatorname{MHW}^{p}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>MHW</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MHW</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}(X)\subseteq\operatorname{MHW}(X)\quad\text{and}\quad\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)=\operatorname{MHM}(X)\cap\operatorname{MHW}^{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS20.E5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">the full subcategories of all (graded-polarizable) mixed Hodge modules.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS20.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS20.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A morphism between two Hodge modules is simply a morphism between the underlying
filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS20.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="SS20.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure that is compatible
with the weight filtrations. As in the case of <math id="SS20.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MHW</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, one can show that every
morphism is strictly compatible with the filtrations <math id="SS20.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="SS20.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> (in the strong sense).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS21" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">21. </span>Properties of mixed Hodge modules</h3>

<div id="SS21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS21.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we collect a few important properties of mixed Hodge modules. First,
<math id="SS21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> are abelian categories; here the main point is that every
subquotient of a mixed Hodge module in the category <math id="SS21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MHW</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is again a
mixed Hodge module. Almost by definition, (graded-polarizable) mixed Hodge modules
are stable under the application of nearby and vanishing cycle functors. Extending
the other standard functors from perverse sheaves to mixed Hodge modules is more
involved. Given a projective morphism <math id="SS21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>, one has a collection of
cohomological functors</p>
<table id="SS21.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS21.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(Y);" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(Y);</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS21.p1.2" class="ltx_p">see the discussion near the beginning of <a href="#SS19" title="19. Weakly mixed Hodge modules ‣ D. Mixed Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></span></a>. As explained in
<a href="#SS30" title="30. Inverse image functors ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></span></a>, one can also define the cohomological inverse image functors</p>
<table id="SS21.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS21.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f^{\ast}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(Y)\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{H}^{i}f^{!}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(Y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f^{\ast}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(Y)\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{H}^{i}f^{!}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS21.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for an arbitrary morphism <math id="SS21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. Lastly, one can define a duality
functor</p>
<table id="SS21.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS21.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)^{\mathit{opp}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝑜𝑝𝑝</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)^{\mathit{opp}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS21.p1.4" class="ltx_p">compatible with Verdier duality for the underlying perverse sheaves (see
<a href="#SS29" title="29. Duality functor ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="SS21.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS21.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There is also a version of <a href="#SS15.E1" title="Theorem 15.1 (Structure Theorem). ‣ 15. Structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></span></a>, relating mixed Hodge modules
and admissible variations of mixed Hodge structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHM</span>, Theorem 3.27]</cite>.
Suppose that <math id="SS21.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded-polarizable mixed Hodge module on a complex manifold <math id="SS21.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="SS21.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> be an irreducible component of the support of <math id="SS21.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. After restricting
to a suitable Zariski-open subset of <math id="SS21.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a graded-polarizable variation
of mixed Hodge structure; it is not hard to deduce from the definition that it must be
admissible relative to <math id="SS21.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. The converse is also true, but much harder to prove.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS21.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS21.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 21.1</span></span><span id="SS21.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS21.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS21.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS21.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="SS21.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex manifold, and <math id="SS21.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> an irreducible closed analytic
subvariety of <math id="SS21.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. A graded-polarizable variation of mixed Hodge structure on a
Zariski-open subset of <math id="SS21.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> can be extended to a mixed Hodge module on <math id="SS21.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> if and only
if it is admissible relative to <math id="SS21.E1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS21.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS21.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another important result is the description of mixed Hodge modules by a “gluing”
procedure, similar to the case of perverse sheaves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Verdier</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Beilinson</span>]</cite>. Suppose
that <math id="SS21.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex manifold, and <math id="SS21.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> a closed analytic subvariety; we
denote by <math id="SS21.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\colon X\setminus Z\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\colon X\setminus Z\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> the inclusion of the open complement. Saito shows that
a mixed Hodge module <math id="SS21.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> can be reconstructed from the following
information: its restriction to <math id="SS21.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus Z</annotation></semantics></math>; a mixed Hodge module on <math id="SS21.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>; and
some gluing data. Let me explain how this works in the special case where
<math id="SS21.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> is the zero locus of a holomorphic function <math id="SS21.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here one can show that <math id="SS21.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely determined by the quadruple</p>
<table id="SS21.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS21.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(j^{-1}M,\phi_{f,1}M,\can,\var\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>can</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>var</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(j^{-1}M,\phi_{f,1}M,\can,\var\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS21.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="SS21.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\can\colon\psi_{f,1}M\to\phi_{f,1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>can</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\can\colon\psi_{f,1}M\to\phi_{f,1}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS21.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\var\colon\phi_{f,1}M\to\psi_{f,1}M(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\var\colon\phi_{f,1}M\to\psi_{f,1}M(-1)</annotation></semantics></math> are morphisms of mixed Hodge modules, and that the unipotent nearby
cycles <math id="SS21.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{f,1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{f,1}M</annotation></semantics></math> only depend on <math id="SS21.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{-1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{-1}M</annotation></semantics></math>. This suggests considering all
quadruples of the form</p>
<table id="SS21.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS21.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(M^{\prime},M^{\prime\prime},u,v\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(M^{\prime},M^{\prime\prime},u,v\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS21.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS21.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}\in\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X\setminus Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}\in\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X\setminus Z)</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="SS21.p3.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">extendable</em> to <math id="SS21.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; where <math id="SS21.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime\prime}\in\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime\prime}\in\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>; and where</p>
<table id="SS21.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS21.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon\psi_{f,1}M^{\prime}\to M^{\prime\prime}\quad\text{and}\quad v\colon M^{\prime\prime}\to\psi_{f,1}M^{\prime}(-1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon\psi_{f,1}M^{\prime}\to M^{\prime\prime}\quad\text{and}\quad v\colon M^{\prime\prime}\to\psi_{f,1}M^{\prime}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS21.p3.4" class="ltx_p">are morphisms of mixed Hodge modules with the property that <math id="SS21.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\circ u=N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\circ u=N</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote the category of such quadruples (with the obvious
morphisms) by the symbol <math id="SS21.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathit{ex}}^{p}(X\setminus Z,Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>𝑒𝑥</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathit{ex}}^{p}(X\setminus Z,Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. One has the following result
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHM</span>, Proposition 2.28]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS21.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS21.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 21.2</span></span><span id="SS21.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS21.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS21.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS21.E2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The functor</span></p>
<table id="SS21.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS21.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathit{ex}}^{p}(X\setminus Z,Z),\quad M\mapsto\bigl(j^{-1}M,\phi_{f,1}M,\can,\var\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>𝑒𝑥</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>can</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>var</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathit{ex}}^{p}(X\setminus Z,Z),\quad M\mapsto\bigl(j^{-1}M,\phi_{f,1}M,\can,\var\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS21.E2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS21.E2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is an equivalence of categories.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS21.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS21.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is essentially the same as in the case of perverse sheaves
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Beilinson</span>]</cite>, and makes use of Beilinson’s maximal extension functor. There is a
similar theorem for more general <math id="SS21.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>, in terms of Verdier specialization.
This result can be used to define the two functors</p>
<table id="SS21.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS21.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{\ast}j^{-1}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\quad\text{and}\quad j_{!}j^{-1}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\ast}j^{-1}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\quad\text{and}\quad j_{!}j^{-1}\colon\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS21.p4.2" class="ltx_p">in a way that is compatible with the corresponding functors for perverse sheaves.
As the notation suggests, <math id="SS21.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\ast}j^{-1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\ast}j^{-1}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS21.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{!}j^{-1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>j</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{!}j^{-1}M</annotation></semantics></math> only depend on the
restriction of <math id="SS21.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="SS21.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and can thus be considered as functors
from extendable mixed Hodge modules on <math id="SS21.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus Z</annotation></semantics></math> to mixed Hodge modules on <math id="SS21.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS21.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS21.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 21.3</span></span><span id="SS21.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS21.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS21.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the special case considered above, <math id="SS21.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\ast}j^{-1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\ast}j^{-1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is the mixed Hodge
module corresponding to the quadruple <math id="SS21.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(j^{-1}M,\psi_{f,1}M(-1),N,\id\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>id</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(j^{-1}M,\psi_{f,1}M(-1),N,\id\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="SS21.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{!}j^{-1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>j</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{!}j^{-1}M</annotation></semantics></math> the one corresponding to <math id="SS21.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(j^{-1}M,\psi_{f,1}M,\id,N\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(j^{-1}M,\psi_{f,1}M,\id,N\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS21.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS21.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that an arbitrary graded-polarizable mixed Hodge module on <math id="SS21.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus Z</annotation></semantics></math> may
not be extendable to <math id="SS21.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>: for graded-polarizable variations of mixed Hodge structure,
for example, extendability is equivalent to admissibility (by
<a href="#SS21.E1" title="Theorem 21.1. ‣ 21. Properties of mixed Hodge modules ‣ D. Mixed Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21.1</span></span></a>). This is why one cannot define <math id="SS21.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS21.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{!}</annotation></semantics></math> as
functors from <math id="SS21.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X\setminus Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X\setminus Z)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="SS21.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS22" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">22. </span>Algebraic mixed Hodge modules</h3>

<div id="SS22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case where <math id="SS22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex algebraic variety, it is more natural to consider
only algebraic mixed Hodge modules on <math id="SS22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a <em id="SS22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compactification</em> of <math id="SS22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
meaning a proper algebraic variety <math id="SS22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{X}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="SS22.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as a dense Zariski-open
subset. Then according to Serre’s G.A.G.A. theorem, a coherent sheaf on the
associated analytic space <math id="SS22.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathit{an}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathit{an}}</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraic if and only if it is the restriction of
a coherent sheaf from <math id="SS22.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{X}^{\mathit{an}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{X}^{\mathit{an}}</annotation></semantics></math>; this condition is independent of the choice of
<math id="SS22.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. A similar result holds for coherent filtered <math id="SS22.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.
This suggests the following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS22.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS22.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 22.1</span></span><span id="SS22.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS22.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS22.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS22.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex algebraic variety. The category <math id="SS22.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of
<em id="SS22.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">algebraic mixed Hodge modules</em> is defined as the image of <math id="SS22.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(\bar{X}^{\mathit{an}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(\bar{X}^{\mathit{an}})</annotation></semantics></math> under
the natural restriction functor <math id="SS22.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(\bar{X}^{\mathit{an}})\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X^{\mathit{an}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(\bar{X}^{\mathit{an}})\to\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X^{\mathit{an}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS22.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS22.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One can show that the resulting category is independent of the choice of <math id="SS22.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{X}</annotation></semantics></math>; the
reason is that any two complete algebraic varieties containing <math id="SS22.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as a dense
Zariski-open subset are birationally equivalent. When <math id="SS22.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic mixed Hodge
module, the perverse sheaf <math id="SS22.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rat</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat M</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS22.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathit{an}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathit{an}}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructible with respect to an
algebraic stratification of <math id="SS22.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and the coherent sheaves <math id="SS22.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are
algebraic; this justifies the name. It is important to remember that algebraic mixed
Hodge modules are, by definition, polarizable and extendable.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS22.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS22.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 22.2</span></span><span id="SS22.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS22.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS22.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Use the structure theorem to prove that every polarizable variation of Hodge
structure on an algebraic variety <math id="SS22.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an object of <math id="SS22.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS22.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS22.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A drawback of the above definition is that it involves choosing a complete variety
containing <math id="SS22.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Fortunately, Saito <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-new</span>]</cite> has recently published an
elegant intrinsic description of the category <math id="SS22.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. We shall state it as a
theorem here; in an alternative treatment of the theory, it could perhaps become
the definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS22.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS22.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 22.3</span></span><span id="SS22.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS22.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS22.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS22.E3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A weakly mixed Hodge module <math id="SS22.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)\in\operatorname{MHW}(X^{\mathit{an}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MHW</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)\in\operatorname{MHW}(X^{\mathit{an}})</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the category
<math id="SS22.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="SS22.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> can be covered by Zariski-open subsets <math id="SS22.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> with the
following four properties:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathscr{O}_{X}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathscr{O}_{X}(U)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\setminus f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\setminus f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is smooth and dense
in </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The restriction of </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to the open subset </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\mathit{an}}\setminus f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\mathit{an}}\setminus f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
is a graded-polarizable admissible variation of mixed Hodge structure.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The pair </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is admissible along </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The pair </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\psi_{f}M,W_{\bullet}(\psi_{f}M)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\psi_{f}M,W_{\bullet}(\psi_{f}M)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> belongs to
</span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}\bigl(f^{-1}(0)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}\bigl(f^{-1}(0)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS22.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS22.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that if one takes the conditions in the theorem as the definition <math id="SS22.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>,
then every graded-polarizable admissible variation of mixed Hodge structure on <math id="SS22.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
automatically an algebraic mixed Hodge module (because <math id="SS22.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> is allowed to be
empty). In fact, the point behind <a href="#SS22.E3" title="Theorem 22.3. ‣ 22. Algebraic mixed Hodge modules ‣ D. Mixed Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22.3</span></span></a> is precisely that
every graded-polarizable admissible variation of mixed Hodge structure on <math id="SS22.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathit{an}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathit{an}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be
extended to an object of <math id="SS22.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(\bar{X}^{\mathit{an}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(\bar{X}^{\mathit{an}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS23" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">23. </span>Derived categories and weights</h3>

<div id="SS23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS23.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in the introduction, Saito’s theory is more satisfactory in the case of
algebraic varieties. The reason is that admissibility is independent of the
compactification (because any two compactifications are birationally
equivalent); and that one has direct image functors for arbitrary morphisms
(because every morphism can be factored into the composition of an open embedding and
a proper morphism).</p>
</div>
<div id="SS23.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS23.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="SS23.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> the bounded derived category of the abelian category
<math id="SS23.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>; its objects are bounded complexes of algebraic mixed Hodge modules on
<math id="SS23.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, we have an exact functor</p>
<table id="SS23.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS23.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rat\colon\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)\to\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}_{\mathit{c}}(\mathbb{Q}_{X^{\mathit{an}}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rat</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>𝑎𝑛</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rat\colon\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)\to\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}_{\mathit{c}}(\mathbb{Q}_{X^{\mathit{an}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS23.p2.2" class="ltx_p">to the bounded derived category of algebraically constructible complexes. The functor
associates to a mixed Hodge module the underlying perverse sheaf; it is
faithful, essentially because a morphism between two mixed Hodge modules is zero if
and only if the corresponding morphism on perverse sheaves is zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS23.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS23.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The advantage of working with algebraic varieties is that <math id="SS23.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
the same six-functor formalism as in the case of constructible complexes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BBD</span>]</cite>.
Setting up this formalism is, however, not a trivial task. The easiest case is that
of the duality functor (see <a href="#SS29" title="29. Duality functor ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></span></a>): because</p>
<table id="SS23.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS23.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}\colon\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)^{\mathit{opp}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝑜𝑝𝑝</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}\colon\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)^{\mathit{opp}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS23.p3.2" class="ltx_p">is exact, it extends without ado to the derived category. As explained in
<a href="#SS30" title="30. Inverse image functors ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></span></a>, we have a collection of cohomological inverse image functors</p>
<table id="SS23.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS23.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{j}f^{\ast}\colon\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(Y)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{H}^{j}f^{!}\colon\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(Y)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{j}f^{\ast}\colon\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(Y)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{H}^{j}f^{!}\colon\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(Y)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS23.p3.3" class="ltx_p">for an arbitrary morphism <math id="SS23.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> between algebraic varieties; the
functors <math id="SS23.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS23.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{!}</annotation></semantics></math> on the derived category are defined by working with
Čech complexes for suitable affine open coverings. One also has a collection of
cohomological direct image functors</p>
<table id="SS23.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS23.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{j}f_{\ast}\colon\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(Y)\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{H}^{j}f_{!}\colon\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{j}f_{\ast}\colon\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(Y)\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{H}^{j}f_{!}\colon\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS23.p3.4" class="ltx_p">In the case where <math id="SS23.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, this follows from <a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a> and
Chow’s lemma; in the general case, one factors <math id="SS23.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> into an open embedding <math id="SS23.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> followed by a
proper morphism, and uses the fact that the two functors <math id="SS23.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS23.p3.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{!}</annotation></semantics></math> are
well-defined for algebraic mixed Hodge modules. The functors <math id="SS23.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS23.p3.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{!}</annotation></semantics></math> are then again
constructed with the help of affine open coverings, following the method introduced
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Beilinson</span>]</cite>. After all the functors have been constructed, one then has to
prove that they satisfy all the standard compatibility and adjointness relations.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS23.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS23.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 23.1</span></span><span id="SS23.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS23.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS23.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once the whole theory is in place, one can use it to put mixed Hodge structures on
the cohomology groups of algebraic varieties. Let <math id="SS23.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathit{pt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝑝𝑡</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathit{pt}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
morphism to a point; then one has a complex of algebraic mixed Hodge modules</p>
<table id="SS23.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS23.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}=f^{\ast}\mathbb{Q}(0)\in\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}=f^{\ast}\mathbb{Q}(0)\in\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS23.E1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">When <math id="SS23.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and <math id="SS23.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional, <math id="SS23.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]</annotation></semantics></math> is concentrated in degree
<math id="SS23.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, and is isomorphic to the pure Hodge module we were denoting by <math id="SS23.E1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]</annotation></semantics></math> earlier. Now</p>
<table id="SS23.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS23.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H})=H^{i}f_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}\quad\text{and}\quad H_{c}^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H})=H^{i}f_{!}\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mtext>and</mtext><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H})=H^{i}f_{\ast}\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}\quad\text{and}\quad H_{c}^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H})=H^{i}f_{!}\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS23.E1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">are graded-polarizable mixed Hodge structures on the <math id="SS23.E1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces <math id="SS23.E1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS23.E1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>; with considerable effort, Saito managed to show that these
mixed Hodge structures are the same as the ones defined by Deligne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHC</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS23.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS23.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let me end this chapter by briefly discussing the weight formalism on the derived
category of algebraic mixed Hodge modules; it works in exactly the same way as in the
case of mixed complexes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BBD</span>, §5.1.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS23.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS23.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 23.2</span></span><span id="SS23.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS23.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS23.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a complex <math id="SS23.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixed of weight <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq w</annotation></semantics></math></em> if <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{i}^{W}\mathcal{H}^{j}(M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{i}^{W}\mathcal{H}^{j}(M)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j+w</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixed of weight <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq w</annotation></semantics></math></em> if <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{i}^{W}\mathcal{H}^{j}(M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{i}^{W}\mathcal{H}^{j}(M)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j+w</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pure of weight <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math></em> if <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{i}^{W}\mathcal{H}^{j}(M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{i}^{W}\mathcal{H}^{j}(M)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j+w</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS23.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS23.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Saito shows that when <math id="SS23.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is mixed of weight <math id="SS23.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq w</annotation></semantics></math>, both <math id="SS23.p5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{!}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{!}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS23.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math> are
again mixed of weight <math id="SS23.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq w</annotation></semantics></math>; when <math id="SS23.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is mixed of weight <math id="SS23.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq w</annotation></semantics></math>, both <math id="SS23.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="SS23.p5.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{!}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{!}M</annotation></semantics></math> are again mixed of weight <math id="SS23.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq w</annotation></semantics></math>. It is also easy to see that <math id="SS23.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is
mixed of weight <math id="SS23.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq w</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="SS23.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}M</annotation></semantics></math> is mixed of weight <math id="SS23.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq-w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq-w</annotation></semantics></math>. In
particular, pure complexes are stable under direct images by proper morphisms, and
under the duality functor. If <math id="SS23.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is mixed of weight <math id="SS23.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq w_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq w_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="SS23.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is mixed
of weight <math id="SS23.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq w_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq w_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="SS23.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Ext^{i}(M_{1},M_{2})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Ext</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Ext^{i}(M_{1},M_{2})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="SS23.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;w_{1}-w_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;w_{1}-w_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies
formally that every pure complex splits into the direct sum of its cohomology
modules. The resulting non-canonical isomorphism</p>
<table id="SS23.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS23.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\simeq\bigoplus_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{H}^{j}(M)[-j]\in\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MHM</mi><mi>alg</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\simeq\bigoplus_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{H}^{j}(M)[-j]\in\mathrm{D}^{\mathit{b}}\operatorname{MHM}_{\mathrm{alg}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS23.p5.2" class="ltx_p">gives another perspective on the decomposition theorem.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">E. </span>Two applications</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The two results in this chapter are among the first applications of Saito’s theory;
the interested reader can find more details in an article by Masahiko Saito
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">RIMS</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="SS24" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">24. </span>Saito’s vanishing theorem</h3>

<div id="SS24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHM</span>, §2.g]</cite>, Saito proved the following vanishing theorem
for mixed Hodge modules. It contains many familiar results in algebraic geometry as
special cases: for example, if <math id="SS24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="SS24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional smooth projective variety,
and if we take <math id="SS24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathbb{Q}_{X}^{H}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="SS24.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS24.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{-p}^{F}\DR(\omega_{X})\simeq\Omega_{X}^{p}[n-p]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{-p}^{F}\DR(\omega_{X})\simeq\Omega_{X}^{p}[n-p],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS24.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and Saito’s result specializes to the Kodaira-Nakano vanishing theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS24.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS24.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 24.1</span></span><span id="SS24.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Vanishing Theorem)<span id="SS24.E1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS24.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS24.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS24.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="SS24.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> underlies a mixed Hodge module <math id="SS24.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> on a
projective algebraic variety <math id="SS24.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then one has</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="SS24.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS24.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{i}\Bigl(X,\gr_{k}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})\otimes L\Bigr)=0\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{i}\Bigl(X,\gr_{k}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})\otimes L\Bigr)=0\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="SS24.Ex2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">if <math id="SS24.Ex2.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="SS24.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS24.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{i}\Bigl(X,\gr_{k}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})\otimes L^{-1}\Bigr)=0\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{i}\Bigl(X,\gr_{k}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})\otimes L^{-1}\Bigr)=0\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="SS24.Ex3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">if <math id="SS24.Ex3.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS24.E1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS24.E1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="SS24.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary ample line bundle on <math id="SS24.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS24.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS24.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from the discussion in <a href="#SS14" title="14. Kashiwara’s equivalence and singular spaces ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></span></a> that <math id="SS24.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> lives on some ambient projective space; nevertheless, each
<math id="SS24.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is a well-defined complex of coherent sheaves on <math id="SS24.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
independent of the choice of embedding (by <a href="#SS14.E5" title="Exercise 14.5. ‣ 14. Kashiwara’s equivalence and singular spaces ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Exercise <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.5</span></span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="SS24.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS24.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of <a href="#SS24.E1" title="Theorem 24.1 (Vanishing Theorem). ‣ 24. Saito’s vanishing theorem ‣ E. Two applications ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24.1</span></span></a> is similar to Ramanujam’s method for deducing
the Kodaira vanishing theorem from the weak Lefschetz theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Ramanujam</span>]</cite>.
Choose a sufficiently large integer <math id="SS24.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> that makes <math id="SS24.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> very ample, and let
<math id="SS24.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\colon X\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\colon X\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the corresponding embedding into projective space; we
can assume that <math id="SS24.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{MHM}(\mathbb{P}^{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{MHM}(\mathbb{P}^{N})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="SS24.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp M\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp M\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="SS24.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subseteq\mathbb{P}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subseteq\mathbb{P}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be a generic hyperplane; then <math id="SS24.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\cap X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\cap X</annotation></semantics></math> is the divisor of a section of <math id="SS24.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and therefore determines a branched covering <math id="SS24.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="SS24.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> to
which one can pull back <math id="SS24.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Then one can show that the <math id="SS24.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module underlying
<math id="SS24.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\ast}\pi^{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\ast}\pi^{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math> contains a summand isomorphic to <math id="SS24.p3.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}(\ast H)\otimes L^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(\ast H)\otimes L^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>; this
implies the vanishing of</p>
<table id="SS24.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS24.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i}\Bigl(X,\gr_{k}^{F}\DR\bigl(\mathcal{M}(\ast H)\bigr)\otimes L^{-1}\Bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mi>DR</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}\Bigl(X,\gr_{k}^{F}\DR\bigl(\mathcal{M}(\ast H)\bigr)\otimes L^{-1}\Bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS24.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="SS24.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, the formalism of mixed Hodge modules reduces this to Artin’s
vanishing theorem: on the affine variety <math id="SS24.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\setminus\pi^{-1}(H\cap X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\setminus\pi^{-1}(H\cap X)</annotation></semantics></math>, the
cohomology of a self-dual perverse sheaf vanishes in all degrees <math id="SS24.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. One can
now deduce the desired vanishing for <math id="SS24.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})\otimes L^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})\otimes L^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> from the
exact sequence</p>
<table id="SS24.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS24.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\to\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{M}(\ast H)\to i_{+}\Bigl(\mathcal{M}\big|_{H}\Bigr)\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>H</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{M}(\ast H)\to i_{+}\Bigl(\mathcal{M}\big|_{H}\Bigr)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS24.p3.3" class="ltx_p">by induction on the dimension. A recent article by Popa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Popa</span>]</cite> explains the
details of the proof, as well as some applications of Saito’s theorem to algebraic
geometry. An alternative proof of <a href="#SS24.E1" title="Theorem 24.1 (Vanishing Theorem). ‣ 24. Saito’s vanishing theorem ‣ E. Two applications ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24.1</span></span></a> can be found in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">saito-vanishing</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS25" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">25. </span>Kollár’s conjecture</h3>

<div id="SS25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS25.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">KollarI</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">KollarII</span>]</cite>, Kollár proved several striking results about the higher
direct images of dualizing sheaves: when <math id="SS25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a surjective morphism
from a smooth projective variety <math id="SS25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to an arbitrary projective variety <math id="SS25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
the sheaves <math id="SS25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}f_{\ast}\omega_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}f_{\ast}\omega_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> are torsion-free and satisfy a vanishing theorem, and the
complex <math id="SS25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}\omega_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}\omega_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> splits in the derived category <math id="SS25.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}(\mathscr{O}_{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}(\mathscr{O}_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>. He also
conjectured that the same results should hold in much greater generality
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">KollarII</span>, Section 5]</cite>. More precisely, he predicted that, for every polarizable
variation of Hodge structure <math id="SS25.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> on a Zariski-open subset of a projective variety
<math id="SS25.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, there should be a coherent sheaf <math id="SS25.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(X,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(X,V)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="SS25.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with the following
properties:<span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
              
              
              
            This is a simplified version; the actual conjecture is more precise.</span></span></span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on all of <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(X,V)=\omega_{X}\otimes F^{p}\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(X,V)=\omega_{X}\otimes F^{p}\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p+1}\mathcal{V}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p+1}\mathcal{V}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}\mathcal{V}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}\mathcal{V}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is an ample line bundle on <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}\bigl(X,S(X,V)\otimes L\bigr)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}\bigl(X,S(X,V)\otimes L\bigr)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a surjective morphism to another projective variety <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
then the sheaves <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}f_{\ast}S(X,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}f_{\ast}S(X,V)</annotation></semantics></math> are torsion-free.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the same situation, one has a decomposition</p>
<table id="SS25.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS25.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}S(X,V)\simeq\bigoplus_{i=0}^{r}\bigl(R^{i}f_{\ast}S(X,V)\bigr)[-i]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}S(X,V)\simeq\bigoplus_{i=0}^{r}\bigl(R^{i}f_{\ast}S(X,V)\bigr)[-i],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\dim X-\dim Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\dim X-\dim Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="SS25.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS25.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, Kollár’s conjecture follows quite easily from the theory of mixed Hodge
modules. Saito announced the proof with the laconic remark: “This implies
some conjecture by Kollár, combined with the results in §3.”
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-MHM</span>, p. 276]</cite>. Fortunately, he later published a more detailed proof <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-K</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS25.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS25.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The idea is to extend the polarizable variation of Hodge structure <math id="SS25.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> to a
polarizable Hodge module <math id="SS25.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)\in\operatorname{HM}_{X}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)\in\operatorname{HM}_{X}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math> with
strict support <math id="SS25.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, by appealing to <a href="#SS15.E1" title="Theorem 15.1 (Structure Theorem). ‣ 15. Structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></span></a>, and then to define</p>
<table id="SS25.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS25.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(X,V)=F_{p(M)}\mathcal{M}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(X,V)=F_{p(M)}\mathcal{M},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS25.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS25.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(M)=\min\bigl\{\thinspace p\in\mathbb{Z}\thinspace\thinspace\big|\thinspace\thinspace F_{p}\mathcal{M}\neq 0\thinspace\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.340em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(M)=\min\bigl\{\thinspace p\in\mathbb{Z}\thinspace\thinspace\big|\thinspace\thinspace F_{p}\mathcal{M}\neq 0\thinspace\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall from
<a href="#SS14.E5" title="Exercise 14.5. ‣ 14. Kashiwara’s equivalence and singular spaces ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Exercise <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.5</span></span></a> that <math id="SS25.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p(M)}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p(M)}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a well-defined coherent sheaf on <math id="SS25.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
even though <math id="SS25.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> only lives on some ambient projective space.
With this definition of <math id="SS25.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(X,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(X,V)</annotation></semantics></math>, Kollár’s predictions become consequences of
general results about polarizable Hodge modules: the formula in <a href="#S5.I1.i1" title="item (1) ‣ 25. Kollár’s conjecture ‣ E. Two applications ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(1)</span></a>
follows from <a href="#SS15.E1" title="Theorem 15.1 (Structure Theorem). ‣ 15. Structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></span></a>; the vanishing in <a href="#S5.I1.i2" title="item (2) ‣ 25. Kollár’s conjecture ‣ E. Two applications ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(2)</span></a> follows
from <a href="#SS24.E1" title="Theorem 24.1 (Vanishing Theorem). ‣ 24. Saito’s vanishing theorem ‣ E. Two applications ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24.1</span></span></a>; the splitting of the complex <math id="SS25.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}S(X,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}S(X,V)</annotation></semantics></math>
in <a href="#S5.I1.i4" title="item (4) ‣ 25. Kollár’s conjecture ‣ E. Two applications ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(4)</span></a> follows from <a href="#SS16.E4" title="Corollary 16.4. ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Corollary <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.4</span></span></a>; etc. Saito
also found a very pretty argument for showing that the higher direct
image sheaves <math id="SS25.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}f_{\ast}S(X,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}f_{\ast}S(X,V)</annotation></semantics></math> are torsion-free when <math id="SS25.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is
surjective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-K</span>, Proposition 2.6]</cite>. It is based on the following observation.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS25.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS25.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 25.1</span></span><span id="SS25.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS25.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS25.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS25.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="SS25.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a projective morphism between complex manifolds, and let
<math id="SS25.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a polarizable Hodge module with strict support <math id="SS25.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="SS25.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is any
summand of <math id="SS25.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="SS25.E1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(M^{\prime})&gt;p(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(M^{\prime})&gt;p(M)</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="SS25.E1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp M^{\prime}=f(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp M^{\prime}=f(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS25.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="SS25.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS25.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS25.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Here is a brief outline of the proof. Suppose that <math id="SS25.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp M^{\prime}\neq f(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp M^{\prime}\neq f(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>; then it
suffices to show that <math id="SS25.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p(M)}\mathcal{M}^{\prime}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p(M)}\mathcal{M}^{\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the compatibility of the nearby
cycle functor with direct images, one reduces the problem to showing that</span></p>
<table id="SS25.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS25.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p(M)}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{h}=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p(M)}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{h}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS25.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS25.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="SS25.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="SS25.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration with respect to <math id="SS25.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and
where <math id="SS25.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=g\circ f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=g\circ f</annotation></semantics></math> for a locally defined holomorphic function <math id="SS25.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> that vanishes
along <math id="SS25.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> but not along <math id="SS25.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. But because <math id="SS25.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p(M)-1}\mathcal{M}_{h}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p(M)-1}\mathcal{M}_{h}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, this
follows from the fact that</span></p>
<table id="SS25.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS25.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\colon F_{p-1}\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{h}\to F_{p}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{h}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\colon F_{p-1}\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{h}\to F_{p}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS25.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS25.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">is surjective for every <math id="SS25.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> (see the discussion after
<a href="#SS11.E4" title="Definition 11.4. ‣ 11. Compatibility with the filtration ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Definition <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.4</span></span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">F. </span>Various functors and strictness</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this final chapter, we are concerned with various functors on mixed Hodge modules, in
particular, the direct image, duality, and inverse image functors. They are
relatively easy to define for perverse sheaves and regular holonomic <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules,
but the presence of the filtration leads to complications. We shall also discuss some
of the remarkable properties of those filtered <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> that underlie mixed Hodge modules – they are due to the strong restrictions on
the filtration <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> that are built into the definition.</p>
</div>
<section id="SS26" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">26. </span>Derived category and strictness</h3>

<div id="SS26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS26.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we briefly discuss the definition of the derived category of
filtered <math id="SS26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, and the important notion of strictness.
Let <math id="SS26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex manifold, and let <math id="SS26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the category of filtered
<math id="SS26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules; its objects are pairs <math id="SS26.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="SS26.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is
a right <math id="SS26.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, and <math id="SS26.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a compatible filtration by
<math id="SS26.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-submodules. Note that <math id="SS26.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is not an abelian category. It is,
however, an exact category: a sequence of the form</p>
<table id="SS26.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS26.Ex1.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="32.31" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-12.68px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 413.99 32.31" width="413.99"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,32.31) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(207,0) translate(0,16.15)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -202.38 -3.47)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 23.08)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.01)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(14.99,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -10.38 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex1.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 29.98 0) translate(63.02,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -40.53 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.86em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="67.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex1.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M}^{\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M}^{\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 138.14 0) translate(59.13,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -36.65 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.3em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="59.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex1.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 238.53 0) translate(66.21,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -43.72 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.32em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="73.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex1.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 353.07 0) translate(34.78,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.3 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex1.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0,}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -172.13 -0.01 L -154.81 -0.01"></path></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -63.97 -0.01 L -46.65 -0.01"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -58.58 7.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.54"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex1.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle u</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 36.43 -0.01 L 53.74 -0.01"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 42.14 7.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="5.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex1.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle v</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 150.96 -0.01 L 168.28 -0.01"></path></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS26.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is considered to be short exact if and only if <math id="SS26.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\circ u=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\circ u=0</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="SS26.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS26.Ex2.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="34.66" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-11.91px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 370.68 34.66" width="370.68"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,34.66) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(185.34,0) translate(0,16.39)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -180.73 -4.47)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 23.55)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.25)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(14.99,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -10.38 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex2.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 29.98 0) translate(58.95,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -26.66 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.85em;--ltx-fo-height:0.84em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.33" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.64)" width="39.49"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex2.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\mathcal{M}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\mathcal{M}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 120.2 0) translate(57.01,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -24.72 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.57em;--ltx-fo-height:0.84em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.33" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.64)" width="35.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex2.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 206.55 0) translate(60.54,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -28.26 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.08em;--ltx-fo-height:0.84em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.33" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.64)" width="42.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex2.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 299.96 0) translate(44.59,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.3 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex2.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0,}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -150.47 -1.02 L -123.35 -1.02"></path></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -60.25 -1.02 L -33.13 -1.02"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -56.82 9.34)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.46em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="20.27"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex2.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle\gr u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>gr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle\gr u</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 26.09 -1.02 L 53.21 -1.02"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 29.85 9.34)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.42em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="19.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex2.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle\gr v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>gr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle\gr v</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 119.51 -1.02 L 146.63 -1.02"></path></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS26.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is a short exact sequence of graded <math id="SS26.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Acyclic
complexes are defined in a similar way; after localizing at the subcategory of all
acyclic complexes, one obtains the derived category <math id="SS26.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{DF}(\mathscr{D}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>DF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{DF}(\mathscr{D}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>. This category
turns out to have a natural t-structure, whose heart is an abelian category
containing <math id="SS26.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>; more details about this construction can be found in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Laumon</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS26.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS26.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, the category <math id="SS26.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{DF}(\mathscr{D}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>DF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{DF}(\mathscr{D}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> and the t-structure on it can be described in
more concrete terms, starting from the general principle that graded objects form an
abelian category, whereas filtered objects do not. Consider the
<em id="SS26.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Rees algebra</em> of <math id="SS26.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathscr{D}_{X},F_{\bullet}\mathscr{D}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathscr{D}_{X},F_{\bullet}\mathscr{D}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as</p>
<table id="SS26.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS26.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}=R_{F}\mathscr{D}_{X}=\bigoplus_{k=0}^{\infty}F_{k}\mathscr{D}_{X}\cdot z^{k};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}=R_{F}\mathscr{D}_{X}=\bigoplus_{k=0}^{\infty}F_{k}\mathscr{D}_{X}\cdot z^{k};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS26.p2.2" class="ltx_p">here <math id="SS26.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is an auxiliary variable. It is a graded <math id="SS26.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra with</p>
<table id="SS26.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS26.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}/(z-1)\mathscr{R}_{X}\simeq\mathscr{D}_{X}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathscr{R}_{X}/z\mathscr{R}_{X}\simeq\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}/(z-1)\mathscr{R}_{X}\simeq\mathscr{D}_{X}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathscr{R}_{X}/z\mathscr{R}_{X}\simeq\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS26.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and therefore interpolates between the non-commutative <math id="SS26.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="SS26.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and its
associated graded <math id="SS26.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}\simeq\Sym^{\bullet}\mathscr{T}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}\simeq\Sym^{\bullet}\mathscr{T}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the same method, we can associate with every filtered <math id="SS26.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS26.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> a graded <math id="SS26.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module</p>
<table id="SS26.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS26.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{F}\mathcal{M}=\bigoplus_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}F_{k}\mathcal{M}\cdot z^{k}\subseteq\mathcal{M}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{O}_{X}[z,z^{-1}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{F}\mathcal{M}=\bigoplus_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}F_{k}\mathcal{M}\cdot z^{k}\subseteq\mathcal{M}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{O}_{X}[z,z^{-1}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS26.p2.4" class="ltx_p">which is coherent over <math id="SS26.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> exactly when the filtration <math id="SS26.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is
good.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS26.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS26.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 26.1</span></span><span id="SS26.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS26.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS26.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prove that <math id="SS26.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{F}\mathcal{M}/(z-1)R_{F}\mathcal{M}\simeq\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{F}\mathcal{M}/(z-1)R_{F}\mathcal{M}\simeq\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS26.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{F}\mathcal{M}/zR_{F}\mathcal{M}\simeq\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{F}\mathcal{M}/zR_{F}\mathcal{M}\simeq\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS26.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS26.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This construction defines a functor <math id="SS26.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{F}\colon\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\MG(\mathscr{R}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>MF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>MG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{F}\colon\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\MG(\mathscr{R}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>
from the category of filtered right <math id="SS26.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules to the category of graded right
<math id="SS26.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS26.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS26.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 26.2</span></span><span id="SS26.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS26.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS26.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prove that <math id="SS26.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{F}\colon\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\MG(\mathscr{R}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>MF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>MG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{F}\colon\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\MG(\mathscr{R}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is faithful
and identifies <math id="SS26.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\MF(\mathscr{D}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> with the subcategory of all graded <math id="SS26.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules
without <math id="SS26.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-torsion.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS26.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS26.p4.1" class="ltx_p">One can show that <math id="SS26.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> induces an equivalence of categories</p>
<table id="SS26.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(26.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS26.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{F}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{X})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{F}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{X}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS26.p4.2" class="ltx_p">under which the t-structure on <math id="SS26.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the standard
t-structure on <math id="SS26.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>. From this equivalence, we get a collection of
cohomology functors</p>
<table id="SS26.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS26.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\MG_{\mathit{coh}}(\mathscr{R}_{X});" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MG</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\MG_{\mathit{coh}}(\mathscr{R}_{X});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS26.p4.3" class="ltx_p">the thing to keep in mind is that <math id="SS26.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of a complex of filtered <math id="SS26.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules
is generally <em id="SS26.p4.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> a filtered <math id="SS26.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, only a graded <math id="SS26.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-module. By a
similar procedure, one can define the bounded derived category of filtered regular
holonomic <math id="SS26.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="SS26.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure; in that case, the cohomology functors
<math id="SS26.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> go to the abelian category of graded regular holonomic
<math id="SS26.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="SS26.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS26.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS26.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 26.4</span></span><span id="SS26.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS26.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS26.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A graded <math id="SS26.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-module is called <em id="SS26.E4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strict</em> if it has no <math id="SS26.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-torsion.
More generally, a complex of filtered <math id="SS26.E4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules is called <em id="SS26.E4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strict</em> if,
for every <math id="SS26.E4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, the graded <math id="SS26.E4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-module obtained by applying <math id="SS26.E4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is
strict.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS26.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS26.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, a graded <math id="SS26.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-module comes from a filtered <math id="SS26.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module if and
only if it is strict. Strictness of a complex is equivalent to all differentials in
the complex being strictly compatible with the filtrations.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS26.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS26.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 26.5</span></span><span id="SS26.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS26.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS26.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Show that a complex of filtered <math id="SS26.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules</p>
<table id="SS26.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS26.Ex7.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="32.31" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-12.68px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 314.44 32.31" width="314.44"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,32.31) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(157.22,0) translate(0,16.15)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -152.61 -3.47)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 23.08)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.01)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(45.14,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -40.53 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.86em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="67.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex7.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M}^{\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M}^{\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 90.29 0) translate(59.13,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -36.65 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.3em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="59.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex7.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 190.68 0) translate(66.21,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -43.72 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.32em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="73.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex7.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -62.05 -0.01 L -44.73 -0.01"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -56.66 7.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.54"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex7.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle u</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 38.35 -0.01 L 55.67 -0.01"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 44.07 7.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="5.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS26.Ex7.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle v</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS26.E5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is strict at <math id="SS26.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="SS26.E5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly compatible
with the filtrations.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS26.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS26.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The definition of various derived functors (such as the direct image functor or the
duality functor) requires an abelian category, and therefore produces not filtered
<math id="SS26.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules but graded <math id="SS26.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. For Saito’s theory, it is important to
know that they are strict in the case of mixed Hodge modules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS27" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">27. </span>Direct image functor</h3>

<div id="SS27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS27.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first concern is the definition of the direct image functor in the case
where <math id="SS27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper morphism between two complex manifolds; by using
local charts, one can extend the definition to the case where <math id="SS27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are
analytic spaces. We would like to have an exact functor</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS27.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{Y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.p1.2" class="ltx_p">that is compatible with the direct image functor <math id="SS27.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> for constructible
complexes. Saito’s construction is based on his theory of filtered differential
complexes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-HM</span>, §2.2]</cite>; what we shall do here is briefly sketch another one
based on Koszul duality.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS27.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS27.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall first how the elementary construction of <math id="SS27.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> works in the case of a single
filtered right <math id="SS27.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS27.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the factorization</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS27.Ex2.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="67.36" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-47.21px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 214.12 67.36" width="214.12"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,67.36) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(107.06,0) translate(0,51.36)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -102.45 -4.15)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 19.84)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.07)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(17.81,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -13.19 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="12.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS27.Ex2.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 35.61 0) translate(67.56,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -27.2 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.93em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.08em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="40.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS27.Ex2.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X\times Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X\times Y}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 134.99 0) translate(52.83,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.47 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="11.11"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS27.Ex2.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -66.56 -0.69 L -31.37 -0.69"></path></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -74.71 -9.04 C -29.86 -46.67 30.58 -46.67 75.43 -9.04"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -2.88 -27.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS27.Ex2.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle f</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 32.81 -0.69 L 68 -0.69"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 44.86 8.85)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="11.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS27.Ex2.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.p2.2" class="ltx_p">through the graph of <math id="SS27.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, one only has to define the direct image for closed
embeddings and for smooth projections. In the case of a closed embedding <math id="SS27.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\colon X\hookrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\colon X\hookrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>, the direct image is again a filtered <math id="SS27.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, given by</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS27.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})=i_{\ast}\bigl(\mathcal{M}\otimes_{\mathscr{D}_{X}}\mathscr{D}_{X\to Y}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})=i_{\ast}\bigl(\mathcal{M}\otimes_{\mathscr{D}_{X}}\mathscr{D}_{X\to Y}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS27.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathscr{D}_{X\to Y},F_{\bullet}\mathscr{D}_{X\to Y})=\mathscr{O}_{X}\otimes_{i^{-1}\mathscr{O}_{Y}}i^{-1}(\mathscr{D}_{Y},F_{\bullet}\mathscr{D}_{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathscr{D}_{X\to Y},F_{\bullet}\mathscr{D}_{X\to Y})=\mathscr{O}_{X}\otimes_{i^{-1}\mathscr{O}_{Y}}i^{-1}(\mathscr{D}_{Y},F_{\bullet}\mathscr{D}_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>. Locally, the embedding is defined by
holomorphic functions <math id="SS27.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\dotsc,t_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\dotsc,t_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>; if we set <math id="SS27.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{i}=\partial/\partial t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{i}=\partial/\partial t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS27.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{+}\mathcal{M}\simeq\mathcal{M}[\partial_{1},\dotsc,\partial_{r}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{+}\mathcal{M}\simeq\mathcal{M}[\partial_{1},\dotsc,\partial_{r}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.p2.4" class="ltx_p">with filtration given by</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS27.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{k}\bigl(i_{+}\mathcal{M}\bigr)=\sum_{a\in\mathbb{N}^{r}}F_{k-(a_{1}+\dotsb+a_{r})}\mathcal{M}\otimes\partial_{1}^{a_{1}}\dotsb\partial_{r}^{a_{r}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}\bigl(i_{+}\mathcal{M}\bigr)=\sum_{a\in\mathbb{N}^{r}}F_{k-(a_{1}+\dotsb+a_{r})}\mathcal{M}\otimes\partial_{1}^{a_{1}}\dotsb\partial_{r}^{a_{r}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Strictness is clearly not an issue in this situation. In the case of a smooth
projection <math id="SS27.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}\colon X\times Y\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}\colon X\times Y\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>, the direct image is a complex of filtered
<math id="SS27.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, given by</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS27.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{R}p_{2\ast}\DR_{X\times Y/Y}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>DR</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}p_{2\ast}\DR_{X\times Y/Y}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.p2.6" class="ltx_p">If we use the canonical Godement resolution to define <math id="SS27.p2.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{R}p_{2\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}p_{2\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, the
filtration on the complex is given by the subcomplexes</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS27.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{R}p_{2\ast}F_{k}\DR_{X\times Y/Y}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M});" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>DR</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}p_{2\ast}F_{k}\DR_{X\times Y/Y}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.p2.7" class="ltx_p">there is no reason why the resulting filtered complex should be strict.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS27.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS27.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 27.1</span></span><span id="SS27.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS27.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS27.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a morphism <math id="SS27.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathit{pt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝑝𝑡</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathit{pt}</annotation></semantics></math> to a point, strictness of <math id="SS27.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the <math id="SS27.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-degeneration of the Hodge-de Rham
spectral sequence</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS27.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{p,q}=H^{p+q}\bigl(X,\gr_{-p}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)\Longrightarrow H^{p+q}\bigl(X,\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{p,q}=H^{p+q}\bigl(X,\gr_{-p}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr)\Longrightarrow H^{p+q}\bigl(X,\DR(\mathcal{M})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.E1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This degeneration is of course an important issue in classical Hodge theory, too.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS27.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS27.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A one-step construction of the direct image functor uses the equivalence of
categories in (<a href="#SS26.E3" title="In 26. Derived category and strictness ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26.3</span></a>) and Koszul duality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BGS</span>, Theorem 2.12.6]</cite>; it works
more or less in the same way as Saito’s theory of induced <math id="SS27.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-ind</span>]</cite>. This construction also explains why direct images are more
naturally defined using right <math id="SS27.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS27.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS27.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The key point is that the graded <math id="SS27.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="SS27.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="SS27.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Koszul
algebra</em>, meaning that a certain Koszul-type complex
constructed from it is exact. It therefore has a Koszul dual <math id="SS27.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{K}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{K}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, which
is a certain graded <math id="SS27.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra constructed from the holomorphic de Rham
complex <math id="SS27.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega_{X}^{\bullet},d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega_{X}^{\bullet},d)</annotation></semantics></math>; concretely, <math id="SS27.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{K}_{X}=\Omega_{X}^{\bullet}[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{K}_{X}=\Omega_{X}^{\bullet}[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, with relations <math id="SS27.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS27.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d,\alpha]+(-1)^{\deg\alpha}d\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d,\alpha]+(-1)^{\deg\alpha}d\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Now <em id="SS27.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Koszul duality</em> gives an equivalence of categories of right
modules</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS27.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{X}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{X})\to\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{K}_{X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{X}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{X})\to\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{K}_{X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.p4.2" class="ltx_p">What makes this useful is that one has a morphism <math id="SS27.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{K}_{Y}\to f_{\ast}\mathscr{K}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{K}_{Y}\to f_{\ast}\mathscr{K}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="SS27.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> of a complex of graded right <math id="SS27.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{K}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{K}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules is naturally a complex of graded
right <math id="SS27.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{K}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{K}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Since <math id="SS27.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{K}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{K}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> has finite rank as on <math id="SS27.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, and <math id="SS27.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>
is proper, it is also obvious that this operation preserves boundedness and
coherence. If we put everything together, we obtain an exact functor</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS27.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{X})\to\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{Y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{X})\to\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.p4.3" class="ltx_p">by taking the composition <math id="SS27.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{Y}^{-1}\circ\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}\circ K_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{Y}^{-1}\circ\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}\circ K_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS27.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS27.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 27.2</span></span><span id="SS27.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS27.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS27.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the case of a filtered <math id="SS27.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS27.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>.
By definition,</p>
<table id="SS27.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS27.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})=f_{+}(R_{F}\mathcal{M})\in\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{Y})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})=f_{+}(R_{F}\mathcal{M})\in\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{G}(\mathscr{R}_{Y}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS27.E2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and without additional assumptions on the filtration <math id="SS27.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the graded
<math id="SS27.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="SS27.E2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> may fail to be strict.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS28" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">28. </span>Strictness of direct images</h3>

<div id="SS28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS28.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As part of <a href="#SS16.E1" title="Theorem 16.1 (Direct Image Theorem). ‣ 16. Direct image theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16.1</span></span></a>, Saito proved that when <math id="SS28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> underlies a polarizable Hodge module on a complex manifold <math id="SS28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="SS28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a projective morphism to another complex manifold <math id="SS28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, then the complex
<math id="SS28.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is strict; the result is easily extended to mixed
Hodge modules and to proper morphisms between algebraic varieties. In particular,
every cohomology module <math id="SS28.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is again a filtered
<math id="SS28.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS28.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS28.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Saito’s theorem is a powerful generalization of the fact that, on a smooth projective
variety <math id="SS28.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hodge-de Rham spectral sequence degenerates at <math id="SS28.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. It has
numerous important consequences; here we only have room to mention one, namely
<em id="SS28.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Laumon’s formula</em> for the associated graded of the
filtered <math id="SS28.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module underlying the Hodge module <math id="SS28.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Laumon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Laumon-for</span>]</cite> described the “associated graded” of the complex
<math id="SS28.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})\in\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{+}(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})\in\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>, which really means the
derived tensor product with <math id="SS28.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}/z\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}/z\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is not at all the
same thing as the associated graded of the underlying <math id="SS28.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="SS28.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, except when the complex is strict. In the case where <math id="SS28.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a mixed
Hodge module and <math id="SS28.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a projective morphism, this leads to the
isomorphism</p>
<table id="SS28.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS28.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\bigl(\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\simeq R^{i}f_{\ast}\Bigl(\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}\overset{\mathbf{L}}{\otimes}_{\mathscr{A}_{X}}f^{\ast}\mathscr{A}_{Y}\Bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>⊗</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝐋</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\bigl(\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\simeq R^{i}f_{\ast}\Bigl(\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}\overset{\mathbf{L}}{\otimes}_{\mathscr{A}_{X}}f^{\ast}\mathscr{A}_{Y}\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS28.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS28.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}_{X}=\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}_{X}=\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. To say this in more geometric terms,
let <math id="SS28.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}(X,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}(X,M)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the coherent sheaf on the cotangent bundle <math id="SS28.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\ast}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\ast}X</annotation></semantics></math>
determined by <math id="SS28.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="SS28.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS28.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathscr{G}(Y,\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M)\simeq R^{i}p_{1\ast}\Bigl(\mathbf{L}(\mathit{df})^{\ast}\mathscr{G}(X,M)\Bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>𝐋</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑑𝑓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}(Y,\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{\ast}M)\simeq R^{i}p_{1\ast}\Bigl(\mathbf{L}(\mathit{df})^{\ast}\mathscr{G}(X,M)\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS28.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where the notation is as in the following diagram:</p>
<table id="SS28.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS28.Ex3.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="82.55" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-39.3px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 183.45 82.55" width="183.45"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,82.55) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(91.73,0) translate(0,39.3)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -87.11 -30.07)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 69.37)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.14)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(43.25,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -38.64 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.59em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.6" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="63.44"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS28.Ex3.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces T^{\ast}Y\times_{Y}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces T^{\ast}Y\times_{Y}X}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 86.5 0) translate(61.74,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -21.38 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.09em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="28.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS28.Ex3.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces T^{\ast}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces T^{\ast}X}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 64.76)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 17.98 0) translate(25.27,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -20.66 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="27.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS28.Ex3.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces T^{\ast}Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces T^{\ast}Y}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -43.86 13.58 L -43.86 -15.65"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -36.2 -2.18)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="11.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS28.Ex3.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -0.33 24 L 34.86 24"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.56 33.55)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.68em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="9.41"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS28.Ex3.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle\mathit{df}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝑑𝑓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle\mathit{df}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS28.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Laumon’s formula explains, in the case of mixed Hodge modules, how taking the
associated graded interacts with the direct image functor. Saito has proved a similar
statement for the associated graded of the de Rham complex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-HM</span>, §2.3.7]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS28.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS28.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 28.1</span></span><span id="SS28.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS28.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS28.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS28.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="SS28.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a projective morphism between complex manifolds. If
<math id="SS28.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> underlies a mixed Hodge module on <math id="SS28.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</span></p>
<table id="SS28.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS28.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}\gr_{p}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})\simeq\gr_{p}^{F}\DR(f_{+}\mathcal{M})\simeq\bigoplus_{i\in\mathbb{Z}}\gr_{p}^{F}\DR\bigl(\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}\mathcal{M}\bigr)[-i]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}f_{\ast}\gr_{p}^{F}\DR(\mathcal{M})\simeq\gr_{p}^{F}\DR(f_{+}\mathcal{M})\simeq\bigoplus_{i\in\mathbb{Z}}\gr_{p}^{F}\DR\bigl(\mathcal{H}^{i}f_{+}\mathcal{M}\bigr)[-i]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS28.E1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS28.E1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for every <math id="SS28.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS29" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">29. </span>Duality functor</h3>

<div id="SS29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS29.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also have to say a few words about the definition of the duality functor for mixed
Hodge modules. The goal is to have an exact functor</p>
<table id="SS29.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS29.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})^{\mathit{opp}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝑜𝑝𝑝</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}\colon\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})\to\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}\mathrm{F}(\mathscr{D}_{X})^{\mathit{opp}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS29.p1.2" class="ltx_p">that is compatible with the Verdier dual <math id="SS29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}K</annotation></semantics></math> for constructible complexes. It is
again easiest to define such a functor in terms of graded right <math id="SS29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. The
point is that the tensor product <math id="SS29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> has two commuting
structures of right <math id="SS29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module; when we apply</p>
<table id="SS29.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS29.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{R}_{X}}\Bigl(-,\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{R}_{X}\Bigr)[\dim X]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑜𝑚</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{R}_{X}}\Bigl(-,\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{R}_{X}\Bigr)[\dim X]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS29.p1.3" class="ltx_p">to a complex of graded right <math id="SS29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, we therefore obtain another complex
of graded right <math id="SS29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Note that if we tensor by <math id="SS29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}/(z-1)\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}/(z-1)\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>,
this operation specializes to the usual duality functor</p>
<table id="SS29.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS29.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{D}_{X}}\Bigl(-,\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{D}_{X}\Bigr)[\dim X]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑜𝑚</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{D}_{X}}\Bigl(-,\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{D}_{X}\Bigr)[\dim X]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS29.p1.4" class="ltx_p">for <math id="SS29.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, and is therefore nicely compatible with Verdier duality.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS29.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS29.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 29.1</span></span><span id="SS29.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS29.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS29.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of a single filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS29.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="SS29.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, the complex of graded <math id="SS29.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules</p>
<table id="SS29.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(29.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS29.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{R}_{X}}\Bigl(R_{F}\mathcal{M},\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{R}_{X}\Bigr)[\dim X]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑜𝑚</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{R}_{X}}\Bigl(R_{F}\mathcal{M},\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{R}_{X}\Bigr)[\dim X]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS29.E1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">can have cohomology in negative degrees and therefore fail to be strict. In general,
all one can say is that the cohomology in negative degrees must be <math id="SS29.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-torsion,
because the complex reduces to the holonomic dual of <math id="SS29.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> after tensoring by
<math id="SS29.E1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}_{X}/(z-1)\mathscr{R}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}_{X}/(z-1)\mathscr{R}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS29.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS29.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fortunately, the problem above does not arise for Hodge modules; this is the content
of the following theorem by Saito <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Saito-HM</span>, Lemme 5.1.13]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS29.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS29.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 29.3</span></span><span id="SS29.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS29.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS29.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS29.E3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="SS29.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> underlies a Hodge module <math id="SS29.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the
dual complex is strict and again underlies a Hodge module <math id="SS29.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,-w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>HM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}M\in\operatorname{HM}(X,-w)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS29.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS29.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is proved by induction on the dimension of <math id="SS29.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp M</annotation></semantics></math>; the key point is the
compatibility of the duality functor with nearby and vanishing cycles. The result
extends without much trouble to the case of mixed Hodge modules: given a mixed Hodge
module <math id="SS29.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet}M)\in\operatorname{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet}M)\in\operatorname{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, the pair</p>
<table id="SS29.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS29.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\mathbf{D}M,\mathbf{D}W_{-\bullet}M\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∙</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\mathbf{D}M,\mathbf{D}W_{-\bullet}M\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS29.p3.2" class="ltx_p">is again a mixed Hodge module.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS29.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS29.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, the strictness of the complex in (<a href="#SS29.E2" title="In Example 29.1. ‣ 29. Duality functor ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29.2</span></a>) is equivalent to
<math id="SS29.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> being <em id="SS29.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cohen-Macaulay</em> as an <math id="SS29.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, where
<math id="SS29.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}_{X}=\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}_{X}=\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>; therefore <a href="#SS29.E3" title="Theorem 29.3. ‣ 29. Duality functor ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29.3</span></span></a> is saying
that <math id="SS29.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Cohen-Macaulay module whenever <math id="SS29.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> underlies a mixed Hodge module. This has the following useful consequence:
if <math id="SS29.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M}^{\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M}^{\prime},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the filtered <math id="SS29.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module underlying <math id="SS29.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}=\mathbf{D}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}=\mathbf{D}M</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="SS29.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS29.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}^{\prime}\simeq\mathbf{R}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{A}_{X}}\Bigl(\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M},\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{A}_{X}\Bigr)[n]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑜𝑚</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M}^{\prime}\simeq\mathbf{R}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{A}_{X}}\Bigl(\gr_{\bullet}^{F}\!\mathcal{M},\omega_{X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}\mathscr{A}_{X}\Bigr)[n],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS29.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS29.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>. It also implies that the coherent sheaf <math id="SS29.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}(X,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}(X,M)</annotation></semantics></math> on the
cotangent bundle <math id="SS29.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\ast}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\ast}X</annotation></semantics></math> is Cohen-Macaulay. This fact can be used to get
information about the coherent sheaves <math id="SS29.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}(\gr_{p}^{F}\!\mathcal{M},\mathscr{O}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑜𝑚</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}(\gr_{p}^{F}\!\mathcal{M},\mathscr{O}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> from
the geometry of the characteristic variety <math id="SS29.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Ch(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ch</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Ch(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="SS29.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mhmduality</span>]</cite>. For example, suppose that the fiber of the projection <math id="SS29.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Ch(\mathcal{M})\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Ch</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Ch(\mathcal{M})\to X</annotation></semantics></math> over a point <math id="SS29.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="SS29.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>; then</p>
<table id="SS29.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS29.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}(\gr_{p}^{F}\!\mathcal{M},\mathscr{O}_{X})=0\quad\text{for every $i\geq d+1$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑜𝑚</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for every </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}\mathcal{H}\hskip-1.0pt\mathit{om}_{\mathscr{O}_{X}}(\gr_{p}^{F}\!\mathcal{M},\mathscr{O}_{X})=0\quad\text{for every $i\geq d+1$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="SS30" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">30. </span>Inverse image functors</h3>

<div id="SS30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The inverse image functors for mixed Hodge modules lift the two operations <math id="SS30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS30.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{!}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{!}K</annotation></semantics></math> for perverse sheaves. We shall only discuss the case
of a morphism <math id="SS30.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> between two complex manifolds; some additional work
is needed to deal with morphisms between analytic spaces. Using the factorization</p>
<table id="SS30.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS30.Ex1.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="67.36" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-47.21px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 214.12 67.36" width="214.12"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,67.36) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(107.06,0) translate(0,51.36)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -102.45 -4.15)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 19.84)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.07)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(17.08,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.47 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="11.11"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex1.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 34.17 0) translate(67.56,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -27.2 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.93em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.08em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="40.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex1.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y\times X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y\times X}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 133.55 0) translate(53.55,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -13.19 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="12.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex1.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -68 -0.69 L -32.81 -0.69"></path></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -75.43 -9.04 C -30.58 -46.67 29.86 -46.67 74.71 -9.04"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.6 -27.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex1.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle f</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 31.37 -0.69 L 66.56 -0.69"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 43.41 8.85)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="11.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex1.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p1.2" class="ltx_p">through the graph of <math id="SS30.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, it is enough to define inverse images for closed embeddings
and for smooth projections.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS30.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us first consider the case of a smooth morphism <math id="SS30.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. For
variations of Hodge structure, the inverse image is obtained by pulling back the
vector bundle and the connection; to get a sensible definition for Hodge
modules, we therefore have to tensor by the relative canonical bundle <math id="SS30.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{Y/X}=\omega_{Y}\otimes f^{\ast}\omega_{X}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{Y/X}=\omega_{Y}\otimes f^{\ast}\omega_{X}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. There also has to be a shift by the relative dimension <math id="SS30.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\dim Y-\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\dim Y-\dim X</annotation></semantics></math> of the morphism, because of Saito’s convention that a variation of
Hodge structure of weight <math id="SS30.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> defines a Hodge module of weight <math id="SS30.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w+\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w+\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS30.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With this in mind, let <math id="SS30.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M},K)</annotation></semantics></math> be
a filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS30.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="SS30.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure on <math id="SS30.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. We first
define an auxiliary object</p>
<table id="SS30.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS30.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}=\bigl(\tilde{\mathcal{M}},F_{\bullet}\tilde{\mathcal{M}},\tilde{K}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}=\bigl(\tilde{\mathcal{M}},F_{\bullet}\tilde{\mathcal{M}},\tilde{K}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="SS30.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}=f^{-1}K(-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}=f^{-1}K(-r)</annotation></semantics></math> is again a perverse sheaf on <math id="SS30.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and where</p>
<table id="SS30.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS30.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{M}}=\omega_{Y/X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{Y}}f^{\ast}\mathcal{M}\quad\text{and}\quad F_{p}\tilde{\mathcal{M}}=\omega_{Y/X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{Y}}f^{\ast}F_{p+r}\mathcal{M}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{M}}=\omega_{Y/X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{Y}}f^{\ast}\mathcal{M}\quad\text{and}\quad F_{p}\tilde{\mathcal{M}}=\omega_{Y/X}\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{Y}}f^{\ast}F_{p+r}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p3.3" class="ltx_p">is again a filtered <math id="SS30.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-module on <math id="SS30.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, through the natural morphism
<math id="SS30.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{Y}\to f^{\ast}\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{Y}\to f^{\ast}\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. It requires a small calculation to show that
<math id="SS30.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\DR(\tilde{\mathcal{M}})\simeq\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>DR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\DR(\tilde{\mathcal{M}})\simeq\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math>; this is where the factor <math id="SS30.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{Y/X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{Y/X}</annotation></semantics></math> comes
in. Now the crucial result is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS30.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS30.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 30.1</span></span><span id="SS30.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS30.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="SS30.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="SS30.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="SS30.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(Y,w+r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>HM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}\in\operatorname{HM}^{p}(Y,w+r)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS30.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, one cannot prove directly that <math id="SS30.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the conditions for
being a Hodge module; instead, the proof has to go through the equivalence of categories
in <a href="#SS15.E1" title="Theorem 15.1 (Structure Theorem). ‣ 15. Structure theorem ‣ C. Two important theorems ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></span></a>. Since <math id="SS30.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a decomposition by strict support, we
may assume that <math id="SS30.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{HM}_{Z}^{p}(X,w)</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore comes from a generically
defined polarizable variation of Hodge structure on <math id="SS30.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="SS30.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math>; its
pullback is a generically defined polarizable variation of Hodge structure on
<math id="SS30.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> of the same weight, and therefore extends uniquely to an object of</p>
<table id="SS30.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS30.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{HM}_{f^{-1}(Z)}^{p}(Y,w+r)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>HM</mi><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{HM}_{f^{-1}(Z)}^{p}(Y,w+r).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p4.2" class="ltx_p">One then checks that this extension is isomorphic to <math id="SS30.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS30.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With this surprisingly deep result in hand, it is straightforward to construct
the two inverse image functors in general. Let <math id="SS30.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,W_{\bullet})\in\operatorname{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MHW</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,W_{\bullet})\in\operatorname{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be a
weakly mixed Hodge module on <math id="SS30.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. For a smooth morphism <math id="SS30.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</p>
<table id="S6.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="SS30.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS30.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}^{-r}f^{\ast}(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}^{-r}f^{\ast}(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS30.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Bigl(\tilde{M},W_{\bullet+r}\tilde{M}\Bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Bigl(\tilde{M},W_{\bullet+r}\tilde{M}\Bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="SS30.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS30.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}^{r}f^{!}(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}^{r}f^{!}(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS30.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Bigl(\tilde{M}(r),W_{\bullet-r}\tilde{M}(r)\Bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Bigl(\tilde{M}(r),W_{\bullet-r}\tilde{M}(r)\Bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p5.2" class="ltx_p">as objects of <math id="SS30.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHW}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MHW</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHW}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>; the need for shifting the weight filtration is explained
by <a href="#SS30.E1" title="Theorem 30.1. ‣ 30. Inverse image functors ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30.1</span></span></a>. One can show that both functors take
(graded-polarizable) mixed Hodge modules to (graded-polarizable) mixed Hodge modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS30.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we turn our attention to the case of a closed embedding <math id="SS30.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\colon Y\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\colon Y\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here the idea is to construct cohomological functors</p>
<table id="SS30.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(30.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS30.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{j}i^{\ast}\colon\operatorname{MHM}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}(Y)\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{H}^{j}i^{!}\colon\operatorname{MHM}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}(Y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>MHM</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>MHM</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>MHM</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>MHM</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{j}i^{\ast}\colon\operatorname{MHM}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}(Y)\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{H}^{j}i^{!}\colon\operatorname{MHM}(X)\to\operatorname{MHM}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p6.2" class="ltx_p">in terms of nearby and vanishing cycles. This procedure has the advantage of
directly producing mixed Hodge modules; note that it leads in general to a
different filtration than the one used by Laumon for arbitrary filtered <math id="SS30.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Laumon</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS30.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To make things simpler, let me explain the construction when <math id="SS30.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Y=\dim X-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Y=\dim X-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Locally, the submanifold <math id="SS30.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is then defined by a single holomorphic function
<math id="SS30.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, so let us first treat the case where <math id="SS30.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=t^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=t^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a mixed Hodge module <math id="SS30.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\operatorname{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\operatorname{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="SS30.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\rat M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rat</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\rat M</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction of the vanishing cycle functor (see
<a href="#SS8" title="8. Review of nearby and vanishing cycles ‣ B. Pure Hodge modules ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></span></a>), we have a distinguished triangle</p>
<table id="SS30.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS30.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{-1}K\to\psi_{t,1}K\to\phi_{t,1}K\to i^{-1}K[1]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{-1}K\to\psi_{t,1}K\to\phi_{t,1}K\to i^{-1}K[1]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p7.2" class="ltx_p">in the derived category of constructible complexes; it is therefore reasonable to
define <math id="SS30.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{j}i^{\ast}M\in\operatorname{MHM}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MHM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{j}i^{\ast}M\in\operatorname{MHM}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="SS30.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{-1,0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{-1,0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, as the cohomology modules
of the complex of mixed Hodge modules</p>
<table id="SS30.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS30.Ex8.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="40.59" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-14.76px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 187.07 40.59" width="187.07"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,40.59) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(93.53,0) translate(0,20.29)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -88.92 -5.53)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 31.37)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 21.22)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(33.34,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -28.73 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.15em;--ltx-fo-height:1.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.4em;font-size:10pt;" height="22.14" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 16.6)" width="43.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex8.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bigl[\psi_{t,1}M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bigl[\psi_{t,1}M}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 66.68 0) translate(68.99,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -37.57 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.43em;--ltx-fo-height:1.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.4em;font-size:10pt;" height="22.14" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 16.6)" width="61.3"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex8.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\phi_{t,1}M\Bigr][1].}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>M</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\phi_{t,1}M\Bigr][1].}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -21.97 -2.08 L 4.29 -2.08"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -19.64 5.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.96" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="21.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex8.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle\can" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>can</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle\can</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p7.3" class="ltx_p">To describe this operation on the level of filtered <math id="SS30.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, let <math id="SS30.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="SS30.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration relative to the divisor <math id="SS30.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>; then the
corresponding complex of filtered <math id="SS30.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules is</p>
<table id="SS30.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(30.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS30.E3.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="40.59" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-14.76px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 338.82 40.59" width="338.82"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,40.59) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(169.41,0) translate(0,20.29)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -164.8 -5.53)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 31.37)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 21.22)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(73.56,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -68.95 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.97em;--ltx-fo-height:1.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.4em;font-size:10pt;" height="22.14" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 16.6)" width="124.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.E3.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bigl[\bigl(\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet-1}\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\bigr)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bigl[\bigl(\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet-1}\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\bigr)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 147.12 0) translate(104.64,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -73.22 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.58em;--ltx-fo-height:1.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.4em;font-size:10pt;" height="22.14" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 16.6)" width="132.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.E3.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigl(\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\Bigr][1],}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigl(\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\Bigr][1],}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -17.4 -2.08 L 8.85 -2.08"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -10.68 6.97)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.93em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="12.81"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.E3.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">∂</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p7.4" class="ltx_p">where the filtration is the one induced by <math id="SS30.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS30.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To deal with the general case, we observe that the <math id="SS30.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\!</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration is independent of
the choice of local equation for <math id="SS30.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and that both <math id="SS30.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="SS30.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are well-defined sheaves of <math id="SS30.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. The same is true for the action of
<math id="SS30.p8.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>; the only thing that actually depends on <math id="SS30.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="SS30.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-module
structure. In fact, both sheaves carry an action by <math id="SS30.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{0}^{V}\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{0}^{V}\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, but this <math id="SS30.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra is only locally isomorphic to <math id="SS30.p8.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{Y}[t\partial_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{Y}[t\partial_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, one can show that the quotient of <math id="SS30.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{0}^{V}\mathscr{D}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{0}^{V}\mathscr{D}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="SS30.p8.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically isomorphic to <math id="SS30.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>; this gives both</p>
<table id="SS30.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS30.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\coker\Bigl(\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\Bigr)\quad\text{and}\quad\ker\Bigl(\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\Bigr)\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{Y}}\mathscr{N}_{Y\mid X}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.278em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><mi>ker</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.278em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\coker\Bigl(\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\Bigr)\quad\text{and}\quad\ker\Bigl(\partial_{t}\colon\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\to\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\Bigr)\otimes_{\mathscr{O}_{Y}}\mathscr{N}_{Y\mid X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p8.2" class="ltx_p">a canonical <math id="SS30.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure that does not depend on the choice of <math id="SS30.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus we obtain two filtered regular holonomic <math id="SS30.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="SS30.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure
and weight filtration; because the conditions in the definition are local, both are
mixed Hodge modules on <math id="SS30.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS30.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The same procedure can be used to define <math id="SS30.p9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{j}i^{!}M\in\operatorname{MHM}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>MHM</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{j}i^{!}M\in\operatorname{MHM}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="SS30.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="SS30.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=t^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=t^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, one has a similar distinguished triangle for <math id="SS30.p9.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i^{!}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{!}K</annotation></semantics></math>,
which suggests to look at the complex of mixed Hodge modules</p>
<table id="SS30.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS30.Ex10.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="40.59" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-14.76px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 197.83 40.59" width="197.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,40.59) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(98.92,0) translate(0,20.29)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -94.3 -5.53)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 31.37)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 21.22)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(32.95,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -28.34 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.1em;--ltx-fo-height:1.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.4em;font-size:10pt;" height="22.14" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 16.6)" width="42.85"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex10.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bigl[\phi_{t,1}M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bigl[\phi_{t,1}M}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 65.91 0) translate(74.75,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -43.33 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.26em;--ltx-fo-height:1.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.4em;font-size:10pt;" height="22.14" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 16.6)" width="72.83"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex10.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\psi_{t,1}M(-1)\Bigr].}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\psi_{t,1}M(-1)\Bigr].}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -28.12 -2.08 L -1.86 -2.08"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -25.56 5.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.53em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.96" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="21.14"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.Ex10.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle\var" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>var</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle\var</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p9.2" class="ltx_p">The corresponding complex of filtered <math id="SS30.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules is</p>
<table id="SS30.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(30.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="SS30.E4.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="40.59" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-14.76px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 314.97 40.59" width="314.97"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,40.59) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(157.49,0) translate(0,20.29)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -152.87 -5.53)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 31.37)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 21.22)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(68.61,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -64 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.25em;--ltx-fo-height:1.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.4em;font-size:10pt;" height="22.14" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 16.6)" width="114.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.E4.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bigl[\bigl(\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\bigr)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bigl[\bigl(\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\gr_{0}^{V}\mathcal{M}\bigr)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 137.22 0) translate(97.67,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -66.25 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.58em;--ltx-fo-height:1.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.4em;font-size:10pt;" height="22.14" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 16.6)" width="118.66"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.E4.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigl(\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\Bigr].}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigl(\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\gr_{-1}^{V}\mathcal{M}\bigr)\Bigr].}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="6.77002pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -15.38 -2.08 L 10.88 -2.08"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -4.34 5.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.3em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.96" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="4.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="SS30.E4.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle t</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p9.3" class="ltx_p">As before, one proves that the cohomology of this complex leads to two
well-defined mixed Hodge modules on <math id="SS30.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="SS30.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exercise">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="SS30.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exercise 30.5</span></span><span id="SS30.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="SS30.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="SS30.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i^{!}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{!}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the complex of <math id="SS30.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules in (<a href="#SS30.E4" title="In 30. Inverse image functors ‣ F. Various functors and strictness ‣ An overview of Morihiko Saito’s theoryof mixed Hodge modules" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30.4</span></a>). Use the
fact that <math id="SS30.E5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},F_{\bullet}\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent and regular along <math id="SS30.E5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> to
construct a morphism</p>
<table id="SS30.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="SS30.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{p}(i^{!}\mathcal{M})\to\mathbf{L}i^{\ast}(F_{p}\mathcal{M})[-1]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐋</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}(i^{!}\mathcal{M})\to\mathbf{L}i^{\ast}(F_{p}\mathcal{M})[-1]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.E5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">in the derived category <math id="SS30.E5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}(\mathscr{O}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝑐𝑜ℎ</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}_{\mathit{coh}}^{\mathit{b}}(\mathscr{O}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>. Show that this morphism is an isomorphism when
<math id="SS30.E5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> comes from a vector bundle with integrable connection.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="SS30.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="SS30.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For an arbitrary morphism <math id="SS30.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math>, we use
the factorization <math id="SS30.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=p_{2}\circ i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=p_{2}\circ i</annotation></semantics></math> given by the graph of <math id="SS30.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, and define</p>
<table id="S6.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="SS30.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS30.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}^{j}f^{\ast}(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}^{j}f^{\ast}(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS30.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{H}^{j+\dim Y}i^{\ast}\Bigl(\mathcal{H}^{-\dim Y}p^{\ast}_{2}(M,W_{\bullet}M)\Bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{H}^{j+\dim Y}i^{\ast}\Bigl(\mathcal{H}^{-\dim Y}p^{\ast}_{2}(M,W_{\bullet}M)\Bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="SS30.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS30.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}^{j}f^{!}(M,W_{\bullet}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}^{j}f^{!}(M,W_{\bullet}M)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="SS30.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{H}^{j-\dim Y}i^{!}\Bigl(\mathcal{H}^{\dim Y}p^{!}_{2}(M,W_{\bullet}M)\Bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{H}^{j-\dim Y}i^{!}\Bigl(\mathcal{H}^{\dim Y}p^{!}_{2}(M,W_{\bullet}M)\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="SS30.p10.2" class="ltx_p">One can check that the resulting functors take <math id="SS30.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="SS30.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MHM</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MHM}^{p}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and that
they are (up to canonical isomorphism) compatible with composition.</p>
</div>
</section>
